window.PATINA_SEARCH = [{"p":"index.html","a":"","page":"Welcome & quick start","t":"Welcome to Patina","x":"Welcome to Patina Patina is a texture painter for Linux and Windows. You paint materials such as rust, worn paint, wood or fabric straight onto a 3D model, and they come out as PBR texture maps for Blender, game engines and glTF. Suzanne with a fill layer of chipped paint, rust on the edges from a smart mask, cavity dirt and a hand-painted gold trim. New to Patina? Patina in 5 Minutes is a guided tutorial with real screenshots: connect Blender and Claude, import, add objects, unwrap, bake, paint layers, make your own materials and smart materials, and export. Each step shows the long way and the pie. It is also in Help \u203a Patina in 5 Minutes (Tutorial) and on the welcome window."},{"p":"index.html","a":"what-makes-it-different","page":"Welcome & quick start","t":"What makes it different","x":"Paint ignores the UV layout. Each brush dab is worked out in 3D, on the surface under your cursor. Strokes therefore run straight across UV seams, across islands at different scales and across rotated islands, with no breaks or stretching. Materials, not just colors. One stroke paints base color, roughness, metallic, height and emission together. The Patina default library has 270 ready-made materials, and you can add your own PBR scans or design procedural ones. UV editing in the same app. Paint the part you want as an island and Patina unwraps, orients and sizes it for you, or unwrap, pack, move islands and mark seams by hand. Paint you have already done moves along with the UVs. A live link to Blender. Send a mesh from Blender with one click, and the textures come back into a Principled BSDF material automatically. You can keep modeling after you've painted. Claude can drive it. Through MCP, Claude can paint, set up layers, unwrap and export. It can also take screenshots of the window so it sees what you see. New in 0.24 More procedural materials: 16 new generators (stripes, checks, dots, animal prints, cracked earth and lava, flagstones, pebbles, rock layers, crackle, chainmail"},{"p":"index.html","a":"quick-start","page":"Welcome & quick start","t":"Quick start","x":"Open a model. Use File \u203a Open\u2026 ( Ctrl + O ) or drag a .glb , .gltf , .fbx , .obj , .stl , .3mf or .ply onto the window ( which formats ). The samples folder includes Suzanne.glb . To start on a primitive shape, use File \u203a New . Pick a material. Click a sphere in the Materials panel at the bottom. Hover over it for a description. Paint. Hold the left mouse button over the model. To orbit, hold Alt and drag, or drag with the middle mouse button. The mouse wheel zooms. [ and ] change the brush size. Build it up in layers. Fill layer in the Layers panel covers the whole model with the current material. Choose Edges as the smart mask and the material appears only on the edges. Or click Smart materials and pick a ready-made smart material . New to masks? The beginner's guide to masks and smart materials explains them from scratch. Export. Use File \u203a Export Textures\u2026 ( Ctrl + E ), choose a preset for Blender, glTF, Unreal or Unity, and click Export. If you came from Blender, press Ctrl + B instead. Tip Press F1 at any time for a shortcut sheet. Press / in this manual to search it."},{"p":"index.html","a":"where-to-go-next","page":"Welcome & quick start","t":"Where to go next","x":"Install Fedora, Windows, the Blender add-on and Claude. The interface Every panel, and how to navigate the view. Painting Brushes, tools, projection modes, pen pressure, symmetry. Materials The default library, importing scans, the material editor. Guide: masks & smart materials What masks are for, step by step, for newcomers to texture painting. Layers & masks Paint, fill and adjustment layers, plus smart masks. Folders & smart materials Folders, solo, color tags, smart materials, history. UV editing The Island brush, texel density, unwrap, pack, seams, texture-space painting. Texture set & export Resolution, bakes (high-poly too), export presets, projects. Blender bridge The round trip, step by step. Claude & automation MCP, the console and the command server. HTTP API & batch mode Pipelines, render farms, job files. Patina Web The lightweight three.js painter that runs in your browser. Gotchas & troubleshooting Things that surprise people, and what to do about them."},{"p":"index.html","a":"formats","page":"Welcome & quick start","t":"Model formats","x":"Format What Patina reads glTF .glb / .gltf Every mesh in the scene with its transforms, UVs, normals, material slots and their textures . The best choice for games and the web. FBX .fbx Binary and ASCII FBX from 2006 on (FBX 6 and 7), from Maya, 3ds Max, Blender, ZBrush, Unreal, Unity and others: every mesh in the scene, UVs (the first UV set), normals and material slots with their textures . Converted to meters and Y up, so a Maya file in centimeters comes in at the right size. OBJ .obj Polygons, UVs, normals and material groups, with the textures its .mtl names. STL .stl Binary and ASCII, as exported for 3D printing. Read as millimeters with Z up, and converted. 3MF .3mf The 3D printing package: all objects and components on the build plate with their transforms, the units the file says, texture coordinates when it has them, and its base materials as material slots. PLY .ply ASCII and binary PLY, as scans and generated models come: faces, UVs (per vertex, or MeshLab's per face), normals and vertex colors . Read Z up, as Blender reads it. A PLY without faces (a point cloud) cannot be opened. Patina bridge .ptmesh What the Blender add-on sends."},{"p":"index.html","a":"model-textures","page":"Welcome & quick start","t":"Models that come with textures or vertex colors","x":"A textured model (a game asset, a scan, a model made by Meshy, Tripo or another generator, a Sketchfab download) opens looking as it did where it came from. Patina reads each material slot's textures and values from the file and paints them into a layer called From the model at the bottom of the stack: base color, roughness and metallic, the normal map, glow and opacity (cut-outs and see-through parts). Everything you add goes on top, so you can wear it, dirty it, recolor one part or paint over it, and turn the layer off to see the model without it. A model exported as a .glb opens again with its textures, as the layer From the model ; the status line says what came in. glTF : the metallic-roughness maps and values, normal map, glow (with its strength), alpha ( Mask as a cut-out, Blend as it is), texture transforms, clearcoat and sheen. Older specular-glossiness materials are read as roughness. Textures may be inside the .glb or beside the .gltf . OBJ : the .mtl file's map_Kd , map_Pr , map_Pm , map_Ke , map_d and normal or bump maps (a bump map that is really a normal map is recognized), and Kd , Pr , Pm , Ns and d where there is no map, as Blender reads them. FBX : the textures ("},{"p":"install.html","a":"","page":"Install","t":"Installing Patina","x":"Installing Patina Patina comes as a prebuilt download for Linux (x86-64) and Windows 10/11 (x64), or as source code you build yourself. It needs an OpenGL 4.5 graphics driver; any GPU from roughly the last ten years has one."},{"p":"install.html","a":"prebuilt","page":"Install","t":"Prebuilt","x":"tar xf patina-*-linux-x86_64.tar.gz # the version you downloaded, for example 1.2.0 cd patina-*-linux-x86_64 ./install.sh # installs to ~/.local; no root needed # PREFIX=/opt/patina ./install.sh # or somewhere else This installs ~/.local/bin/patina , a Patina entry in your application menu, the material library, this manual and Patina Web. ./install.sh --uninstall removes it all again. Your own materials, brushes and settings are left in place. The prebuilt download is made on a current Fedora. It needs that glibc or a newer one, and SDL 3: INSTALL.txt in the download names the exact glibc version. A Fedora Workstation of that release already has the other libraries. If install.sh reports that some are missing: sudo dnf install SDL3 mesa-dri-drivers libglvnd-egl libglvnd-glx libX11 libXi zenity On an older system, or a distribution with an older glibc, install.sh says so. Build from source instead, as described below. Wayland The prebuilt binary uses X11, so on a Wayland desktop it runs through XWayland, which GNOME and KDE enable by default. Pen pressure still works. For a native Wayland build, build from source as described below. With SDL3-devel installed, Wayland support is bui"},{"p":"install.html","a":"from-source","page":"Install","t":"From source","x":"tar xf patina-*-src.tar.gz && cd patina-*/ ./scripts/build-fedora.sh # installs build deps with dnf, builds, runs the tests, installs to ~/.local Manual build: sudo dnf install gcc-c++ cmake ninja-build SDL3-devel mesa-libGL-devel mesa-libEGL-devel zenity , then cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && cmake --build build . All other libraries are included in the source."},{"p":"install.html","a":"windows-10-11","page":"Install","t":"Windows 10 / 11","x":"Unzip patina-<version>-windows-x64.zip anywhere, for example your Downloads folder. Right-click install.ps1 and choose Run with PowerShell . It copies Patina to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Patina and adds a Start-menu shortcut. It can optionally add Patina to your PATH . No administrator rights are needed. Alternatively, run patina.exe straight from the unzipped folder. The folder is portable. There are two programs in the folder. patina.exe is the normal windowed app. patina-cli.exe is the same program as a console app. Use it for --mcp and --headless , because those modes talk over standard input and output. Gotcha: SmartScreen The download isn't code-signed, so Windows may show \u201cWindows protected your PC\u201d. Click More info \u203a Run anyway . PowerShell may also refuse to run install.ps1 . If it does, run powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1 . Gotcha: remote desktop and virtual machines Patina needs a real OpenGL 4.5 driver. Inside a VM or over Remote Desktop, Windows often provides only OpenGL 1.1, and Patina then reports that it can't create a context. Run Patina on the physical machine, or give the VM GPU pass-through."},{"p":"install.html","a":"the-blender-add-on","page":"Install","t":"The Blender add-on","x":"The add-on is the patina_bridge.zip next to the installer. Blender 4.2 and newer: Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Get Extensions , then the \u2304 menu at the top right, then Install from Disk\u2026 , and pick the zip. Blender 3.6 \u2013 4.1: Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Add-ons \u203a Install\u2026 , pick the zip, and enable Import-Export: Patina Bridge . Then open the Patina tab in the 3D view's sidebar ( N ). The Blender bridge explains the round trip."},{"p":"install.html","a":"connecting-claude","page":"Install","t":"Connecting Claude","x":"The Claude desktop app: click Connect to Claude app in Patina's Blender Link panel, or run patina --setup-claude (Windows: patina-cli.exe --setup-claude ). Then restart the Claude app. Claude Code: # Linux claude mcp add patina -- ~/.local/bin/patina --mcp # Windows (PowerShell) claude mcp add patina -- \"$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Patina\\patina-cli.exe\" --mcp Other MCP clients take the same command and arguments in their JSON configuration. Claude & automation covers the details."},{"p":"install.html","a":"where-patina-keeps-files","page":"Install","t":"Where Patina keeps files","x":"Linux Windows Your materials & brushes ~/.local/share/patina/user %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Patina\\user Settings & window layout ~/.config/patina %APPDATA%\\Patina Autosave, Blender exchange, bake cache ~/.cache/patina %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Patina\\cache Built-in library (read-only) ~/.local/share/patina/materials materials\\ next to patina.exe Help \u203a About Patina shows the exact paths on your machine."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"","page":"Guided layout","t":"The Guided layout: your first texture","x":"The Guided layout: your first texture The Guided layout splits the work into five steps along the top: Model , UVs , Bake , Paint and Export . Each step is a screen with what that step needs and nothing else. A new Patina starts in it. The Full layout shows every panel at once, docked the way you arrange it. Switch between them at any time with Guided and Full at the right of the menu bar. Both work on the same project with the same tools, so nothing is lost when you switch."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"steps","page":"Guided layout","t":"The step bar","x":"The steps are folder tabs, each in a color of its own: Model blue, UVs teal, Bake violet, Paint copper and Export green. Click a tab to go to that step. It comes to the front, a little higher than the others and joined to the strip under them, and the whole window takes on a tint of its color, so you can tell at a glance which step you are in. The steps are in the order the work usually goes, but you can jump between them in any order. The same colors mark the tools, panels and menus of each step in the Full layout . The mark beside each step's name shows where it stands: Mark Means Check mark Done: a model is open, the UVs are clean, the maps are baked, something is painted, the textures are exported. Amber warning sign Needs a look: overlapping UVs, faces with no UV area, a bake older than the UVs, a partial bake. Rest the pointer on the step to see why. Empty circle Not done yet. The button at the bottom of each step names the next one ( Next: bake the mesh maps ) and has that step's color. Search everything at the right of the bar finds any action, panel, material, brush, layer or file by name. It also finds the things the Full layout has and a step leaves out. Its keys are Ctr"},{"p":"guided.html","a":"model","page":"Guided layout","t":"1. Model","x":"With nothing open, the Model step offers a file to open, the sample (Suzanne), a shape to try things on, and your recent files. You can also drop a file anywhere on the window. To texture a Blender object, send it from Blender with the Patina Bridge add-on ( N panel \u203a Patina \u203a Send to Patina ; see Blender ). With a model open, the step shows what it is made of and what the mesh check found wrong with it (holes, flipped faces, open seams...). Show them on the model marks the problems, and Fix everything that can be fixed mends them; your paint moves with the faces. Textures sets the texture size and how the model's faces share textures: one texture, one per Blender material slot, or UDIM tiles. More mesh tools has the size and origin, polygon reduction, lightmap UVs and collision shapes. Retopology has tools and a panel of its own, so it opens in the Full layout. In the Model, Bake and Export steps the 3D view is for looking at the model. A left drag turns it, Shift moves it and Ctrl zooms, and no tool acts. Your usual navigation keys work too."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"uvs","page":"Guided layout","t":"2. UVs","x":"Patina paints across the surface, so most models paint well whatever their UVs. The textures you export do need clean UVs, though. The step checks them for you and marks only what needs fixing: Overlapping islands : paint shows twice there. Fix moves them apart. Faces with no UV area : they get no paint. Unwrap them gives them UVs of their own. No UVs at all : unwrap the model. Unwrap automatically cuts the model where it bends sharply, which suits hard-surface models. Unwrap along the seams flattens the pieces your seams cut out, which suits characters and cloth. Both work on the selected faces, or on the whole model when nothing is selected. Pack the islands arranges them to fill the texture. With Keep the paint when the UVs change on, the paint moves to the new UVs. The tool strip has the UV tools: Select (with Visible only ), Seams and the Island brush . The UV editor beside the view works as described in UV editing . Its right-click menu and the UV menu have every UV tool. Checker pattern on the model shows stretch: squares that stay square mean good UVs."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"bake","page":"Guided layout","t":"3. Bake","x":"Baking measures the model's shape once. Ambient occlusion is what is tucked in, curvature is the edges and creases, and thickness is the thin parts. Smart materials and smart masks use these maps to put dirt in the creases and wear on the edges. Tick the maps you want and press Bake . Quality sets how smooth the ambient occlusion is; 64 is good. Show puts a map on the model. When the UVs change after a bake, the step says the maps are out of date and Bake again brings them up to date. Each baked map is a layer at the bottom of the layer stack, where you can paint fixes onto it . To bake sculpted detail into the normal map, use Bake from a high-poly model . If you painted a high-poly model, Bake down to a low-poly model moves every layer onto its low-poly version (see Baking down )."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"paint","page":"Guided layout","t":"4. Paint","x":"This is where the texturing happens. The layers are at the right, and the library shelf is under the view: materials, smart materials and smart masks, with the brushes beside them. The tool strip has the paint tools and symmetry. While nothing is painted, a note at the top says where to start: drag a smart material from the shelf onto the model, or paint with the brush (B). On a new Patina the brush paints a red color; pick another in Colors , or a material from the shelf. Under the layers are four tabs. Brush has the brush and the paint material, Colors the color picker and swatches, and History every undo step. Scene has the base material under every layer and the lighting. All of it works as the rest of this manual describes: Painting , Materials , Layers . Why isn't it painting? When a stroke would paint nothing you can see, a line in the corner of the 3D view says why. The selected layer may be hidden, or inside a hidden folder, or its opacity may be 0. Or every paint channel may be switched off, or no layer is selected."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"export","page":"Guided layout","t":"5. Export","x":"For a model from Blender, Send the textures to Blender sends the textures back to the object, with its UV edits. Send them after every change keeps Blender up to date as you paint. To write image files instead, choose what they are for , their size and the folder, then press Export the textures . The preset sets which maps are written and how they are packed: Blender, glTF, Unreal (ORM and DirectX normals) or Unity (MaskMap). Above the button, the step lists every file it will write: its name, what its channels hold ( R occlusion, G roughness, B metallic ), its bits, and whether it is a color map ( sRGB ) or a data map ( Non-Color , as Blender's image nodes call it). Files with the same name are replaced. Every export option opens the full Export Textures window. It has the file names, layered PSD files, and one layer or its mask. Save the project too: the exported images are flat, and only the project keeps every layer."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"choices","page":"Guided layout","t":"First start","x":"The first time Patina starts, the welcome window asks three things. You come from picks the keys and the way the view turns: Blender (the middle button orbits, F3 searches), Maya (Alt with the left, middle and right buttons), or something else (Patina's own keys, Alt or the middle button). Your textures go to picks the export preset. Layout picks Guided or Full. All three can be changed later: the keys in Edit \u203a Preferences , the layout at the right of the menu bar. In the Guided layout the welcome window appears only that first time, because the Model step does its job from then on. Help \u203a Welcome Window brings it back."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"full","page":"Guided layout","t":"The Full layout","x":"The Full layout shows every panel at once and keeps the arrangement you give it. It is the layout to work in once you know your way around, or when you want two things side by side that the steps keep apart. An existing Patina keeps the layout it had: a Patina used before 0.46 opens in the Full layout. See The interface ."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"unsaved","page":"Guided layout","t":"Unsaved work","x":"Patina asks before anything replaces a project with unsaved changes: File \u203a New , Open , Open Recent , the autosave, the sample, the welcome window's buttons and a dropped file. Save saves first. For a project that was never saved, it asks where to save and then goes on. Don't save drops the changes, and Cancel keeps working."},{"p":"guided.html","a":"agents","page":"Guided layout","t":"For agents","x":"The layout command reports the layout and each step's state ( done , look , todo , and why), and switches the layout ( mode ) or the step ( step ). It works only with the window open. The Guided layout feature switch ( Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Features ) turns the whole thing off."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"","page":"The interface","t":"The interface","x":"The interface Patina has two layouts. The Guided layout splits the work into five steps, each with what it needs in front, and a new Patina starts in it. This page describes the Full layout , which shows every panel at once. Switch between them with Guided and Full at the right of the menu bar. In the Full layout every panel is a dockable window. Drag a panel's tab to rearrange the layout, and Patina remembers it, along with which tool windows were open (Retopology, Assistant, Texel density, Mesh symmetry, Auto texture, Macro recorder, Material Editor). The Retopology window starts as a tab beside Properties; nothing of retopology runs or shows until you choose a retopology tool or its tab. To get the default layout back, use View \u203a Reset the Layout . Area What it is for Tools (left strip) Every tool, grouped by job: Paint (paint, erase, blur, smudge, clone, fill, pick), UV (select faces, seams, the island brush), Retopo (zone lines, zones, flow, density), Symmetry (mirror X, Y, Z and radial) and Notes . See The toolbars . Viewport The model, lit by a studio environment. This is where you paint. The bar along the top holds the brush essentials, the projection mode, the locks, the v"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"step-colors","page":"The interface","t":"The steps' colors","x":"Each step of the Guided layout has a color: Model blue, UVs teal, Bake violet, Paint copper and Export green. The Full layout uses the same colors to show which step a tool, panel or menu belongs to: The tool strip: a stripe beside each group and its name in the group's color (Paint copper, UV teal, Retopo blue). The tool in use is filled with that color. The panels' tabs: a hint of the color on the tab, and a line over the one in front. UV Editor is teal; Layers, Properties, Materials, Brushes and Colors copper; Texture Set and Retopology blue; Blender Link green. Viewport, History, Notes and Console belong to every step and keep no color. The menu bar: the menus of the work follow the steps' order, Mesh , Retopology , UV , Bake , Blender , each underlined in its step's color. In the Blender layout, choosing a tool or a workspace shows its tab in the Properties editor: the Select tool the Step tab, the Paint tool the Layer tab, a retopology tool the Retopology tab. The pin above the Properties tabs keeps the tab you chose instead (1.2.10). Click it again to unpin."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"welcome","page":"The interface","t":"The welcome window","x":"When Patina starts without a file, the welcome window offers the recent files, the sample model (Suzanne), and a new sphere or cube. At the top, Patina in 5 Minutes opens the guided tutorial in your browser (1.2.12). The x beside it hides that button for good. The tutorial stays in Help \u203a Patina in 5 Minutes (Tutorial) . The welcome window also asks three things: You come from : Blender (the Blender keymap , where the middle button orbits and F3 searches), Maya (Alt with the left, middle and right buttons) or something else (Patina's keys). Your textures go to : the export preset. Layout : Guided or Full. Untick Show this window on start to skip it, and Help \u203a Welcome Window brings it back. In the Guided layout it appears only the first time, because the Model step offers the same things after that. Unsaved work Patina asks before anything replaces a project with unsaved changes: New, Open, Open Recent, the autosave, the sample, the welcome window's buttons, a dropped file."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"toolbars","page":"The interface","t":"The toolbars","x":"The tool strip on the left and the bars over the 3D view, the UV editor and the layers show icons. They are drawn after the ones painting and 3D programs use, so a brush is a brush and a bucket fills. Rest the pointer on an icon to see its name and shortcut. Right-click a tool to see or change its shortcut. The tool strip, grouped by job The File menu The retopology tools are always in the strip (with the Retopology feature on). Choosing one shows the zone lines and the quads; its settings are in the bar over the view and in the Retopology window, which opens as a tab if it was closed. You don't need to open the Retopology tab first. Icon Tool What it does Paint B Paints the brush's material or color. Erase E Erases paint (or a mask). Blur U Softens paint, across UV seams too. Smudge J Drags the paint under the brush along the stroke. Clone C Copies paint from a source ( Ctrl +click sets it). Fill P Fills a face, a UV island, a connected part, the mesh, the selection or a material slot. Pick I Picks the color and values under the pointer. Select S Selects faces or UV islands, for UV work. Seams M Lays UV seams along the model's edges as you drag. Island L Paint over a part, then ma"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"right-click","page":"The interface","t":"Right-click menus","x":"A right-click opens a menu of what you can do there. In the 3D view it is a click without dragging, so the right-button navigation (zoom with Alt , orbit in the ZBrush preset) still works. Alt +right-click still picks a color, and Ctrl +right-drag still sizes the brush. Right-click any slider to type its value : the slider turns into a box with the number selected; type, then Enter keeps it and Esc goes back. It is the quick way to set exact values (a brush size of 100, a strength of 0.5). A typed value stays in the slider's range. A left click on a slider works as before, and Ctrl +click types too. Preferences \u203a Features \u203a Type values into sliders switches it off. The 3D view's menu, for the spot under the pointer. Where The menu has 3D view , on the model What is there (face, UV island, material slot). Pick the Color Here . Layers Painted Here : every layer that shows on that spot, with how much, to select one. Fill with the Paint Material (this face, its UV island, the connected part, its material slot) and erase an island. Add a Note Here , Set the Clone Source Here and Center the View Here . 3D view , anywhere Select : on the model, what is under the pointer (this face, its UV"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"language","page":"The interface","t":"Language","x":"Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Language shows Patina in another language, from a translation file. Anyone can make one; see Translating Patina ."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"navigate","page":"The interface","t":"Navigating the view","x":"The axis gizmo, pointing at the X end. The axis gizmo in the top right corner of the 3D view shows which way the X, Y and Z axes point as the camera turns: X red, Y green and Z blue, as in Blender and most 3D programs (the letters on the mirror buttons have the same colors). Patina's Y axis points up. The dots without letters are the negative ends. Click an end to look along that axis: X for the right view, Y for the top view, Z for the front view, and the plain dots for the opposite sides. The view jumps there (see Side views ). Click the end facing you to see the model from the other side. Drag inside the gizmo to orbit. The wheel zooms over it, as over the rest of the view. View \u203a Axis Gizmo hides or shows it. Patina Web has the same gizmo."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"side-views","page":"The interface","t":"Side views, and back to your view","x":"On a side view, the Back button under the gizmo. The numpad ( 1 front, 3 right, 7 top, with Ctrl the opposite side), a click on the gizmo and View \u203a View front and the others jump to a side, top or bottom view. Side views are orthographic (no perspective), as in Blender, until you orbit away; then the perspective comes back. Preferences \u203a Mouse can keep the perspective instead, or turn to the side views in a quarter second instead of jumping. A side view remembers the view you had before it. Numpad 0 , or the Back button under the gizmo, takes you back there. Press it again to go back to the side view, so you can flip between the two. Snapping while orbiting: hold Alt while you orbit with the middle button and the view snaps to the nearest side, top or bottom view as you move the mouse, the way Blender does. Let go of Alt to orbit freely again from there. With the Blender preset, Alt + middle drag snaps from the start; with Patina's preset, Alt + middle drag pans (as in Substance and Maya), so press Alt once the orbit has begun. When you orbit with Alt + left (or the right button in the ZBrush preset), Shift does the snapping. Numpad 0 goes back to where the orbit began."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"view-pie","page":"The interface","t":"Pie menus (radial menus)","x":"Hold Z , point at an item, let go. Here: Solid. The Solid view: the shape alone, without textures. Pie menus, also called radial menus, open in a ring round the pointer, as in Blender. There are four of them over the 3D view, with eight items each. Hold the key, move toward an item and let go. Or tap the key and click an item. Esc closes a pie, and so does a right-click away from the items. Key Pie What is in it Z View How the model shows: lit, solid, unlit, path traced, wireframe, seams, the UV checker; and Frame selection. Shift + Z Channels One channel at a time: base color, normal, roughness, metallic, height, emissive, AO, the layer mask. ` (under Esc ) Edit mode The tools: paint, erase, fill, select, seams, the Island brush, the retopology lines and edge loops. Q Quick Favorites Eight items you choose (see Quick Favorites ). The view pie is laid out like Blender's shading pie: Wireframe left, Solid right, Lit down, Path traced up. Where Item What it does Left Wireframe The mesh's edges on or off. Right Solid The shape alone: light gray, lit, no textures or normal map. Again: back to the view mode before. Down Lit The material, lit by the environment: the usual view. Up Path t"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"quick-favorites","page":"The interface","t":"Quick Favorites","x":"Q : your eight. An empty place says Add a favorite. Choose an empty place: search, then click or press Enter . Q opens a pie of your own, as Blender's Quick Favorites menu. It starts with Clear selection, Make island, X mirror, Swap colors, Back to your view and Render image, and two empty places. To fill a place: Right-click a button on the toolbars, or an item in another pie, and choose Add to Quick Favorites . It goes into the first empty place. Choose an empty place in the pie. A list opens with everything a pie can hold: the tools, the view modes, the toggles and every shortcut action. Type to search. In Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Pie menus , click a place to choose its item, or x to empty it. Right-click an item in the Quick Favorites pie to replace it or take it out. Right-click Solid in the view pie. Preferences \u203a Pie menus . The Solid view is also in the view mode list, with the other view modes. Patina's default navigation is below. If your hands already know another program, pick it in View \u203a Navigation : Preset Orbit Pan Zoom Patina (default) Alt + left, or middle Shift + middle wheel, Alt + right Blender middle Shift + middle wheel, Ctrl + middle Maya / Substance Alt + left "},{"p":"interface.html","a":"workspace","page":"The interface","t":"Making it yours","x":"View : distraction-free mode, UI scale and the navigation presets. Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts . Shortcuts used twice are shown in red. Preferences: Edit \u203a Preferences\u2026 collects the keymap, the mouse navigation, Ctrl-paints-secondary and the UI scale in one window. Blender keymap: choose Blender keymap in Preferences to use Blender's keys (S samples, F resizes the brush, Shift+A adds, Shift+D duplicates, middle mouse orbits\u2026). See the Blender keymap reference . Keyboard shortcuts: Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts lists every rebindable action. Click a shortcut and press the new combination. Backspace clears it and Esc cancels. They are saved in keymap.json in the settings folder ( Help \u203a About shows where), so you can copy them to another machine. Hide the panels: Tab shows only the viewport and its brush bar. Press Tab again to bring everything back exactly as it was. UI scale: View \u203a UI Scale makes text and controls 75\u2013200% of their normal size. It is applied immediately and remembered. Versions and re-exports: File \u203a Save Incremental ( Ctrl + Alt + S ) and File \u203a Export Again ( Ctrl + Shift + E ); see Projects . Recent files: File \u203a Open Recent lists the last 12 projects and meshes, incl"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"view-modes","page":"The interface","t":"View modes","x":"The view combo in the viewport bar shows a single channel, a baked map or a debugging aid. UV checker shows a checker pattern through the model's UVs, which reveals stretching and texel density at a glance. Paint is projected in 3D, so a stretched checker doesn't affect how a stroke looks. It does mean those areas get fewer texture pixels. Base color, roughness, normal, curvature, ambient occlusion and the UV checker. Three view modes help with the layout and the parts of the model: Texel Density and UV Stretch are heat maps (see Texel density ), and Material Slots colors each Blender material slot differently. Symmetry shows how far each part of the model is from its mirror image across X (see Mesh symmetry check ). PBR Range marks base colors that no real material has, the way Substance's PBR validation does. Red: a non-metal darker than sRGB 30 (darker than charcoal). Blue: a non-metal brighter than 240 (whiter than fresh snow). Orange: a metal darker than 180 (metals reflect at least about half the light). Purple: metallic half-way between metal and non-metal, which is fine along a border, but odd over an area (a few of Patina's own materials, such as the metallic car paints, a"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"compare","page":"The interface","t":"Compare view","x":"To see what a change does, split the view: View \u203a Compare View , or \\ . The left of the divider shows a snapshot of the textures, the right the live result. Drag the divider to wipe between them. Shift + \\ takes a new snapshot, so the usual way is: take a snapshot, change a mask or a material, and wipe. The camera, lighting and brush are shared by both sides, and you can keep painting on the right. The snapshot on the left still has the edge rust that is now hidden. The numbered pins are notes. The left side can also show a view mode instead ( Compare View \u203a Left: A View Mode ), for example the roughness or the normal map next to the lit material. After a bake-down , Left: the high-poly before the bake-down shows the painted high-poly as it was, beside the low-poly with the layers baked down. The snapshot lives only for the session, and a new texture resolution clears it. Claude uses set_compare , and render_view renders the split too."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"notes","page":"The interface","t":"Notes","x":"Notes are comments pinned to the model, saved in the project: review remarks, to-dos, hand-over notes. Pick the Note tool ( N ) and click on the model, type, and press Save ( Ctrl + Enter ). Click a pin with the Note tool to edit it, mark it resolved or delete it. Hover over a pin with any tool to read it. Pins behind the model are drawn faint. The camera moves as with any tool while the Note tool is chosen. The Notes panel (next to History) lists every note with its number. Click one to turn the camera to the view it was written from, double-click to edit it, and tick it when it's resolved. Resolved shows or hides resolved notes, Pins hides the pins in the view, and Copy all copies the list as a Markdown checklist, for an issue tracker or an email. Notes keep the author (your user name) and the date. Claude reads and writes them with list_notes , add_note , update_note , delete_note and go_to_note , so it can work through review notes left in a project."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"path-traced","page":"The interface","t":"Path traced","x":"The Material view: quick, lit by the environment, no shadows. Path traced , 256 samples: the ear reflects in the head, the shadow lies on the ground. The Path traced view mode follows the light the way a renderer such as Blender's Cycles does: real shadows, reflections of the model in itself, and light bouncing between its parts. It uses everything you paint (base color, roughness, metallic, the normal map, emissive, opacity, sheen, clearcoat, subsurface) and the same environment light as the other views, so Shift + right drag turns the light here too. An invisible ground under the model shows its shadow, as in a product photo. The picture starts grainy and gets cleaner the longer nothing changes; the count in the view's corner says how far it has got. Anything you change (the camera, a stroke, the light) starts it again, with a quick picture first so the view stays smooth. It stops at its number of samples, so the graphics card can rest. Path tracing on the bar. In this mode the bar over the view has a Path tracing button with the settings: Samples : where the view stops (512). More removes the last grain in soft shadows and dull metal. Light bounces : how many times light may bou"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"render","page":"The interface","t":"Rendering an image","x":"File \u203a Render Image\u2026 ( F12 ) saves what the viewport shows as a PNG, without the brush circle, seams or wireframe: for a portfolio, a client, or a quick work-in-progress post. Size: any width and height, with HD, 4K and square presets. It doesn't have to match the window. Transparent background: only the model, for compositing or a web page. Anti-aliasing: renders 2\u20134 times larger and scales down, for smooth edges. Turntable frames: 2 or more renders the model turning once around, as name_0001.png , name_0002.png \u2026 ready for a GIF or video tool. Path traced: the render made by the path tracer (see Path traced ), with its number of Samples (256 is clean for most models). It takes seconds to minutes; a bar shows how far it has got, and Cancel stops it. The window keeps working meanwhile. No anti-aliasing is needed: the samples smooth the edges. With a transparent background, the ground's shadow is kept as see-through black. Use Shift + right drag first to turn the lighting to taste. A render at 640 \u00d7 400, 2\u00d7 anti-aliasing."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"the-console","page":"The interface","t":"The console","x":"The Console tab takes the same commands as Claude and the Blender add-on. Type a command name, then optional JSON arguments, for example set_brush {\"size\": 48} . list_commands lists them all, and the command reference describes each one. It is handy for precise values and for repeating a set-up."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"help-windows","page":"The interface","t":"Help windows","x":"F1 opens a shortcut sheet. Help \u203a Patina in 5 Minutes (Tutorial) opens the guided tutorial and Help \u203a Manual opens this manual, both in your browser. Help \u203a About shows the version, the graphics driver and the folders on your machine."},{"p":"tools.html","a":"","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Search, checks and macros","x":"Search, checks and macros Patina 0.10 adds a command palette that finds anything by name, a mesh symmetry check, tools for models with several texture sets, a one-click first pass of materials from the model's slot names, and a macro recorder. Each one can be switched off in Preferences \u203a Features if it gets in your way."},{"p":"tools.html","a":"features","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Switching features on and off","x":"New features arrive switched on. Edit \u203a Preferences lists them under Features , with what each one does. Untick one and its menu entries, buttons, shortcuts and commands go away (scripts and Claude get an error saying it is switched off). The choice is saved with your session; tick it again to bring it back. Nothing else changes: a project painted with a feature on opens fine with it off. Every switch says what it controls and which version added it. Feature Where it shows up Command palette Ctrl + Shift + P or F3 ( F3 or Ctrl + K in the Blender keymap), Edit \u203a Search Everything , Search everything on the Guided layout's step bar Guided layout The step bar (Model, UVs, Bake, Paint, Export), Guided and Full at the right of the menu bar, the welcome window's Layout , the layout command (see The Guided layout ) Bake-down Bake \u203a Bake Down to a Low-Poly Model , the Bake-Down Check view mode, the compare view's high-poly, the layers kept by Use the quads as the model , the bake_down command (see Baking down ) Mesh symmetry check UV \u203a Mirrored Halves \u203a Check Mesh Symmetry , the Symmetry view mode, the mesh_symmetry command, Select Asymmetric Vertices in Blender Texture set lock and isolat"},{"p":"tools.html","a":"palette","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"The command palette","x":"Press Ctrl + Shift + P (or F3 ; Ctrl + K too in the Blender keymap) and type a few letters of what you want. The palette searches every keyboard action, window, view mode, command, library material, brush, layer and recent file. \u2191 \u2193 choose, Enter runs it and Esc closes the palette. \"rust\" finds the rust materials and brushes. Picking a material makes it the paint material. Typing: a whole word ranks highest, then the start of a word, then letters that begin words, so msc finds Mesh symmetry check and prefs finds Preferences . Letters scattered at random through a name don't count, so the list stays short. What you used recently comes first when the box is empty, and gets a small boost when you type. Actions run as if you had pressed their shortcut, so they also work for actions you never gave a key. Commands that need arguments are left out; use the console or a script for those. Layers select the layer; recent files open; features can be switched from here too."},{"p":"tools.html","a":"symmetry","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Mesh symmetry check","x":"Characters and props are usually meant to be mirror-symmetric, and small differences between the halves cause trouble later: mirrored painting misses, symmetric UVs don't stack, and shape keys or rigging built for one side don't fit the other. UV \u203a Mirrored Halves \u203a Check Mesh Symmetry measures it. Every vertex is mirrored across X (Blender's X) and Patina measures how far it lands from the surface on the other side. Because it compares shapes, not vertex lists, it works even where the two halves have different topology. The verdict, the numbers, and the worst spots. Look frames one in the viewport. The Symmetry view mode: gray-blue is symmetric, yellow is off by about the tolerance, red by ten times as much or more. Tolerance is 0.1% of the model's largest size: on a 1.8 m character, 1.8 mm. Vertices further off than that count as asymmetric. Both halves light up: a bump on the right also shows on the left, where the mirror of the left side finds no bump. Magenta means there is no surface at all on the other side within 5% of the size: one-sided parts such as a holster, or a model that isn't meant to be symmetric. The mirror plane is x = 0 when the model sits on it, else the middl"},{"p":"tools.html","a":"texture-set-tools","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Working with texture sets","x":"With texture sets per material slot or UDIM tiles , the Texture Set panel gets three more controls: Current set picks the texture set the UV editor shows (the same as the list in the UV editor's toolbar). Isolate draws the other texture sets as dark ghosts in the viewport, and strokes only paint the current set, wherever they start. Handy for the inside of a jacket or a belt over a shirt. Set lock keeps each stroke on the texture set it starts on, so a stroke that starts on the skin stops at the collar. With a single texture it uses the material slot instead. It combines with the topology and island locks. Select faces selects every face of the current set; fill with Selection to fill just that set. Left: the whole model. Right: Isolate on the 'Cloth' set. Layered PSD export now works with texture sets and UDIM tiles too: each set or tile gets its own PSD files, named like the PNG maps ( Hero_Body_basecolor.psd , Hero_basecolor.1002.psd )."},{"p":"tools.html","a":"auto-texture","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Auto texture from material slots","x":"A model from Blender usually arrives with sensible slot names: Skin , Hair , Jeans , Boots_Leather , M_Steel . Edit \u203a Auto Texture from Material Slots picks a library material for each slot from its name and adds a fill layer per slot, masked by the Material slot smart mask, so you start from a textured model instead of a gray one. Each slot with its guess. Change any of them, or choose (skip) , before adding the layers. Names are split into words ( M_BootLeather.001 \u2192 boot, leather) and matched against material names, tags and categories, with a list of everyday words: body and face mean skin, shirt linen, jacket a puffer, buckle brass, sole rubber, hull sci-fi panels and so on. Prefer : realistic or stylized materials. A slot named Toon_Hair asks for the stylized ones by itself. Slots that want painting by hand are skipped: eyes, teeth, tongue, glass, decals, logos. So are names with nothing to go on ( Material.001 ). The layers go into an Auto texture folder at the bottom of the stack, so anything you paint stays on top. Delete the folder to start again. Projection : triplanar (no seams, ignores the UVs) or UV."},{"p":"tools.html","a":"macros","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Macros","x":"The macro recorder keeps what you do as commands, to replay on another model. Edit \u203a Record Macro starts it (the status bar shows REC and the number of steps); do your thing; Edit \u203a Stop Recording Macro . Play Macro runs the steps on whatever is open now. Three fill layers recorded: a base, edge wear and dust. Remove a step with x . What is recorded: menu actions and windows that run commands (adding layers and fills, smart materials, smart masks, UV tools, bakes, exports, auto texture), and every command a script or Claude runs while recording. Questions that change nothing (state, lists, renders) are left out. What isn't: brush strokes you paint in the viewport (they are tied to one model and one view), and opening, saving and the Blender link, so a macro works on whatever is open when you play it. Undo while recording: a step you undo drops out of the macro, and comes back if you redo it. Save writes the macro as a job file in your configuration folder ( macros/ ). It is the same format patina run uses, so the macro also runs without a window: patina run ~/.config/patina/macros/rust_pass.json . Saved macros are listed in the window with a Play button. Playing stops at the first "},{"p":"tools.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Search, checks & macros","t":"Gotchas","x":"The symmetry check compares across X only. A model that is symmetric across another axis reads as asymmetric. Auto texture guesses from names; it doesn't look at the shape. A slot called Body on a car gets skin. A macro replays commands with the values they had: layer indices, materials and paths. Steps that pick a layer by number may pick a different layer on a model with another stack."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"","page":"Assistant","t":"Patina Assistant","x":"Patina Assistant Patina 0.11 adds an assistant you talk to in plain words: \"make the creases of the skin slightly redder\" , \"one UV island for the head and another for the body\" . A small model running on your own computer turns the request into Patina commands, shows you what it will do, and does it as one step you can undo. Nothing is sent anywhere, and there is no account or key."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-model","page":"Assistant","t":"Getting the model","x":"The model is a separate download, so Patina itself stays small. It is one file, patina-assistant-1.7b-q4_k_m.gguf (1.1 GB). Download it from the link given with this Patina release , then in Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Features , under Patina Assistant , click Choose model file... and pick it. Patina copies it into its own folder ( ~/.local/share/patina/user/models on Linux, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Patina\\user\\models on Windows) and checks it. The line under the button then says where the model runs: on the graphics card, or on the processor. The file's SHA-256 is c0105bb18bceb516259312df7fc2a3e6ca45a8ccbadad5eefd1f6b214c89e9e0 . If Patina doesn't recognize a file, it still uses it and says so."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-use","page":"Assistant","t":"Asking","x":"Open Window \u203a Assistant , or type into the command palette ( Ctrl+Shift+P ): when nothing matches, the last row offers Ask the Assistant . Type what you want and press Enter . The answer takes about a second on a recent graphics card and a few seconds on the processor alone. The Assistant says in one sentence what it will do, and lists the steps in plain words. Steps that delete a layer, clear paint or rebuild the UVs have a Careful line. Preview runs the plan, so you see the real result in the viewport. Keep keeps it; Cancel takes it back. While previewing, the Strength slider (and the Color of a tint) adjusts the result directly, without asking again. Everything a request does is one undo step , named after it in the history: Ctrl+Z takes back the whole request. Requests that only change the view, the camera or the brush, or open a window, run at once. A few requests change something Patina can't undo: a texture set of its own for a part, the texture resolution, and the texture set layout (each of them clears the undo history). Their plan has no Preview ; Do it runs it, and says first that it can't be undone. The plan for the tint request: one step, with the Color and Strength to"},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"follow-ups","page":"Assistant","t":"Follow-ups","x":"The Assistant remembers what the last request made, so you can carry on: \"a bit stronger\" , \"less red\" , \"only on the face\" , \"do that everywhere\" , \"put it on the edges instead\" , \"put it on the boots too\" , \"rename it to Base\" , \"hide it for now\" , \"actually, remove that\" ."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"when-it-asks-back","page":"Assistant","t":"When it asks back","x":"If a request is unclear, or names a part the model doesn't have, the Assistant asks a question instead of guessing. The parts it can see appear as buttons under the question: hover over one to see its faces, click it to answer. A vague request ( \"make it look better\" ) gets a few ideas for your model as buttons too."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-cant","page":"Assistant","t":"When it can't","x":"Some things are outside Patina: changing the shape of the model, adding objects, animating or posing it, painting artwork for you, making video, uploading. The Assistant says so in one sentence instead of doing something else that sounds similar, and offers up to three close things it can do, as buttons: \"give him a cape\" gets an offer to set you up to paint a cape by hand, and a few changes to his parts; click one to ask for it. Something Patina doesn't do: one sentence why, and requests it can do instead."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-parts","page":"Assistant","t":"Parts of the model","x":"Requests name parts the way you would: the jacket, the left arm, the head. The Assistant knows the parts Patina finds ( mesh_parts lists them): Material slots , by name: Skin , Jeans , Boots_Leather ... Vertex groups sent from Blender (vertices with a weight of 0.5 or more): Head , Arm.L ... Pieces : separate parts of the mesh, largest first, when no slot or group already names them. The head and the body , found from the shape: Patina looks for a neck, a narrow ring between a wider head and wider shoulders. It looks again under long hair. If there is no neck (a prop, a creature with no clear neck) there is no head part, and the Assistant asks which part is the head. The selection : select faces with the Island brush or the Select tool and say \"make these rusty\" . A part from a material slot or vertex group is the surest; name your slots and groups and the Assistant (and Auto texture) can find them. The head and the body Patina finds from the shape, shown in color."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-what","page":"Assistant","t":"What it can do","x":"The Assistant sets things up; you finish them. Most requests leave layers with a Strength slider each, so you tune the result by hand instead of hunting for settings. Whole looks in one go: \"make the armor look battle-worn\" , weathered, rusty, abandoned, muddy, burnt, snowy, overgrown, dusty, grimy. A look is a folder of weathering layers (dirt in the creases, dust on top, wear on the edges...), one slider each. \"Slightly\" or \"very\" sets how strong it starts. Several things at once : \"make the jacket black leather, the jeans denim and put mud on the boots\" . Getting ready to paint by hand : \"I want to paint scratches on the helmet\" adds a paint layer that only shows on the helmet, and picks the material, the brush and the Paint tool. Adjustments : \"make the jacket a bit darker\" , lighter, more or less saturated, more contrast, rougher, glossier. Each is an adjustment layer limited to the part, so it can be undone or tuned at any time. Tints and materials limited to a part and to a smart mask: creases, edges, sheltered places, where dust settles, toward the bottom, in patches. Materials can have a color: \"red leather\" . A region can leave a part out: \"dirt everywhere except the face"},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-uv","page":"Assistant","t":"One island per part","x":"\"One UV island for the head and another for the body\" runs uv_island_per_part . Each part is cut along its border and unwrapped on its own. A part that is closed or a tube (a whole head, a torso) gets a cut down its back, and a part with limbs gets cuts from the hands and feet along the back of the arms and legs. That is how you would cut a character by hand. It is then relaxed (LSCM, then as-rigid-as-possible) and everything is packed. A part made of separate pieces (eyes, teeth, lashes) gets an island per piece. The command reports the stretch of each part. A whole body as one island is hard to lay flat: the arms and legs can end up on top of the torso, and layered hair on top of itself. Then two spots share the same texture. When that happens, the Assistant says so under the plan. For a cleaner layout, make more islands: paint an arm, a leg or a strand of hair with the Island brush ( L ) and press Enter . Rebuilding the UVs gets a Careful line, because it replaces the old layout. The result: one island for the head and one for the body."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-feedback","page":"Assistant","t":"When it gets it wrong","x":"Cancel, and try other words. Not what I wanted saves the request, the plan and a note of what you meant to ~/.local/share/patina/user/assistant/feedback.jsonl . It stays on your computer; it's there if you want to help make the next model better. Copy as commands copies the plan's commands, to paste into the console or a script."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-scripting","page":"Assistant","t":"From scripts and Claude","x":"The same commands are available to scripts, the HTTP API and MCP: assistant_ask {request} : the plan for a request, checked but not run. assistant_apply {plan, request} : runs a plan as one undo step. A step that fails undoes the ones before it. assistant_check {plan} : the steps in plain words and the warnings. assistant_panel {request, preview} : opens the Assistant window in the desktop app and asks there; with preview the plan is previewed as soon as it arrives, waiting for Keep or Cancel . assistant_model {action: load | unload | status} , assistant_context {request} , assistant_grammar . The commands plans use are ordinary commands too: mesh_parts , select_part , add_tint_layer , add_material_layer , update_layer_mask , uv_island_per_part , and apply_smart_material with a region. A plan is JSON: {\"say\": \"...\", \"steps\": [{\"cmd\": \"...\", \"args\": {...}}]} . \"$0.index\" in a step stands for the index that step 0 returned. With the macro recorder running, a request is recorded as its steps, so it replays without the model."},{"p":"assistant.html","a":"assistant-off","page":"Assistant","t":"Switching it off","x":"Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Features \u203a Patina Assistant . Switched off, the window, the palette row and the Assistant's commands disappear. Without a model file it does nothing at all."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"","page":"Painting","t":"Painting","x":"Painting Choose a layer, a material and a brush, then paint on the model. This page explains what happens under the brush, so the results are predictable. A soft rust stroke in progress. The circle is the brush footprint on screen."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"how-painting-works","page":"Painting","t":"How painting works","x":"Most painters stamp the brush into the texture image, so the stroke follows the UV layout and breaks at every seam. Patina works the other way round. For every texel (texture pixel) of the model it knows the point on the surface the texel belongs to. Each brush dab asks every texel the same question: is your surface point under the brush, and is it visible? .b{fill:var(--panel);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2}.t{font:600 14px system-ui,sans-serif;fill:var(--fg)}.s{font:12.5px system-ui,sans-serif;fill:var(--dim)}.a{stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:2.5;fill:none;marker-end:url(#ar)} 1 \u00b7 Texel in UV space every texel of the texture set knows its 3D point and normal (baked once) 2 \u00b7 Project to screen through the camera, the same way the model is drawn 3 \u00b7 Visible? Under brush? depth test vs. the view, facing test, brush falloff \u2192 stroke mask 4 \u00b7 Mix the material material sampled at the 3D point (tri-planar), blended into the layer by the mask The result is that a stroke covers exactly what you see under the cursor, wherever the texels live in the texture. Seams don't break it, islands at different scales don't stretch it, and rotated or mirrored islands don't twist it. Materials ar"},{"p":"painting.html","a":"tools","page":"Painting","t":"Tools","x":"Tool Key What it does Paint B Paints the current material onto the selected layer, or onto its mask if you are editing the mask. Erase E Removes paint from the layer, so the layers underneath show through. Blur U Softens paint under the brush. Blur radius in Properties sets the strength. It carries on across UV seams (see Smudge and seams ). Smudge J Drags the paint under the brush along the stroke, like a finger through wet paint. Flow and pen pressure set how much it carries. Clone C Copies paint from a source point. Ctrl +click sets the source, on the model or in the UV editor's paint mode. With Aligned clone on, the source keeps its offset from one stroke to the next. Fill P Polygon fill. Fills a face, a UV island, the connected part of the mesh, the whole mesh, the face selection, or every face of the clicked material slot (the Blender material). Pick which in the viewport bar. Alt +click erases the same faces instead. Pick I Picks the color, roughness and metallic under the cursor, and switches the brush to those flat values. Hold I while painting for a quick pick: click, release I , and you are back on your previous tool. Alt +right-click picks with any tool. Select S Select"},{"p":"painting.html","a":"brush-settings","page":"Painting","t":"Brush settings","x":"The viewport bar holds the settings you change most: size, hardness, flow, opacity, spacing, projection mode and the topology and island locks . When the viewport is narrow, the bar continues on a second row. The Properties panel has all of them. Setting Meaning Size Diameter in screen pixels, which is what you see. In Surface 3D mode it is a size in object units. Hardness 0 gives a soft, feathered edge. 1 gives a crisp edge. Flow How much each dab adds. Low flow builds up gradually, like an airbrush. Opacity The ceiling for a whole stroke. No matter how often you go over an area within one stroke, it never exceeds this. Spacing The distance between dabs as a fraction of the diameter. Use small values for smooth lines and large values for stamps. Tip The brush shape. There are 16 procedural tips (round, square, star, splatter, chalk, scratches, grunge, cracks\u2026), or Image tip\u2026 loads any grayscale image. Angle / Roundness Rotation and squash of the tip. Follow path (under Dynamics) turns the tip along the stroke. Dynamics Size, angle and flow jitter, scatter, lazy mouse (the brush trails behind the cursor for smooth lines), and what pen pressure controls. The Brushes tab has 27 prese"},{"p":"painting.html","a":"your-brushes","page":"Painting","t":"Your own brushes","x":"Every image in your brushes folder is a brush (1.2): PNG, JPEG, TGA, BMP, GIF, PSD or EXR. Each folder inside it is a group of its own in the Brushes panel, and your groups come first. A Photoshop brush file ( .abr ) there gives a brush for each of its tips. Patina looks at the folder every few seconds, so a file you add shows up by itself, and one you remove goes. Three images in the brushes folder, one of them in a folder of its own: they are the first groups of the Brushes panel. The leaf was drawn dark on white, and paints with the leaf. Import brushes... at the top of the Brushes panel copies images or .abr files into the folder. So does dropping them on the panel. Open the folder beside it opens the folder itself. It is ~/.local/share/patina/user/brushes on Linux and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Patina\\user\\brushes on Windows. Patina reads a tip the way it was made. A dark shape on a light ground paints with the dark part, as Photoshop, GIMP and Krita tips are made. A light shape on a dark ground paints with the light part. An image with transparency paints with its opaque part. A brush's tip and size can be changed and saved as a preset like any other. Agents use import_brushes and reload"},{"p":"painting.html","a":"projection-modes","page":"Painting","t":"Projection modes","x":"Mode Use it for Screen (default) Projects the brush from the view, like Substance Painter. You get what you see, which suits most work. Surface 3D A sphere brush that sits on the surface. Its size is set in object space, so it stays the same however you zoom, and it wraps around corners. UV Dabs are placed in texture space. Use this for decals that should follow the UV layout. Under Projection you also find Occlusion . With it off (or with the paint_through preset), the brush also reaches surfaces hidden behind others. Backface culling and Angle falloff fade the paint out on surfaces that turn away from you."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"smudge","page":"Painting","t":"Smudge and seams","x":"Smudge ( J ) picks up the paint under the brush and drags it along the stroke. It works in 3D like the other tools: the drag follows the surface, so it isn't stretched by the UV layout, and it runs on into the next island across a seam. Every channel the brush paints is smudged, so height and roughness move with the color. Flow sets how much paint each step carries (about 0.5 smears, 1 pushes the paint along with the brush), and pen pressure can control it under Dynamics . Symmetry copies each smudge their own way. Blur and smudge carry on across UV seams: where an island ends at a seam, they read the matching texels of the neighboring island, or of the other edge of the texture when an island wraps around (a cylinder, the sphere's seam). So a blur along a seam leaves no hard line. Across UV seams in Properties turns this off. Patina works out the seams once per UV layout (in well under a second at 2K). Red paint up to the sphere's seam (the red line). Left: the blur stops at the seam. Right: it carries on."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"stencils","page":"Painting","t":"Stencils, decals and text","x":"A stencil is a picture laid over the 3D view. It stays where it is on the screen while you turn the model, so you can line it up, and then the brush paints through it. Load one under Properties \u203a Stencil & text ( Image\u2026 ), or make one from text. A text stencil, shown over the view, lined up on the head. After Project onto the model . It follows the curved surface. Setting What it does Mode: Mask The stencil only shapes the paint: white and opaque parts let it through, black or transparent parts block it. You paint the brush material through it, like spray paint through a cut-out. Mode: Decal The stencil's own colors are painted, with roughness, metallic and the other channels from the brush material. Use it for logos, labels and photos. Invert \u00b7 Tile Paint where the stencil is dark instead \u00b7 repeat it across the whole view (patterns, mesh, perforations). Position \u00b7 Scale \u00b7 Rotation Or hold T in the view: drag to move it, Shift +drag to rotate it, the wheel to scale it. Fit centers it. Overlay \u00b7 Show How strongly the stencil is drawn over the view. Hiding it doesn't switch it off. Project onto the model Paints the whole stencil in one step onto everything the camera sees, with the b"},{"p":"painting.html","a":"text","page":"Painting","t":"Text","x":"Under Stencil & text \u203a Text , type one or more lines and press Make text stencil . Patina renders the text into a stencil image (decal mode when the color isn't white), which you place and paint like any other stencil. With Live on, edits to the text update the stencil straight away. Fonts: four are included (DejaVu Sans, Sans Bold, Serif and Sans Mono), and the list also shows the fonts installed on the computer. File\u2026 uses any .ttf or .otf file. Size, color, outline (width and color), alignment , letter and line spacing. Save image\u2026 writes the stencil as a PNG, for example to use the text as an image material or brush tip. Claude sets all of this with set_stencil ( text , font , font_size , outline \u2026) and paints it with project_stencil ; list_fonts lists the fonts."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"stroke-locks","page":"Painting","t":"Topology lock and island lock","x":"Two toggles on the viewport bar keep a stroke where it started. They work with the Paint, Erase, Blur, Smudge and Clone tools, in every projection mode, and in the UV editor's paint mode. Toggle Key A stroke only paints\u2026 Topology lock Alt + T the connected piece of mesh it starts on. Pieces that are separate in the mesh (eyes, buttons, straps, a separate lid) stay clean, even where they touch or overlap on screen. Island lock Alt + L the UV island it starts on. The stroke stops at the island's seams, so you can paint one panel of a hard-surface model, or one side of a box, right up to the edge. Set lock (none) the texture set it starts on, or, with a single texture, its material slot: the skin, not the collar next to it. Shown when the model has several sets or slots. The region is chosen by the first dab that lands on the model, so you can start a stroke beside the model and drag onto it. With symmetry on, the mirrored part is unlocked as well, so both sides get painted. With both toggles on, the island lock decides (an island never spans two separate pieces of mesh). The toggles are remembered between sessions, and Claude can set them with set_brush ( lock_topology , lock_island "},{"p":"painting.html","a":"colors","page":"Painting","t":"Colors","x":"When the brush paints a flat color (no library material selected), the Colors panel next to Properties is the place to choose it. Primary and secondary: the large square is the color you paint with. X (or Swap ) swaps it with the secondary color, like the foreground and background colors in an image editor. Picker: a full color picker with a hex field. Choosing a color here switches the brush from a library material to flat color. Recent: the last 16 colors you painted with or picked from the model. Swatches: your own palette. + adds the primary color. Right-click a swatch to replace it, use it as the secondary color or remove it. Swatches, recent colors and the secondary color are kept between sessions, together with the brush, the material, the tool and the symmetry settings."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"lines-symmetry-and-pressure","page":"Painting","t":"Lines, symmetry and pressure","x":"Straight lines: paint once, then Shift +click somewhere else. Patina draws a line from the end of the last stroke. Mirror symmetry: Mirror X / Y / Z in the tool strip mirror every dab across the object's axes. They combine: X and Z together paint four copies, all three paint eight. Shift + X toggles X. The copies paint the other side even when the camera can't see it: a copy that is out of view or facing away goes straight onto the model's surface. Radial symmetry: Radial in the tool strip repeats every dab 2\u201332 times around the X, Y or Z axis, for wheels, rosettes, bolts around a flange and so on. It works together with the mirrors. All copies go through the object's origin, so the model should be centered on it. Pen pressure: works with drawing tablets on Wayland, X11 and Windows. By default pressure controls size; flow and opacity are optional under Dynamics. See Pen tablets . Two strokes with radial symmetry, 8 copies around Y."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"pen-tablets","page":"Painting","t":"Pen tablets","x":"Patina reads drawing tablets (Wacom, Huion, XP-Pen and others) directly. It uses every sample the tablet sends, typically 130 to 266 per second, so fast strokes keep their shape and their pressure, independent of the frame rate. Feature What it does Pressure Size, flow and/or opacity, chosen per brush under Properties \u203a Dynamics . Mouse strokes always paint at full pressure, even right after using the pen. Pressure curve Feel (softer: light touches already paint strongly; firmer: press harder), Ignore below (pressure that counts as none, for pens that register when they only rest on the tablet) and Full pressure at (so a light hand still reaches full size). In Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Pen tablet and the test window. Eraser end Turn the pen around and it erases, whatever the tool. Switch it off with The eraser end erases . Barrel buttons Each button can be a right click, a middle click (which orbits or pans, depending on the navigation preset ), swap colors, undo, or nothing. The defaults are right click (lower button) and middle click (upper button). Tilt Pen tilt turns the tip (under Dynamics) points non-round tips the way the pen leans. Needs a pen with tilt (Wacom Pro Pen, most Huio"},{"p":"painting.html","a":"checking-a-tablet","page":"Painting","t":"Checking a tablet","x":"Help \u203a Pen Tablet Test with four strokes whose pressure rises, falls, swells and pulses. Open Help \u203a Pen Tablet Test\u2026 (also Pen tablet settings\u2026 under Dynamics, and Test the pen\u2026 in Preferences). Hover over the window: the status line should say Pen: hovering . Draw in the pad, pressing lightly, then hard. The bars show the raw pressure and the pressure after the curve, and the graph shows the last four seconds. Check the numbers: the Range seen should go from about 0 to close to 1. Samples per second while you move the pen should be about 100 or more. Turn the pen round to see Eraser seen , lean it to see the tilt. Set Feel until light and hard strokes feel right in the pad. The settings are kept between sessions. If something is off, Copy report puts a description of what Patina receives on the clipboard (the system, the event counts, the pressure range, the findings) for a bug report. Claude can read the same with the pen_info command."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"setting-up-the-driver","page":"Painting","t":"Setting up the driver","x":"Linux. Wacom tablets work through the kernel's wacom driver and libinput, with nothing to install. Huion tablets work through the kernel's hid-uclogic driver: most models get pressure on a current kernel such as Fedora's. GNOME ( Settings \u203a Wacom Tablet ) and KDE ( System Settings \u203a Drawing Tablet ) set the tablet area, the pen buttons and the pressure feel for the whole desktop; Patina's curve comes on top of that. To check what the system sees, install libinput-utils and run sudo libinput list-devices (the tablet should list the tablet capability) and sudo libinput debug-tablet (shows the pressure while you press). Windows. Patina reads tablets through Windows Ink, so it must be enabled in the tablet driver: in Wacom Tablet Properties , the Mapping tab, tick Use Windows Ink ; in the Huion Tablet app, under Digital Pen , tick Enable Windows Ink . Restart Patina after changing it. Patina doesn't use the older Wintab interface."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"channels","page":"Painting","t":"Channels","x":"A paint stroke writes several channels at once: base color, roughness, metallic, height, normal and emissive, and opacity, sheen, clearcoat and subsurface when the brush's material has them (or the layer already has them, so the eraser, blur, smudge and clone reach them). A plain brush leaves those four alone. In the Brush panel's Paint channels you choose which channels strokes write. Unticking a channel in the layer's Channels row stops that layer affecting it. For example, a varnish layer might change only roughness. Each channel also has its own opacity under Channel opacity ."},{"p":"painting.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Painting","t":"Gotchas","x":"Paint appears in two places at once Two parts of the model share the same part of the texture. This is common with mirrored halves and with stacked islands, as in the DamagedHelmet sample. Painting one copy paints the other. To fix it, give the mesh unique UVs: UV \u203a Unwrap \u203a Smart UV Project , then Pack Islands . More\u2026 \u201cNothing to paint\u201d The selected layer is hidden, all its channels are unticked, or no layer is selected. Fill and adjustment layers paint their mask , which is invisible if the layer itself shows nothing yet. Strokes look blocky The texture resolution is too low for how close you're working, or that part of the UVs is small (check with the UV checker view). Raise the resolution in Texture Set . Your layers are resampled, not lost. X no longer toggles symmetry Since 0.3, X swaps the primary and secondary color. The X mirror moved to Shift + X . To get the old key back, change it in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts . Quick pick stays on the picker A short tap of I without clicking selects the Pick tool and stays there (the old behavior). Only holding I , or clicking while it is held, returns to the previous tool when you let go. \u201cMy stroke stops for no reason\u201d The topology or"},{"p":"materials.html","a":"","page":"Materials","t":"Materials","x":"Materials A material is a complete surface: color, roughness, metallic, height (bumps) and emission, all at once. Patina paints materials. You don't paint each map on its own. The Materials panel. P marks materials from the Patina default library."},{"p":"materials.html","a":"the-patina-default-library","page":"Materials","t":"The Patina default library","x":"Patina ships with 270 materials. All of them are made by Patina's own procedural generators, so they tile seamlessly at any resolution and carry no third-party licenses. Their ids all start with patina_ (for example patina_rust_heavy ), so they never clash with your own materials. Category Examples Metal iron, brushed steel and aluminum, scratched steel, gold, copper, chrome, brass, rust, diamond plate, hammered copper and steel, corrugated (galvanized / rusty), perforated, cast iron, anodized titanium, gunmetal, bronze verdigris, tarnished silver, galvanized spangle, expanded metal, chain-link fence, woven wire mesh, knurled steel and aluminum, scale armor, heat-tinted steel, verdigris crust, chainmail (steel, rusty), damascus steel (random, ladder) Paint chipped paint (red, yellow, green), peeling white, powder coat, car paint (blue, red, pearl), camouflage (woodland, desert, urban), crazed glaze, alligatored white paint, craquelure varnish Stone concrete, plaster, granite, cobblestone, bricks (red, old, running bond), brick pavers (herringbone, basket weave), concrete blocks, tiles (white, checker), hexagonal tiles (white, rosette, terracotta), marble (white, nero), terrazzo, sl"},{"p":"materials.html","a":"surface","page":"Materials","t":"Opacity, sheen, clearcoat and subsurface","x":"Besides color, roughness, metallic, height and glow, a material has four more properties (Patina 0.26): Property What it is for Opacity 1 is solid; lower lets what is behind show through. Lace, fishnet, leaves and hair cards cut holes with it, chiffon and glass are partly see-through. Sheen A soft light at grazing angles, the way velvet, satin, fur and peach-skin catch the light. Clearcoat A smooth varnish over the surface: car paint, lacquered wood, nail polish, polished stone. It follows the shape, not the bumps under it. Subsurface Light that goes into the material and comes out softer and warmer: skin, wax, leaves, jade, marble. The skin, satin, velvet, car paint, marble and hair materials of the library have them now, and many of the new materials use them. You paint them like the other properties: a brush paints them where its material has them, a fill layer brings them with its material, and every layer has an Opacity , Sheen , Clearcoat and Subsurface channel (see Layers ). The base material below all layers has them too. The 3D view shows them in the Material view, and the view list has a view of each. Exports write their maps only when the texture set uses them (see Expor"},{"p":"materials.html","a":"material-settings","page":"Materials","t":"Material settings","x":"When you paint, the material is projected onto the model. Properties \u203a Paint material (and the same block in a fill layer's settings) controls how: Setting Meaning Projection Tri-planar (the default) projects from three directions in object space, so it is seamless and ignores UVs. UV follows the UVs, which suits labels or anything that must follow the layout. Screen projects the image from the camera, like a decal or a stencil. Scale / Rotation The pattern size and its angle. Tint Multiplies the color. One rust can become many. Rough scale / offset Makes the material glossier or duller. Height scale, Normal strength How strong the bumps are. Set 0 to paint color without relief."},{"p":"materials.html","a":"import-a-scanned-material","page":"Materials","t":"Import a scanned material","x":"Use File \u203a Import Material Folder\u2026 , or drop a folder onto the window. Patina recognizes the usual file names from ambientCG, Poly Haven, Quixel and Substance exports ( *_Color , *_BaseColor , *_Roughness , *_Metalness , *_NormalGL / *_NormalDX , *_Displacement , *_AO , *_Emission \u2026). The material is copied into your library, so the original folder can be deleted. The status bar says how many maps were found, and the Console lists which file each channel came from."},{"p":"materials.html","a":"substance","page":"Materials","t":"Materials from Substance","x":"Textures exported from Substance 3D Designer, Painter or Sampler, and materials downloaded from Substance 3D Assets as images, import directly, whichever export preset was used: What is in the folder What Patina does Separate maps ( Document channels , PBR Metallic Roughness ) Each file feeds its channel. *_OcclusionRoughnessMetallic (Unreal preset), *_ORM , *_ARM Splits the packed file: green is roughness, blue is metallic, red is ambient occlusion. *_metallicRoughness (glTF) Green is roughness, blue is metallic. *_MaskMap (Unity HDRP), *_MetallicSmoothness (Unity URP / Standard) Red is metallic, the alpha channel is smoothness (turned into roughness), the mask map's green is occlusion. *_BaseMap , *_AlbedoTransparency Base color. Several texture sets in one folder ( Robot_Body_BaseColor.png , Robot_Head_BaseColor.png \u2026) One material per texture set, named after it ( Robot - Body , Robot - Head ). EXR, HDR and 16-bit PNG maps Read at full precision. A height map in scene units (an EXR displacement) is fitted into Patina's height range, and linear EXR color is converted to sRGB. Normal maps: a name that says OpenGL , DirectX , GL or DX decides the convention. A plain *_Normal next "},{"p":"materials.html","a":"images-and-decals","page":"Materials","t":"Images and decals","x":"Drop a single image onto the viewport and it becomes a color-only material with Screen projection. That makes it a stencil: aim the view, then paint the logo or photo onto the model. Drop it onto the Brushes panel instead and it becomes a brush tip."},{"p":"materials.html","a":"the-material-editor","page":"Materials","t":"The material editor","x":"New procedural\u2026 , or Edit / duplicate\u2026 on any library material, opens the editor. Pick a generator (there are 74, grouped by kind, from rust and wood to diamond plate, sci-fi panels, skin, hand-painted stone, prints and stained glass), set its colors and values, and watch the live sphere and tile at the top of the window update. Save to library stores it under your user folder. Each generator's colors and settings are named for what they do: Brick , Second brick and Mortar for bricks, then Joint width , Bond , Wear and Length : height . A color the generator doesn't use is hidden. A setting with a choice of styles (a brick bond, a stripe style, an animal) is a drop-down. Where 0 stands for a default, the slider says default and the value it uses. Claude reads the same descriptions with list_generators ."},{"p":"materials.html","a":"wear-and-wobble","page":"Materials","t":"Wear and wobble","x":"Three settings work with every generator, at the foot of the editor: Setting Does Grime, Grime color Dirt settles in the low parts of the pattern: the grout, the mortar, the gaps between planks, cracks and grooves. They also get rougher, and a thin film covers the rest. Worn edges, Edge color The raised edges of the pattern wear to the edge color and get a little smoother: chipped brick arrises, polished tread, rubbed-through paint. Warp, Warp size Wobbles the whole pattern: wavy stripes, bricks laid by hand, hand-drawn checks. All three keep the material seamless. They work from the generator's own height, so a pattern with no relief (a flat print) has no grooves to fill and no edges to wear. The generators added in 0.24: Generator Settings Stripes stripe width \u00b7 style: plain, pinstripe, chevron, wavy, three colors \u00b7 direction: across, along, diagonal \u00b7 woven (0 a smooth print, 1 woven threads) Checks style: checkerboard, gingham, houndstooth, argyle, windowpane \u00b7 line width \u00b7 woven \u00b7 argyle diamond height Dots dot size \u00b7 layout: grid, staggered, scattered, halftone \u00b7 raised (0 printed, 1 domed studs) \u00b7 woven Animal print animal: leopard, cheetah, zebra, tiger, giraffe, cow \u00b7 fur "},{"p":"materials.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Materials","t":"Gotchas","x":"Built-in materials can't be changed in place Editing a P material creates a copy called user_patina_\u2026 in the User category, and the original stays as it was. That is deliberate: your projects keep working when Patina is updated. Old projects and the patina_ prefix Projects from Patina 0.1 refer to materials without the prefix ( rust_heavy ). They still open, because the old ids are mapped to the patina_\u2026 materials automatically. Normal maps from other tools Some materials come with DirectX-style normal maps (green channel flipped) whose names don't say so. If the bumps of an imported material look inverted, right-click it in the Materials panel \u203a Normal map is DirectX . Claude and the API can pass \"normal\": \"directx\" to import_material_folder . An imported material has no roughness or metal The packed map has a name Patina doesn't know. Check the Console for the list of files it used, and rename the packed file so it ends in _ORM (red occlusion, green roughness, blue metallic) or _MaskMap , then import again. Tri-planar on curved shapes Tri-planar projection meets in a seam where a surface faces diagonally. Since 0.7.4 the seams are by height : at each point, the projection whose s"},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Masks and smart materials: a beginner's guide","x":"Masks and smart materials: a beginner's guide If you have never used Substance Painter or a similar tool, masks and smart materials can be the least obvious parts of Patina. They are also what gets you from \"a model painted one color\" to \"a believable, worn object\" in a few minutes. This page explains what they are and why they work the way they do, then walks through a complete example from an empty model to a smart material you can reuse on any other model. How to read this page The first half explains the ideas, and the second half ( the walkthrough ) has you build one material. If you prefer learning by doing, open samples/Suzanne.glb and jump straight to the walkthrough. The reference pages Layers and masks and Folders, solo and smart materials list every setting."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"the-big-idea-layers-of-material-shaped-by-masks","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"The big idea: layers of material, shaped by masks","x":"In a 2D paint program you usually paint color directly onto the picture. Texture painters work differently. A real worn object is made of several materials on top of each other : steel, a coat of paint over it, dirt on the paint, a rust stain here and there. Patina builds a surface the same way, as a stack of layers , each holding one material: The bottom layer covers everything, for example blue paint. The next layer up holds a different material, for example bare steel, but it should only show where the paint has worn off . Above that, dirt, but only in the creases . The part in italics is the mask . A mask is a black-and-white image, wrapped around the model like the textures are, that decides where a layer shows: Layer: rust material \u00d7 Its mask black: hidden white: shown + Layer below = Result White in the mask shows the layer, black hides it and lets the layer below show through, and gray blends the two. The soft gray band in the middle is why the rust fades into the paint instead of ending in a hard line. That is all a mask is. The rest of this page is about the different ways of making that black-and-white image: painting it by hand, letting Patina work it out from the shape"},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"why-not-just-paint-the-rust-on-directly","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Why not just paint the rust on directly?","x":"You can (a paint layer and a rust material will do it). Masks are better for most surface work for three reasons: Nothing is destroyed. The paint layer is still complete underneath the rust. Erase some of the mask and the paint comes back exactly as it was. Painted directly, the paint under the rust would be gone. You can change your mind about the material later. Swap the rust layer's material for dried mud and the same shape now holds mud. With direct painting you would repaint everything. Masks can be calculated. Patina can make a mask from the model's shape: every edge, every crease, every upward-facing surface. That is a lot of careful painting you don't have to do, and it works on any model, which is what makes smart materials possible."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"fill-layers-the-layer-type-masks-are-made-for","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Fill layers: the layer type masks are made for","x":"Most of the layers in a material stack are fill layers ( Fill layer in the Layers panel). A fill layer covers the whole model with one material, and then its mask cuts it down. Two consequences that confuse people at first: When you add a fill layer, the whole model changes, because a new fill layer has a white mask: it shows everywhere. When you paint on a fill layer, you are painting its mask , not color. Brush strokes paint white and reveal the material, and the eraser paints black and hides it. The material comes from the layer settings, not the brush. So there are two ways to start a fill layer, depending on what you want: Start with How Then Use it for White mask (shown everywhere) Fill layer Pick a smart mask, or paint black where it should not be. Base materials, and anything a smart mask can place (wear, dirt, dust). Black mask (hidden everywhere) The arrow next to Fill layer \u25b8 Fill Layer with a Black Mask Paint white where the material should appear. Hand-placed details: a rust patch, a stain, a colored stripe. Paint layers and folders can have masks too ( Add mask on the layer's row), and adjustment layers use their mask to limit where the adjustment applies. Everything "},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"the-kinds-of-mask","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"The kinds of mask","x":"Patina has three ways of making a mask, and you will usually mix them."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"painted-masks","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Painted masks","x":"Click the mask thumbnail on the layer's row (or the Mask button) and paint. While you paint a mask, the viewport label says [mask] . The Paint tool paints white (or the gray set by Mask value in the Properties panel), the Erase tool ( E ) paints black, and all the usual brush settings apply (size, hardness, flow, pressure, symmetry). To go back to painting the layer itself, click the layer's name. A painted mask follows exactly what you did, so it suits details that belong to this model only: a scorch mark next to an exhaust, a worn patch where a hand grips a handle. You can also make a mask from a selection: select faces, an island or a material slot, then right-click the layer \u25b8 Mask from the Face Selection ."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"smart-masks","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Smart masks","x":"A smart mask is worked out from the shape of the model, using maps that Patina bakes (calculates once and stores) for it: curvature, ambient occlusion and thickness. Pick one in the layer settings, in the Smart mask combo. Patina bakes whatever it needs the first time. Smart mask Shows the layer on\u2026 Typical use Edges Convex edges and corners: anything that sticks out and gets knocked. Paint worn through to metal, polished edges, chipping. Cavity Creases, seams and crevices. Dirt and grime, dark lines in panel gaps. Occlusion Places light reaches poorly: deep corners, under overhangs. Soot, moss, dust that collects out of reach. Thin parts Thin parts of the model: ears, fins, blades, cloth edges. Light shining through skin or leaves, worn-through fabric. Facing direction Surfaces facing one way (up, down, front\u2026). Snow and dust on top, moss underneath. Height gradient The bottom (or top) of the object. Mud on the lower half, water lines. Noise Random organic patches. Stains, color variation. Material slot One material slot from Blender or the model file. A different material per part, with no painting. Every smart mask has the same four controls, and they are worth understanding, be"},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"mask-effect-stacks-combining-masks","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Mask effect stacks: combining masks","x":"Sometimes one smart mask is not enough: wear on the edges, but only on the top half, softened, with a few spots scraped away by hand. A mask effect stack builds a mask from several steps: smart masks, painted masks, noise, levels, blur and invert, each with a blend mode such as multiply or add. Choose Add a mask effect\u2026 in the mask settings to turn a plain mask into a stack. You don't need stacks to get started; come back to them when a single smart mask can't do what you want."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"smart-and-painted-together","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Smart and painted together","x":"A layer can have a smart mask and a painted mask at the same time. They multiply: the layer shows where both are white. That is the usual way to fix a smart mask that is right almost everywhere: keep the smart mask, then paint black on the mask where the wear shouldn't be (the inside of a handle, say)."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"seeing-a-mask","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Seeing a mask","x":"A mask is hard to judge from the finished material, so Patina can show you the mask itself: The Layer Mask view. Choose Layer Mask in the view combo of the viewport bar. The model shows the selected layer's mask in black and white: the effective mask, meaning the painted mask, the smart mask and any effect stack combined, exactly as the layer uses it. Tune Level and Contrast while you watch it, then switch back to Material . Solo. Alt +click a layer's eye to show only that layer (over the base), so you can see what it adds. Alt +click again to show everything. Toggling. Click a layer's eye to hide it and compare. The compare view shows before and after side by side."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"walkthrough-a-worn-metal-finish-step-by-step","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Walkthrough: a worn metal finish, step by step","x":"This builds a blue painted metal that is worn through on the edges, dirty in the creases and rusty in one spot, on Suzanne, and then turns it into a smart material. It takes about ten minutes. Open samples/Suzanne.glb ( File \u203a Open\u2026 ). The steps below, in order. The last image is the rust layer's mask in the Layer Mask view. Make a folder for the material. Click Folder and double-click its name to rename it, for example Worn blue metal . Everything else goes inside it, which keeps the stack tidy and lets you save the whole folder as a smart material at the end. New layers go into the selected folder. The base: blue paint. With the folder selected, pick Car Paint Blue in the Materials panel and click Fill layer . The whole model turns blue. Rename the layer Paint . This layer needs no mask: it is the bottom of the material. Bare steel on the edges. Pick Steel Scratched and click Fill layer again. The model is now all steel, because the new layer's mask is white. In the layer settings, set Smart mask to Edges . The steel retreats to the edges and corners, and the paint comes back everywhere else. Rename it Bare steel . Tune the edge mask. Switch the view to Layer Mask . Move Level up"},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"smart-materials-saving-the-recipe","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Smart materials: saving the recipe","x":"Look at what the folder you just built contains: three fill layers with smart masks, plus one painted mask. Apart from the rust patch, nothing in it is specific to Suzanne . \"Blue paint, steel on the edges with Level 0.65 and some noise, dirt in the occluded areas at 85%\" is a recipe, and the masks are recalculated from whatever model it is used on. A smart material is exactly that: a saved folder (or layer) of settings that you can apply to any model."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"save-it","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Save it","x":"Remove or keep the hand-painted parts. The rust patch was painted for Suzanne, in Suzanne's UV layout. On another model it would land in a random spot, so leave it out of a general-purpose smart material: delete the layer, or move it out of the folder. Right-click the folder \u25b8 Save as smart material\u2026 and give it a name. Leave Include painted textures and masks off, so only the settings are stored."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"use-it","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Use it","x":"Open another model, click Smart materials above the layer list and pick your material. It arrives as one folder, and Patina bakes the maps its smart masks need. The edges, cavities and occlusion are those of the new model: The smart material from the walkthrough, applied unchanged to a cube, a torus and a sphere. The steel lands on the cube's edges because the mask is recalculated for each shape. A smart material is a starting point, not a finished result. Every model is different, so open the folder and adjust: raise an Edges level on a model with many sharp edges, scale materials up on a large object, paint a few corrections into a mask."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"what-carries-over-and-what-doesn-t","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"What carries over and what doesn't","x":"Carries over to any model Stays with the original model Materials and their settings (scale, tint, roughness), layer opacity, blend modes, folder structure, adjustment layers, smart masks and their settings, mask effect stacks (except their painted step). Painted layers and painted masks, which are stored in the model's UV layout. Save with Include painted textures and masks to copy them between projects of the same model."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"built-in-smart-materials","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Built-in smart materials","x":"Patina comes with nineteen smart materials ( Smart materials ), listed in Folders, solo and smart materials : worn painted metal, scratched painted steel, rusted iron, aged brass, oxidized copper, heat-tinted metal, worn gold trim, worn sci-fi panels, dusty and grimy plastic, worn leather, dirty fabric, weathered and mossy wood, mossy stone, living skin, and three overlays that go on top of another material: snow cover, edge wear + grime, and stylized edge highlights for toon and hand-painted materials. Open their folders to see how they are built: each one is a few fill layers with smart masks, like the walkthrough. Scratched painted steel also shows a mask effect stack (paint worn away by an edge mask and by chips from a noise mask), Heat-tinted metal stacks three height gradients into color bands, and Living skin uses the Thin parts mask to warm the ears and nose. Your own smart materials are files in ~/.local/share/patina/user/smart_materials (on Windows, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\patina\\user\\smart_materials ). Copy one to share it with someone else."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"tips-for-good-results","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Tips for good results","x":"Think in physical order. Stack layers the way the object was made and aged: base metal, primer, paint, then wear, dirt and stains on top. Wear that reveals metal is easiest as a steel layer above the paint with an edge mask. Fewer, better layers. Three or four well-tuned fill layers usually look better than ten. Always add some noise. Real wear and dirt are never uniform. Check the mask, not just the result. The Layer Mask view shows you immediately whether a mask catches the right places. Bake again after big mesh or UV changes. Smart masks come from baked maps. If you change the model or its UVs a lot, use Bake \u203a All Mesh Maps so the masks follow. Low-poly models work. The edge mask is measured on the surface at a fixed scale, so a simple box gets clean worn edges, and a smooth sphere is not treated as one big edge."},{"p":"masks-guide.html","a":"glossary","page":"Guide: masks & smart materials","t":"Glossary","x":"Term Meaning Layer One step in the stack: paint, a fill (one material everywhere), an adjustment, or a folder of other layers. Mask A black-and-white image deciding where a layer shows. White shows, black hides, gray blends. Fill layer A layer that covers the model with one material. Painting on it paints its mask. Smart mask (generator) A mask calculated from the model's shape: edges, cavities, occlusion, thin parts, direction, height, noise or material slot. Mask effect stack A mask built from several steps (smart masks, paint, noise, levels, blur) combined with blend modes. Bake / mesh maps Maps Patina calculates once from the model (curvature, ambient occlusion, thickness, position) that smart masks are built from. Curvature How convex (edges) or concave (creases) each point of the surface is. Ambient occlusion (AO) How much of the surrounding light reaches each point. Low in corners and crevices. Smart material A saved folder or layer: materials plus smart masks, reusable on any model. Solo Showing one layer on its own ( Alt +click its eye). Non-destructive Nothing is painted over permanently: every layer and mask can still be changed or removed."},{"p":"layers.html","a":"","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Layers and masks","x":"Layers and masks Each layer adds something on top of the layers below it. Fill and adjustment layers are shaped by masks, including smart masks that find edges and cavities on their own. They let you build a worn, believable surface without painting every scratch by hand. New to masks? Start with the beginner's guide to masks and smart materials , which explains what masks are for and builds a worn metal material step by step. This page is the reference. The stack: paint ( P ), fill ( F ), adjustment ( A ) and instance ( I ) layers, and folders, each with a thumbnail of its content and, when it has one, of its mask ( fx : an effect stack). The top of the list is the top of the stack. A fill layer's material and mask settings."},{"p":"layers.html","a":"layer-types","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Layer types","x":"Type Holds Painting on it\u2026 Paint Your strokes, with every channel. adds or erases paint. Fill One material over the whole model. edits the layer's mask . White shows the material and black hides it. Adjust Develops everything below, the way you would a photo: white balance, exposure, highlights and shadows, vibrance, saturation, hue, De-light, and roughness / metallic / height offsets (see Developing the textures ). edits the mask, which limits where the adjustment applies. Folder Other layers. Its opacity, blend mode and mask apply to all of them (see Folders, solo and smart materials ). edits the folder's mask. Baked map A baked map (ambient occlusion, curvature or thickness) with your fixes and adjustments, in the Baked maps section under the stack. It makes the map smart masks, the 3D view and exports read (see Fixing a bake ). Its check switches the map off in the 3D view and exports. paints fixes over the bake; Erase brings the bake back. Each layer has an opacity, a blend mode (normal, multiply, screen, overlay, add, subtract, darken, lighten, add signed, soft light) and a per-channel switch with its own opacity. Drag layers to reorder them or to drop them into a folder ( Ct"},{"p":"layers.html","a":"drag-and-drop","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Drag and drop","x":"Most things go into the stack by dragging them to where you want them. While you drag, the tooltip says what will happen where the pointer is, and an orange line or box shows the spot. Every drop is one undo step. Drag Onto What happens A material ( Materials tab) The middle of a fill layer's row The layer takes the material. Its mask, smart mask, mask effects, projection, scale and tint stay. A layer named after its old material takes the new one's name. The top or bottom edge of a row, or any other layer A new fill layer above or below it. The middle of a folder's row A new fill layer at the top of the folder. The Fill material box in the layer's settings The same as dropping it on the layer. The model in the viewport A new fill layer above the selected layer. The Add a mask effect\u2026 box in a layer's mask settings A Pattern effect from that material on top of the layer's mask, multiplied: the layer shows only where the pattern is. A Pattern effect's Material box The effect takes that material's pattern. A fill layer's thumbnail Another fill layer, or between layers Its material, with all its settings (tint, scale, projection), goes there. A smart material ( Smart materials tab) Be"},{"p":"layers.html","a":"masks","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Masks","x":"A paint layer can also have a mask. Click Add mask on its row, then the same button ( Mask ) to paint the mask rather than the layer. The viewport label shows [mask] while you are painting a mask. Add White Mask and Add black mask set the starting state. Black hides the whole layer, so you can then reveal it with the brush. From selection (in the mask settings, or right-click \u25b8 Mask from the Face Selection ) makes the mask white on the selected faces and black everywhere else. Select an island, a material slot or a painted region (the Island brush selects too), and the layer shows only there. It is one undo step, and you can keep painting the mask afterward."},{"p":"layers.html","a":"smart-masks","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Smart masks","x":"A smart mask is a procedural mask worked out from the model's shape. Pick one in the layer's Smart mask combo, or drag one from the Smart masks tab of the Materials panel onto the layer. Patina bakes the mesh maps it needs (ambient occlusion, curvature or thickness) the first time you choose it. The Layer Mask view mode shows the selected layer's effective mask: the painted mask, the smart mask and any effect stack combined. Smart mask Picks out Typical use Edges (curvature) Convex edges and corners Paint wearing off, rust and polish on edges Cavity Creases and crevices Dirt, grime, dust in corners Occlusion Areas that light reaches poorly Soot, moss, darkening Height gradient Bottom to top of the object Mud on the lower parts, water lines Noise 3D noise of a kind: clouds, cells (round spots), cracks (a network of lines), ridges (thin veins) or streaks (running down) Patchy variation, stains, spots, crazing, rain marks Facing direction Surfaces that face a direction (up, down, front\u2026) Snow and dust on top, drips and moss underneath, sun bleaching on one side Material slot The faces of one Blender material slot One material per part of the model, without painting a mask Thin parts ("},{"p":"layers.html","a":"develop","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Developing the textures","x":"An adjustment layer works on the finished look of everything below it, the way the basic adjustments in a photo editor work on a photo. It is the place to fix textures that came with a model (see models that come with textures ): too dark, too blue, too dull, or with shadows baked in. Nothing below is changed, so you can turn it off, lower its opacity, or paint its mask to develop only part of the model. Before and after: warmer, a little brighter, the shadows lifted, more color, and the baked shadow in the crevices taken out. Setting What it does Temperature , Tint White balance: cooler (blue) to warmer (yellow), and greener to more magenta. The brightness stays. Exposure In stops, as on a camera: +1 is twice the light, \u22121 half. Contrast , Brightness As before 0.33. Highlights , Shadows The bright parts and the dark parts on their own: lift the shadows of a dark scan without washing out the rest, or bring back a sky-lit top that came out too bright. Vibrance More (or less) color, mostly in the dull colors, so colors that are already strong and skin do not go garish. Saturation changes all colors alike. De-light Takes the shadows baked into the color out: scans and generated models"},{"p":"layers.html","a":"mask-effects","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Mask effect stacks","x":"One smart mask is often not enough: rust on the edges, but broken up by noise, a bit softer, and scraped away by hand in places. A mask effect stack builds a mask from several steps. Pick one in Add a mask effect\u2026 under the mask settings: the painted mask and the smart mask become the first two effects, and the new one goes on top. The stack starts white, and the effects apply from the bottom of the list up. The painted mask, the edges smart mask, noise multiplied in, then levels and a blur. The last effect is at the top of the list. Effect Does Paint The mask you paint (one per stack). Smart mask Any of the smart masks above, with its own settings. Fill A flat value. Noise 3D noise with scale, seed and contrast. Levels Remaps the stack so far: input range, gamma, output range. Blur Softens the stack so far (in texels), staying on the surface. Invert Flips the stack so far. Grow / shrink Spreads the white of the stack so far out (amount above 0) or pulls it in (below 0), in texels, staying on the surface. Edges Keeps only the edges of what the stack covers so far, as a band of the width you set: a pale rim round chips, a dark line round a patch. Pattern Any library material as a ma"},{"p":"layers.html","a":"instances","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Instances","x":"An instance (right-click \u203a Make an Instance , Alt + D ) is a linked copy of a layer: it shows the same paint or fill as its source, everywhere the source changes, but has its own mask, opacity, blend mode and place in the stack. Use one to repeat a decal layer in a second folder with another mask, or to put the same dirt pass at two strengths. Painting on an instance paints its source. Select the Source jumps to it, and Make independent turns the instance into an ordinary copy. Deleting the source hands its content to the first instance."},{"p":"layers.html","a":"a-typical-stack","page":"Layers & masks","t":"A typical stack","x":"Base: a fill layer with the main material, for example Painted Metal Chipped . Wear: a fill layer of Rust Heavy with the Edges smart mask, Level about 0.65 and some Noise. Dirt: a fill layer of Mud with the Occlusion or Cavity mask at 60\u201380% opacity. Details: a paint layer on top for decals, trims and hand-placed scratches. Color grade: an adjustment layer at the very top to try out color variations without repainting. Steps 1\u20133 are exactly what the built-in smart materials contain. Add Smart\u2026 \u25b8 Worn painted metal and adjust it, rather than building the stack from scratch."},{"p":"layers.html","a":"undo","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Undo","x":"Ctrl + Z undoes everything: strokes, fills, layer changes, every slider in the layer settings, masks and UV edits. The History panel lists the steps and lets you jump back to any of them. Patina stores only the parts of the textures that changed, so long sessions don't use up memory."},{"p":"layers.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Layers & masks","t":"Gotchas","x":"Comparing settings Every slider change is one undo step, so you can try a value and press Ctrl + Z . To see what a single layer contributes, Alt +click its eye ( solo ). \u201cI painted but nothing changed\u201d The selected layer is a fill or adjustment layer whose mask is already white, so painting white changes nothing. Use Add Black Mask first, or erase. Smart masks after a mesh change AO and curvature are baked for the current mesh and UVs. After you change the topology or the UVs a lot, re-bake them with Bake \u203a All Mesh Maps . Otherwise edge masks land in the wrong places. Height blends Height in non-normal blend modes is added as an offset around mid-gray (0.5). Darken and Lighten keep the lower or higher surface. That makes stacked dents and scratches add up the way you'd expect."},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Folders, solo and smart materials","x":"Folders, solo and smart materials A real texture quickly grows to twenty or thirty layers. Folders keep them tidy and let you mask a whole group at once. Solo shows what one layer actually contributes. Smart materials save a finished stack, so you can use it again on another model. And the History panel lets you step back through every change, sliders included. A Wear folder holding two fill layers. The colored strips on the left are color tags. Right-click any layer for the full menu."},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"folders","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Folders","x":"A folder groups layers. Everything inside it is composited first. The result then goes on top of the layers below the folder, through the folder's own opacity , blend mode , channels , mask and smart mask . Folders can hold other folders. To\u2026 Do this Make a folder Folder . It goes above the selected layer, or inside the selected folder. Put a layer in a new folder Select it and press Ctrl + G ( Group into a Folder ). Move a layer into a folder Drag it onto the middle of the folder's row. Or right-click it \u25b8 Move into Folder . Reorder Drag a layer onto the top or bottom half of another row (an orange line shows where it goes). move it within its folder. Take a layer out Drag it out of the folder, or right-click \u25b8 Move Out of the Folder . Open or close a folder Click the arrow on its row. The number after the name counts the layers directly inside it. New layers go above the selection. If a folder is selected, they go to the top of that folder. Deleting or duplicating a folder deletes or duplicates everything in it. Masking a whole group Put a base fill, an edge-wear fill and a dirt fill in one folder and give the folder a black mask. Painting the folder's mask now reveals the whole "},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"solo-see-what-one-layer-does","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Solo: see what one layer does","x":"Alt +click a layer's eye (visibility box) to solo it. Only that layer (or folder) is shown, over the base material. The eye gets a yellow ring and a Solo: \u2026 button appears above the list. Click that button, or Alt +click the eye again, to show everything. Alt + S solos the selected layer. The Wear folder soloed: just the rust and the dirt. Solo is only a way of looking. It does not change any layer's visibility, it is not saved, and exports always contain the whole stack. To export one layer on its own, use Export \u25b8 Only the selected layer (see Export )."},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"color-tags-names-and-layer-settings","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Color tags, names and layer settings","x":"Color tags: pick a color under the layer's name in the properties, or right-click \u25b8 Color Tag . A strip of that color marks the row. Rename: double-click the name in the list, or edit the Name field. Copy and paste settings: right-click \u25b8 Copy Layer Settings , then Paste Layer Settings on another layer. This copies the opacity, blend mode, channels, channel opacities and smart mask. Between two fill layers it also copies the material, and between two adjustment layers the adjustment. Fill with a black mask: the arrow next to Fill layer \u25b8 Fill Layer with a Black Mask adds a fill that stays hidden until you paint its mask. This is the usual way to paint a material onto part of a model. Clear paint: Clear paint in a paint layer's properties (or the right-click menu) removes everything painted on the layer and keeps its mask. It is undoable."},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"smart-materials","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Smart materials","x":"If smart materials are new to you, the beginner's guide builds one from scratch and explains what carries over to other models. A smart material is a saved layer or folder: usually a few fill layers with smart masks, plus adjustments. Because smart masks are worked out from the shape of the model, the same smart material looks right on any mesh. The Smart materials tab of the Materials panel shows them all: click one to add it above the selected layer, or drag it to where you want it in the stack (or onto the model). Smart materials above the layer list has them too. The first eight built-in smart materials on Suzanne. Top: aged brass, dusty plastic, edge wear overlay (on red plastic), mossy stone. Bottom: rusted iron, snow cover (on mossy stone), weathered wood, worn painted metal. And eleven more. Top: scratched painted steel, oxidized copper, grimy plastic, worn leather. Middle: dirty fabric, mossy wood, heat-tinted metal, worn gold trim. Bottom: worn sci-fi panels, living skin, stylized edge highlights on toon red and on hand-painted planks. Built-in What it is Worn painted metal Blue paint over scratched steel, worn off on the edges, dirt in the cavities. Rusted iron Cast iron"},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"saving-your-own","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Saving your own","x":"Right-click a layer or folder \u25b8 Save as smart material\u2026 (or Smart materials \u25b8 Save the selected layer\u2026 ). Give it a name, then decide whether to include painted textures and masks . Without paint (the default), the smart material keeps only settings: layers, materials, smart masks, adjustments, opacities and blend modes. It works on any mesh. With paint , painted channels and masks are stored too. Use this to copy hand-painted layers between projects of the same model. Paint follows the UV layout, so on a different mesh it lands in the wrong places. Your smart materials are JSON files in ~/.local/share/patina/user/smart_materials (on Windows, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Patina\\user\\smart_materials ). Smart materials \u25b8 Open the Smart Materials Folder takes you there. To share one, copy its file to another machine. A file with the same name as a built-in one replaces it."},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"history","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Undo and the History panel","x":"Everything that changes the project can be undone: strokes, fills, layers added, deleted, moved or grouped, every layer setting (opacity, blend mode, channels, materials, smart masks, adjustments, names, color tags), masks added or removed, Clear paint , smart materials and UV edits. Dragging a slider makes one step, recorded when you let go. The History panel (next to Properties) lists the steps. Click one to go back to that point. Later steps stay grayed out until you change something, and then they are dropped, as with undo. Painting stores only the parts of the textures that changed. Other steps keep a copy of the textures they change: a UV edit with Keep Paint on UV Edits copies every layer, which on a big model with several texture sets is several gigabytes. Opening a project, changing the texture resolution or loading a new mesh starts a new history."},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"undo-memory","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"How much undo keeps","x":"Since 1.2.7, undo memory has three parts, set in Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Undo : Setting Default What it does Undo steps 100 How many steps each object's history keeps (10 to 1000). Instant undo (graphics card) 1024 MB The latest steps keep their texture copies on the graphics card, so they undo at once. Past this much, older steps are compressed into RAM, a texture at a time while you aren't painting. They undo a moment slower: a few seconds for a step of several gigabytes. Undo memory (RAM) A quarter of your computer's memory, 1 to 8 GB The compressed steps. Past this much, the oldest steps are dropped, so undo never takes all your memory. The limits cover every object of the scene together. The latest step, and the one you just undid, always stay on the graphics card. Painted textures compress well: on a character with seven texture sets, a UV edit's 4.9 GB copy took 131 MB of RAM. Before 1.2.7, each object kept up to 1 GB on the graphics card and nothing in RAM, so on such a model only the latest UV edit could be undone. Undo is never written to disk . Steps come and go all the time, and writing them would wear out an SSD. The History panel shows how much the steps take, on the gra"},{"p":"organizing.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Folders & smart materials","t":"Gotchas","x":"Painting on a folder paints its mask As with fill and adjustment layers, the brush edits the folder's mask. A folder without a mask gets a black one at the first dab, which hides everything in the folder except where you paint. If you only want to hide a few spots, use Add White Mask first and then erase. \u201cMy layer disappeared\u201d Check for a Solo: \u2026 button above the list. Solo is still on, and it lasts until you turn it off (it is not saved with the project). Smart materials with paint on another model Paint is stored by UV position, not by surface position. On a different mesh it appears in random-looking places. Save without paint to share a look between models. Undo and slider drags A value is recorded when the slider is released. If you undo while still holding a slider, the drag is finished first and then undone as one step."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"","page":"Scenes","t":"Scenes: several objects","x":"Scenes: several objects A project can hold several models at once (new in 1.2): a character's body, its clothes, its eyes and its hair, or a prop and its stand. Each one is an object with its own layers, texture set and undo history. Patina places them in one scene and paints them where they are. It is not a modeler, but it builds a scene out. Three objects from one file. The hat is the one painted, so the layers below the list are its layers. The Object tool's gizmo is on it."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"objects-list","page":"Scenes","t":"The Objects list","x":"The Objects list sits at the top of the Layers panel. Click a row to paint that object. From then on the layers below the list, the UV editor, the bakes and the texture set are that object's. Its own undo history comes with it. The check box shows or hides an object in the 3D view, renders and exports. Double-click a name to rename it. Right-click a row to frame it, show only it, hide the others, put it on the ground, move it back to where it came in, copy it or remove it. Under the list, Placement has the object's location, rotation and scale, to type in or drag. With one object, the list is a single line with its name and the button to add a model."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"adding","page":"Scenes","t":"Adding models","x":"A file with several meshes (glTF, FBX or OBJ) opens as an object for each. A file with more than 24 meshes, like a kit of many small parts, opens as one. File \u203a Add a Model to the Scene ( Ctrl + Shift + I ), or the button at the right of the Objects list, adds a file beside what is there. A model added to the sphere Patina starts with takes its place. A model dropped on the window while one is open: Patina asks whether to add it or open it instead. Dropped on the Objects list, it is added right away. From Blender , select several meshes and press Send to Patina (see Several objects ). Each object comes in where its file puts it, so clothes exported from the same scene fit the body. Its texture set gets as many texels per meter as the object already open, at most its size. Small parts like eyes get smaller textures, and the detail stays the same across the parts."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"painting","page":"Scenes","t":"Painting the objects","x":"The brush paints the active object. The others are drawn with their own textures, and those in front hide what is behind them from the brush. A stroke that starts on another object does nothing, and the status line says which object it is. Double-click another object in the 3D view to paint it, or right-click it and choose Paint ... . Local view ( / ) shows only the active object. Press it again to show them all."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"object-tool","page":"Scenes","t":"The Object tool","x":"The Object tool ( W ) moves, turns and scales objects. A click on an object picks it. Its gizmo sits in the middle of the active one: Mode What you drag Move An arrow moves it along that axis. The middle square moves it in the view's plane. Turn A ring turns it about that axis. The outer ring turns it about the line of sight. Scale A handle scales it along its own axis. The middle square scales it evenly. Press W again for the next mode, or choose it on the tool's bar. Hold Ctrl to snap: moves go in round steps, turns in 15 degrees and scales in tenths. Esc or a right-click while dragging cancels. Each drag is one undo step in that object's history. The bar also has On the ground , which puts the lowest point at height 0, and Where it came in ."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"object-keys","page":"Scenes","t":"G, R and S","x":"As in Blender's Object Mode, you don't have to hit a handle. With the Object tool on and the pointer over the 3D view, press: Key What the mouse does G Moves the object in the view's plane. R Turns it about the line of sight as the pointer goes round its middle. S Scales it evenly. Away from its middle is bigger, toward it smaller. Then press X , Y or Z to keep to one axis. Press it again to go back to free. Type a number for an exact amount: S 0 . 5 halves the object, R Z 9 0 turns it a quarter about Z, and G X 2 moves it 2 along X. Backspace takes a digit back, and Ctrl snaps. A click or Enter keeps the change as one undo step. A right-click or Esc puts the object back. In the Object tool these keys come first: S doesn't pick the Select tool there, and X doesn't swap the colors. They are in Edit \u203a Keyboard Shortcuts with your other shortcuts, in both the Patina and the Blender keymap. An object turns and scales about its middle. Its texture-space positions don't change when it moves, so fills and procedural masks stay where they are on it."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"saving","page":"Scenes","t":"The project file","x":"Every object is saved in the one .patina file, with its layers, its place and which one was active. Patina 1.1 and older open such a file as its first object only."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"exporting","page":"Scenes","t":"Exporting a scene","x":"File \u203a Export Model writes every object shown into one file. glTF gets a node for each, where it is, with its own materials and textures. OBJ, STL and PLY get the vertices moved into the scene. Only ... exports the active one. Export Textures writes the active object's maps, or every object's, each set named after its object ( $mesh is the object's name). Renders and the path traced view include every object shown."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"agents","page":"Scenes","t":"For agents","x":"list_objects , add_object , select_object , set_object (name, shown, location, rotation, scale, reset, on the ground), remove_object , duplicate_object , isolate_object , frame_object and pick_object . open_mesh takes separate . export_model and export_textures take objects . Every other command works on the active object. The switch is Objects in a scene in Preferences \u203a Features."},{"p":"scene.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Scenes","t":"Gotchas","x":"Each object has a texture set of its own, and that takes graphics memory. Objects you are not painting free their compositing textures until they are painted again. Removing an object can't be undone: its history goes with it. Patina asks first. Retopology, the bake-down and the mesh tools work on the active object."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"","page":"UV editing","t":"UV editing","x":"UV editing The UV editor shows the model's texture layout over any texture map. You can paint the parts you want as islands, check and set texel density, unwrap, pack and transform islands, mark seams and stitch vertices. Paint you have already done stays on the model while the layout changes under it. Islands mode: one click selects a whole island (orange). The selection is mirrored on the model."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"why-edit-uvs-in-a-painter","page":"UV editing","t":"Why edit UVs in a painter?","x":"Patina paints in 3D, so a messy layout never spoils your strokes. The layout still decides two things: how many texture pixels each part gets , and whether two parts share pixels . If they share, painting one paints the other. Fixing those is a matter of a few clicks."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"island-brush","page":"UV editing","t":"The Island brush: paint a part, get an island","x":"The quickest way to a good layout doesn't need seams or the UV editor at all. Choose the Island tool ( L ), paint over the part of the model you want as one piece of texture (a sleeve, a lid, a panel), and press Enter (in the Blender keymap U , like Blender's Unwrap). Patina then: cuts seams around the painted area and removes any seams inside it; unwraps it with LSCM, which stretches, scales and twists it flat with the least distortion; turns it upright, so \u201cup\u201d on the model is \u201cup\u201d in the texture (or along its longest side); scales it to the target texel density , so it gets as many pixels per meter as the rest of the model; finds empty space in the layout for it, leaving every other island where it is. Only if there is no room left are all islands repacked at an equal density; moves the paint you already have with it, so nothing changes on the model. The whole thing is one undo step. Painting the brow with the Island brush (UV checker view). The circle is cyan while adding. After Enter : seams around the region, and the new island placed in free space at the bottom of the layout. Each stroke is one undo step ( Island Brush in the History panel), so Ctrl + Z takes a stray stroke "},{"p":"uv.html","a":"island-symmetry","page":"UV editing","t":"Symmetric islands","x":"With mirror symmetry on ( Mirror X / Y / Z in the tool strip), the Island brush paints the mirrored faces too, and Make island unwraps only one half: the faces on the positive side of the mirror plane. The other half gets the same UVs, mirrored, so both halves share one place in the texture : paint one ear and the other ear has it too, and the layout spends its pixels only once. A region that crosses the mirror plane (the face of a head) gets a seam along the plane, and its two halves are stacked the same way. Mirror X on: painting one ear selects both, and the island is made once, with the second ear stacked on top of it. The two halves stay linked: moving, rotating or scaling either half moves both , in the UV editor or with any UV tool, so they can't drift apart by accident. The UV editor marks an island with a stacked half on it with x2 . To give one side its own place in the texture (for text that must read the right way on both sides, say), right-click it \u203a Mirrored Halves \u203a Unstack (Make Independent) . If the halves of a project from Patina 0.6 were moved apart, the UV editor says so and Re-stack puts them back. Stacked mirrored halves are on purpose, so the overlap check ( "},{"p":"uv.html","a":"pinning","page":"UV editing","t":"Pinning islands","x":"Once an island is where you want it, pin it: select it and press P in the UV editor (or UV \u203a Pins \u203a Pin Selected Islands , in the menu bar or the UV editor's right-click menu). A pinned island keeps its UVs through every UV edit: dragging it, G / R / S , Pack, Unwrap, Orient, Fix Overlapping UVs and the Island brush all leave it where it is, and the UV editor tells you it is pinned instead of moving it. Packing and unwrapping put the other islands in the space around the pinned ones, at the pinned islands' texel density. Alt + P unpins. A pinned island (blue frame and pin) with its mirrored half stacked on it (x2). Trying to drag it shows why it doesn't move. The line at the bottom describes the island under the cursor. The Island brush pins the islands it makes (the Pin box next to Make island ; untick it to leave new islands unpinned). Pins cover whole islands, and pinning one half of a symmetric island pins the stacked half too. Pins are saved with the project, and undo restores them like any other edit. UV \u203a Select \u203a Pinned Islands finds them, and UV \u203a Pins \u203a Unpin Every Island releases them all."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"pins-3d","page":"UV editing","t":"Pinning in the 3D view","x":"You don't need the UV editor to pin or unpin. With the Select tool, the bar over the 3D view has Pin and Unpin for the selected faces' islands, and the number of selected faces that are pinned. The Island tool has Unpin too, for the painted faces. Alt + P unpins over the 3D view as well as in the UV editor. While either tool is chosen, pinned islands have blue stripes on the model, so you can see what is pinned before you select it. Right-click the model for Pin Its UV Island or Unpin Its UV Island , for the island under the pointer. The Select tool: the pinned islands have blue stripes, the selected face is orange, and the bar shows Pin, Unpin and how many of the selected faces are pinned."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"select-visible","page":"UV editing","t":"Only what the camera sees","x":"With the Select tool in Islands mode, a click or a drag takes the whole UV island under the pointer. That includes the parts you can't see: the inside of a dress, the back of a sleeve, a skin under clothes. Turn on Visible only on the tool's bar (0.45), and it takes just the island's faces the camera sees. Those are the faces turned toward you, on screen, and not hidden behind other faces. Turn the view and click again to add more of the island. Ctrl +click takes the seen faces off. Faces mode doesn't change, since the face under the pointer is always one you see. Agents use select_faces with visible: true . The switch is Select only what the camera sees in Preferences \u203a Features. A click on the skin, through the opening: the skin's whole island is selected (1152 faces), most of it hidden by the shell. With Visible only : just the part seen through the opening (356 faces)."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"loops","page":"UV editing","t":"Loops, rings and linked faces","x":"The Select tool picks faces in batches too (1.2). The clicks work the same in the UV editor, by the UV edge nearest the pointer: Do this To select Alt +click by an edge The loop of quads across that edge, both ways, until a face that isn't a quad, a border, or the loop closes. This is Blender's face loop: round a sleeve, along a belt. Ctrl + Alt +click by an edge The ring along that edge instead: the band the other way. Shift with either Adds the loop or ring to the selection. Connected on the tool's bar The whole connected parts the selected faces are in. Flat region on the tool's bar Grows the selection over the surface while it bends less than the angle beside the button, face to face: a box's side, a flat panel of armor. Right-click a face and open Select to do the same from that face, and to take its UV island or material slot. Loops and rings run through quads, so on a model made of triangles they stop at the face clicked. Each selection is one undo step. Agents use select_loop and select_linked ."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"face-sets","page":"UV editing","t":"Face sets","x":"A face set is a named, colored group of faces, kept in the project, like Blender's sculpt face sets (1.2). A face is in one set at most. Sets are for the parts you paint, fill or mask again and again: a belt, the soles, the trim of a coat. The Face Sets panel (UV \u203a Face Sets, a tab beside Properties) has them. The Face Sets panel: a row for each set, with its eye, its color, its faces and its trash; the active set's buttons under the list. The Face Sets view: each set in its color. The panel works like Blender's vertex groups (1.2.10). One set is active : its row is lit, and the buttons under the list work on it. Create from Selection makes a new set of the selected faces. They leave the sets they were in. Those sets stay, with their names and colors, even when no faces are left in them. Create Sets From... makes a set for each material slot, UV island or connected part, in place of the sets there were. Click a set to make it the active set. Double-click renames it, and the color swatch changes its color. The trash at the end of the row deletes the set. Its faces stay on the model, in no set. Under the list: Add Selection puts the selected faces in the active set, and Remove Select"},{"p":"uv.html","a":"texel-density","page":"UV editing","t":"Texel density","x":"Texel density is how many texture pixels one meter of surface gets (1 unit = 1 m in Blender). When it is the same everywhere, details and scratches are equally sharp all over the model. UV \u203a Islands \u203a Texel Density\u2026 shows the numbers for the current texture size and sets the target the Island brush uses: Target Means Fit the texture The whole surface would fill about 70% of the texture. Mirrored halves stacked on their other half share its texels, so they don't count. 1 texel per pixel With the model framed in a 1080p view (normal viewing), one texel lands on one screen pixel. Sharper than that is wasted. Match the layout What the existing islands in the 0\u20131 square use. It is the median by surface area, so neither one odd island nor a crowd of small ones skews it. Islands mostly outside 0\u20131 (tiled UVs) are left out. Custom A px/m value of your own, for example a studio standard such as 512 or 1024 px/m. Automatic matches the layout when there is one, up to twice Fit the texture (a denser layout overlaps heavily, and new islands at that density would not get room of their own). Otherwise it uses the smaller of the first two. When the texture is too small for one texel per pixel, the"},{"p":"uv.html","a":"density-and-stretch-heat-maps","page":"UV editing","t":"Density and stretch heat maps","x":"Two view modes (in the viewport's view combo, or the check boxes in the window) color the model instead of guessing from the checker: Texel density : green is on target, blue gets fewer pixels than the target, red more. This sample model's UVs are a projection, so they vary a lot. UV stretch : gray-blue is undistorted, yellow is stretched 1.5 times in one direction, red twice or more."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"overlaps","page":"UV editing","t":"Overlapping UVs","x":"When two islands share texture pixels, painting one paints the other. The UV editor shows a warning when that happens, and UV \u203a Fix Overlapping UVs moves the smaller island of each overlapping pair into free space (or repacks everything if there is no room). Mirrored halves that overlap on purpose are moved apart as well, so fix them only if you want each side painted separately. Faces with no UV area. A face whose UVs all sit on one point (or one line) gets no paint, and you can't see or click it in the UV editor. The editor warns when a model has some; Select selects them, so you can unwrap them (right-click \u203a Unwrap the Selection ). Before 0.11.1 such a face could catch clicks meant for other islands."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"orient","page":"UV editing","t":"Orient, free space, grow and shrink","x":"UV menu What it does Orient Upright Turns each island so that the model's up direction is up in the texture. Wood grain, text, drips and tiles then run the right way. Flat, horizontal islands are laid along their longest side. Orient Along Their Longest Side Turns each island so its smallest bounding box lies along U. Packs tighter. Move into Free Space Places the selected islands in empty parts of the layout without moving the others, so a finished layout (and its paint) stays put. Straighten (Rectify Quad Islands) Straightens quad strips and grids into exact rectangles (see below ). Mirror UVs (X) The \u2212X half takes the mirrored UVs of the +X half (see below ). Select \u203a Grow / Shrink Adds or removes one ring of faces ( Ctrl +Numpad + / - )."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"rectify","page":"UV editing","t":"Rectify quad islands","x":"Belts, straps, pipes, trims, planks and ladders are strips or grids of quads, and they are much easier to texture when their island is a straight rectangle: a tiling material, stripes or stitching then run straight along them. An unwrap or projection usually leaves such islands curved or wobbly. UV \u203a Islands \u203a Straighten (Rectify Quad Islands) straightens each island that is a grid of quads into an exact grid: rows become horizontal lines and columns vertical lines. Each column keeps its width and each row its height from the 3D model (averaged along the row), so nothing is stretched, and the island keeps its texel density and its position. Islands that would overlap afterward are moved into free space. A curved belt, projected from above, and the same island after Rectify: one straight strip with its segments at their real lengths. With a face selection it works on the islands of the selection, otherwise on every island. Islands that are not a clean quad grid (triangles, or a vertex where five quads meet) are skipped, and the status line says how many. It is one undo step, and paint moves with the UVs like any other UV edit. Claude uses uv_rectify ."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"mirror-uvs","page":"UV editing","t":"Mirror UVs","x":"On a symmetric model, the two halves can share one place in the texture so that the layout spends its pixels only once (see Symmetric islands ). UV \u203a Mirrored Halves \u203a Mirror UVs (X) does that for UVs you already have: every face on the \u2212X side takes the mirrored UVs of its partner face on the +X side, so the \u2212X half stacks exactly on the +X half. Seams are added where a mirrored face meets one that was left alone. Faces without a mirror partner (the mesh isn't symmetric there) keep their UVs. Follow it with Pack if you want the freed space used. Claude uses uv_mirror , which can also mirror across Y or Z. The UV menu. Items that need a selection are grayed out without one. The UV menu is the same in three places: UV in the menu bar, the UV editor's More button and its right-click menu (which adds Move, Rotate and Scale at the top). The names and places match, so what you learn in one works in the others (since 1.2.7; they had grown apart). With faces selected, the commands work on their islands, and their names say (selection) ; with nothing selected, they work on every island. At the top is what most models need: Unwrap (along the seams, Smart UV Project, box, planar, cylinder, s"},{"p":"uv.html","a":"other-half","page":"UV editing","t":"The other half","x":"Mirrored Halves \u203a Select the Other Half adds the mirror image of the selection across the model's X symmetry, wherever it is in the layout: a face unwrapped in two halves side by side, or halves stacked on each other. Select one half of the face, and the other half joins (whole islands, when you selected whole islands). Then Mirror the Selection's UVs (X) stacks the \u2212X half on the +X half, for those faces only, so paint on one side shows on both. Claude uses select_faces with mode: \"mirror\" ."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"the-uv-editor","page":"UV editing","t":"The UV editor","x":"At the top are Edit UVs and Paint (texture-space painting), the texture shown underneath (any channel, a baked map, a layer, or a checker), and the selection mode."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"uv-context-menu","page":"UV editing","t":"The right-click menu","x":"Right-click in the UV editor for every UV operation in one place. If what is under the cursor isn't selected yet, it is selected first, so right-click an island and pick what to do with it: The right-click menu: the UV menu, with Move, Rotate and Scale at the top (see the UV menu ). The More button above the editor has the same operations, plus the display options. Hovering over an island shows its number, face count, and whether it is pinned or has a mirrored half; warnings about overlapping or drifted islands have a button that fixes them."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"selecting","page":"UV editing","t":"Selecting","x":"Mode Click selects Faces One face Islands The whole island under the cursor Vertices One UV vertex: all face corners of a mesh vertex that sit at the same UV position Shift +click adds to the selection; Shift +click on something already selected takes it off again, as in Blender. Ctrl +click removes from it. Dragging on empty space draws a box selection, and A selects or deselects everything. The Select tool ( S ) in the viewport selects faces and islands directly on the model, which is often easier. Vertices mode with a box selection. Selected UV vertices are orange squares."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"moving-rotating-scaling","page":"UV editing","t":"Moving, rotating, scaling","x":"Drag a selection to move it. It only starts to move once the mouse has traveled a few pixels, so clicking on a selected island (to select just that one) never nudges it. Dragging an unselected island selects and moves it in one go. Pinned islands don't move. G / R / S start a move, rotate or scale that follows the mouse. Click or press Enter to confirm, and right-click or press Esc to cancel. Hold Ctrl to snap (1/64 steps, 15\u00b0, 0.1\u00d7). More menu: rotate by 90\u00b0, flip, fit the selection to 0\u20131, Weld / stitch (moves the selected corners of each mesh vertex to their average; select both sides of a seam to stitch it closed) and Align vertically or horizontally."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"unwrap-and-pack","page":"UV editing","t":"Unwrap and pack","x":"Command What it does LSCM (uses seams) Flattens the surface, cutting along the seams you marked. Gives the least distortion. Best for organic shapes. Smart Project Splits the mesh by face angle (66\u00b0) and projects each part flat. Quick, and good for hard-surface models. Box Project Projects from the six axis directions. Good for boxy shapes and buildings. Planar (from 3D view) Projects straight from the current view direction. Cylinder For arms, legs, fingers, horns, pipes and barrels: cuts the part open along one line and flattens it into a single strip. Its ends become islands of their own. The cut follows the tube's own center, so bent and tapered parts come out even. Select one part first. Sphere Longitude and latitude, like a globe: for eyes, heads seen as balls, and domes. Relax Removes stretch from the selected islands. Each one is solved again and put back where it was, at the same size and angle, so the rest of the layout doesn't move. Pinned islands are left alone. Pack Arranges islands to fill the 0\u20131 square and evens out texel density so no part gets fewer pixels than another. Islands are packed by their outlines , so small islands fill the gaps and hollows of big ones ("},{"p":"uv.html","a":"packing","page":"UV editing","t":"Packing by shape","x":"Patina packs each island by its real outline. Each one, largest first, goes to the lowest place where it fits, turned to whichever of four angles hugs its neighbors best. The islands are then scaled up as far as they all still fit. A curved or L-shaped island no longer takes the space of its whole bounding box. On a typical Smart Project layout the islands get around 60% of the texture instead of under 40%, so the same texture resolution gives finer detail. For layouts of plain rectangles, boxes pack just as tightly and much faster, so Patina tries both and keeps the better one. Pinned islands stay where they are, and the others are packed around them. Claude and scripts can ask for uv_pack with \"quality\": \"fast\" to get bounding-box packing. The same islands packed by bounding boxes (left) and by their outlines (right)."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"seams","page":"UV editing","t":"Seams","x":"Seams are the cuts that LSCM unwraps along. The Seams tool ( M ) is a seam brush: Drag across the model. The seam follows the mesh's edges from vertex to vertex under the cursor, taking the shortest way between them, and is one undo step. Ctrl +drag clears seams the same way. Shift +click continues from where the last seam ended. While Shift is held, the path a click would add is shown faintly. Use it to run a seam around a sleeve or down the inside of a leg with a few clicks. A plain click toggles the one edge nearest the cursor. Vertices at the same position count as one, so seams run straight across meshes that were exported with split vertices. The Seams menu can also create seams from the current islands or by angle, and can clear them all. Seams travel with the mesh to and from Blender. Claude can lay seam paths with uv_seams in path mode. A seam dragged around the eye socket. The circle marks where it ended: Shift +click continues from there."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"painting-in-texture-space","page":"UV editing","t":"Painting in texture space","x":"Switch the UV editor to Paint to paint straight onto the texture with the current brush. Use it for things that belong to the layout rather than the surface: trim sheets, labels placed by UV, or touching up a single island. The brush size is in screen pixels of the UV view, so zoom in for detail. A gold stroke painted in texture space."},{"p":"uv.html","a":"gotchas","page":"UV editing","t":"Gotchas","x":"Paint follows UV edits only with \u201cKeep paint\u201d on UV \u203a Keep Paint on UV Edits is on by default. Each UV edit then moves your painted layers and masks to the new layout through 3D, so nothing appears to change on the model. Turn it off only if you want the texture to stay put while the UVs move, for example to line up a trim sheet. \u201cMy island won't move\u201d It is pinned: the UV editor draws a blue frame and a pin on it and says so when you try. Press Alt + P with it selected (or right-click \u203a Pins \u203a Unpin Selected Islands , or Unpin on the Select tool's bar in the 3D view). Islands made with the Island brush are pinned unless you untick Pin on its bar. Moving an island onto another one When an island lands on paint that belongs to another island, the overlapping part has two owners. Patina keeps each texel's existing paint and doesn't let the incoming paint overwrite it. Pack afterward, or move islands into free space. UVs outside 0\u20131 and UDIM tiles A model whose islands sit in several UV tiles opens with UDIM tiles : each tile gets a texture of its own, and the UV editor shows one tile at a time. When only a few islands sit in another tile (for example 1\u20132, as in the Khronos DamagedHel"},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"","page":"Retopology","t":"Retopology","x":"Retopology: clean quads over a high-poly model Patina makes a low-poly model of quads over a sculpt or a scan. The quads follow the model's shape on their own, and you steer them: zone lines where the edge flow should change (each becomes an edge loop), zones with a resolution of their own, flow strokes for more edge loops (or only which way the quads run), and a density brush . It is not a one-button remesher: the more you set, the closer the quads follow you. Two zone lines (yellow) on a subdivided Suzanne: one round the face, one round the snout. Each zone has its own color; the face is set to \u00d72 and the snout to \u00d73. The blue stroke across the forehead is a flow stroke. After Make quads , shown on their own ( Alone ): an edge loop runs along each line, and the quads get smaller from zone to zone."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-start","page":"Retopology","t":"Starting","x":"Open the high-poly model ( File \u203a Open , or Send to Patina from Blender). Pick a retopology tool in the tool strip's Retopo group: Lines , Zone , Flow or Density . They are always there; the Retopology window opens as a tab if it was closed ( Retopology \u203a Retopology Window opens it too). Set the resolution: Quads per m\u00b2 . Model units are meters; the line under it says how wide a quad is and about how many quads the whole model gets. 2,500 per m\u00b2 is 2 cm quads; 10,000 is 1 cm. The quads appear by themselves (the live preview , drawn faintly), a moment after you pick a retopology tool and after every change. With nothing drawn yet, they already follow the shape, so it is a good first look. Draw zone lines where the result should change, set resolutions, add flow strokes; the preview follows. Click Make quads to keep them: the preview is made the same way, so an up-to-date preview is kept at once (otherwise the quads are made in the background, a few seconds for a model of two million triangles). The quads show over the model as lines, or on their own with Alone ."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-show","page":"Retopology","t":"Seeing it with any tool: the Retopology preview button","x":"The Retopology preview button is on (copper outline), with the Paint tool chosen. Its arrow opens what it shows. The retopology tools show what they set while you use them. To keep the zones and the quads on the model while you do something else, turn on Retopology preview in the bar over the 3D view. It stays on until you turn it off, whatever tool is chosen and whichever panel is in front, and it is kept between sessions. The arrow beside it chooses what shows: Zones , Density or Neither , The quads , Alone , Red marks and Loops (the same settings as the window's Show ). It also has Make quads and opens the Retopology window. Make quads turns the preview on, so the quads you asked for show. It is also in View \u203a Retopology Preview , in the View menu of the 3D view's right-click menu and in the Retopology menu. You can give it a shortcut in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts ( Retopology preview on / off ). While it is on, the model's own wireframe and seams are hidden so they don't cover the quads. Before 0.37 the zones and the quads showed only while the Retopology window was in front. Switching off the Retopology preview button feature ( Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Features ) brings that back."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-preview","page":"Retopology","t":"The live preview","x":"While a retopology tool is in use, the Retopology preview is on, or the Retopology window is in front, Patina makes the quads again by itself about a third of a second after you stop changing something: a line, a stroke, a zone's resolution, the density, the quads per m\u00b2. A change while it is working stops it and starts it again. The preview is made just as Make quads makes the quads, so what you see is what you keep; the window says Preview above its numbers until you keep them. Untick Live preview to make the quads only when you ask. On a big model the first preview takes longest; after that, drawing lines and strokes reuses the work. Settings that ask for more than 150,000 quads (the window's count includes the zones and the density brush) get no live preview: Make quads still makes them, and the window warns that they may take minutes and many GB of memory. Patina runs only as many of QuadriFlow's tries at once as the memory allows, and a zone too big for the memory free is not tried: the window says so, and how much it would need. Nor is a zone asking for more than 250,000 quads, which QuadriFlow can't make in one piece: give it a lower resolution with the Zone tool."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-keep","page":"Retopology","t":"Keeping the quads away from a change","x":"Each change makes only the quads near it again: a few quads round the line or stroke you draw or take away, or round where you paint the density or change a zone's resolution (the whole inside of a small new loop too). Everything further off stays exactly as it was, so a part that came out clean stays clean while you work on another. A row of faces joins the new quads to the kept ones; it can leave a few more poles there than making everything again would. Remake all , beside Make quads , makes all the quads again at once: the cleanest quads for everything as it is now, though any part may come out different. Use it when you have finished drawing, or when a part came out badly. Untick Keep quads away from changes (under the zone settings) to make all the quads again after every change, as Patina did before 0.23. The setting is kept with the model. The window says how many of the vertices have four edges (the rest are poles: three, five or more edges). Without lines, most models come out between 95 and 99%. Each line, and each change of resolution, adds a few poles, because an edge loop has to start or end somewhere."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-lines","page":"Retopology","t":"Zone lines","x":"The Lines tool draws on the model's surface, wherever the pen goes, like a grease pencil: round the eyes and the mouth, along the cranium, round the base of the ears, round the wrists and the neck. Each line becomes an edge loop of the quads, running exactly along it. Each stroke is one undo step. Lines meet exactly. Start or end a stroke near another line and it starts or ends on that line: a white ring shows where before you press, and while you draw. In tight folds (the lips, between the fingers and toes, the crotch) that can pull a line onto its neighbor: untick Snap line ends in the window and the ends stay where you draw them. Closing. End a stroke where it began (a white ring shows at its start) and it closes into a loop. Overshooting is fine. Where a line runs on a little past the last line it crosses (less than a quad), the loose end is left out of the quads. Shift +click on an arm, a leg, the neck, the waist or a finger draws a line right round it there, across the way it runs: no need to turn the model round to draw the back. Hold Shift and rest the pointer to see the line first (a label by the pointer says which kind it is). Where there is no part to go round (a breast,"},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-zones","page":"Retopology","t":"Zones and their resolution","x":"The quads over the zones: twice as many quads per m\u00b2 in the face, three times in the snout. With the Zone tool, click a zone to choose it. The slider above the viewport (and in the window) sets its resolution : \u00d72 gives it twice as many quads per m\u00b2 as the rest, \u00d70.5 half. Each drag of the slider is one undo step; Reset resolutions sets every zone back to \u00d71 and clears the density brush (one undo step too). A line drawn inside a zone splits it into zones that keep its resolution: give the head \u00d78 and then draw the eyes, and the eyes are \u00d78 too. Zones of different resolution are made apart, each at its own size, with its lines as edge loops. Where they meet, the coarser side's row of quads along the line is replaced by a row that steps from one count to the other in three-to-one steps (three edges on one side, one on the other: four quads and two vertices with three edges). When the counts differ by an odd number, one triangle makes up the difference. The resolution goes from \u00d70.25 to \u00d732: fingers, for instance, need about six quads round them, which on a body at 4,000 quads per m\u00b2 means a hand zone at about \u00d716. A big step between neighboring zones (more than about \u00d75) is made in t"},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-rings","page":"Retopology","t":"Rings between two lines","x":"Lines a modeler might draw on Suzanne: round each eyeball and round each eye socket, round the tip of the nose and the nose, round the lips and the mouth. Between two lines the quads run in rings that follow both; the eyeballs, the tip of the nose and the inside of the lips are closed with grids. A zone between two lines (round an eye, round a mouth, along an arm between the wrist and the elbow) is filled with rings of quads that follow both lines, about a quad apart: as many rows as the narrow side holds. When one line is longer than the other, the rings keep one count while their quads stay between about half a quad and one and a half quads wide, as hand-made eye loops do; past that the count changes in three-to-one steps spread evenly between them. The counts are chosen for a whole chain of rings at once (the socket, the eye, the brow), so the steps go where the last ring meets the zone beyond it. A zone inside such a ring (the tip of a nose, the inside of the lips, an eyeball) is closed with a grid , as a modeler's grid fill, a ring in from its line: every vertex on the line and inside has four edges but the grid's four corners, the poles a disc needs, which sit just inside. Ri"},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-character","page":"Retopology","t":"Lines for a character","x":"A few lines in the right places do most of the work. These are the ones that pay on a head and a body: Eyes. One line along the edge of the eye opening (or round the eyeball) and one round the socket. The eye gets rings that keep their count, and the opening (or the eyeball) is closed with a grid, its poles inside. The band out to the socket line is made with the face round it when it is much longer outside than in, so the steps from one count to the other go where the face can take them; a small opening inside it then goes with the face too. Mouth. One line round the lips, one round the mouth: rings round the mouth, a grid inside the lips. Nose. One round the tip; one round the whole nose if you want rings there too. Arms and legs. A line round the wrist ( Zones for thin parts draws it) and one round the top of the arm (or the thigh; Shift +click there with Lines ): the limb between is a band and gets clean rings, with no twist. Without the top line a long limb's rows can wind round it like a thread on a screw (no pole shows it: tick Loops and follow a loop round the arm to check). The head. A line round the neck ( Shift +click it) makes the head a zone of its own, so it can have "},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-loops","page":"Retopology","t":"Seeing the flow: loops and poles","x":"With Loops on: the poles as small crosses (blue: three edges, orange: five), and the edge loop under the pointer lit up in green, here one running over the crown and down the face. Tick Loops (next to Alone ) to see how the quads flow. Each pole (a vertex with three, five or more edges) shows as a small cross: blue with three edges, orange with five, red with more. Point at the quads and the edge loop under the pointer lights up all along, to where it ends at a pole or comes back round; the window says how many edges it has. Poles gathered along a line you drew show where the flow has to change there; moving the line, or giving the zones beside it closer resolutions, moves them."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-flow","page":"Retopology","t":"Flow strokes","x":"The Flow tool draws strokes on the model, the same way, that set which way the quads run where they are drawn: across the forehead, down the nose, along a muscle. A stroke that ends where it started closes into a loop. Ctrl +click a stroke to take it away. Each stroke is one undo step. Each stroke becomes an edge loop that runs exactly where you drew it, as a zone line does, but it doesn't make a zone of its own: the resolution is the same either side of it. Rings drawn round a spot (a nipple, an eye) become loops round it. This is Strokes make edge loops , on by default (it shows with the Flow tool, in the window and above the viewport; the setting is kept with the mesh). Untick it for strokes that only turn the quads: The quads right beside a stroke run along it (and across it), and the turn carries on through the zone until something else holds the quads: a line, another stroke, the zone's edge. One stroke on its own can turn a whole torso. The quads follow its direction, not its exact path, and they keep their size. So a circle of flow round a small spot comes out as a spiral, with poles where the rings need more edges further out. In a zone made as rings between two lines, or "},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-density","page":"Retopology","t":"The density brush","x":"The Density brush paints a multiplier on the quads per m\u00b2 for fine tuning inside a zone: \u00d72 for more quads there, \u00d70.5 for fewer (the slider above the viewport, or in the window). It paints the surface under it, soft at its edge, and only the part it touches (not through to another part behind); the mirror buttons mirror it. Ctrl paints it back to \u00d71. The change in the quads is gentle: they grow or shrink gradually around what you paint, without edge loops of their own. For a clear step, use a zone."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-symmetry","page":"Retopology","t":"Symmetry","x":"With Mirror X , Y or Z on (in the window, or the mirror buttons), zone lines, flow strokes, the density brush and zone resolutions are mirrored across the model's middle (the middle of its bounding box), and so are the quads: they are made on one side and mirrored onto the other, so both sides match vertex for vertex. The halves meet along an edge loop down the middle. The side kept is the one with more of your lines on it (the bigger side when there are none), so lines drawn on one side only, with Mirror off, still count. The middle's loop. Where the model crosses the middle, an edge loop runs along it. A line you drew within half a quad of the middle is moved onto that loop there, so the two meet rather than leave a strip too narrow for a row of quads; so is the rest of the line that stays within a quad of the middle around that place. A line that runs close to the middle for a while (a breast's outline by the cleavage) then shares the middle's loop and leaves it across it, rather than at a slant that needs a pole where it and its mirror image meet. Zones that touch the middle are made like any other zone, not as rings or grids. A model that isn't the same on both sides. Each sep"},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-big","page":"Retopology","t":"Big models","x":"A model with more than about 200,000 triangles (or far more detail than the quads need) is first made lighter: its own surface is simplified to triangles about a third of the quads' size (finer in zones of high resolution), keeping its shape, its separate fingers and its gaps; the quads are made on that and put back on the model's own surface. Loose specks smaller than about four quads are left out, and the window says how many triangles that was. A coarse model is the other way round: its triangles are split where the quads need them smaller. Parts thinner than about a quad (fingers, strands of hair) can't be covered by quads that big, so they are left out: the window says how much of the surface that is, and red crosses mark where. Give those parts a zone with a higher resolution (fingers need about six quads round them), or let Patina do it: Red crosses also mark places where zones meet in a tight spot (patched there), and, with Mirror on, places where the two sides of the model aren't the same. The notes under the last build in the window say which. The marks change nothing in the quads: untick Red marks (next to Alone ) to hide them."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-thin","page":"Retopology","t":"Zones for thin parts","x":"At 4,000 quads per m\u00b2, the fingers are too thin for the quads: they are left out, and red crosses mark them. After Zones for thin parts : a line round the wrist, and the hand at \u00d716, so every finger gets its quads. Zones for thin parts (in the window, under the zone tools) finds the parts too thin for the quads as they are now: where fewer than about four quads would go round. For each one that is a tube (a finger, a hand, a toe) it draws a zone line round it where it meets the rest, a clean ring across the limb (round the wrist, say), and gives that zone a resolution for about six quads round it: \u00d72, \u00d74, \u00d78 or \u00d716. It is one undo step, and the lines and resolutions are yours to change like any others. It leaves some parts as they are, and the status bar says how many: thin sheets such as locks of hair (wider than they are thick, so their quads can be as big as elsewhere), parts that would need more than \u00d716 (thin strands: give them a zone by hand if you want them), and parts with no clean ring round them (between toes, say)."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-use","page":"Retopology","t":"Using the quads","x":"Export .obj... writes the quads to a file. The quads leave the retopology facing out, each turned like its neighbors, wherever they go. Send to Blender (with a linked object) adds the quads as a new object, name_retopo , in the same place as the linked one. Sending again updates it. This needs the 0.7 add-on. Use the quads as the model... saves them next to the high-poly ( name_retopo.obj ), opens them as the model you paint, with new UVs, and bakes the high-poly onto them (normals and ambient occlusion, as Bake \u203a From a High-poly Mesh ). With Bring the layers along (on by default), every layer comes onto the quads: a bake-down , into a new project, so the high-poly's project stays as it was saved. Off, the layers start afresh. The lines, zones, resolutions, strokes and painted density are kept between sessions: open the same high-poly again and they are back. The Retopology window: the resolution, the tools, the chosen zone's resolution, symmetry, and the quads (with the live preview on) with their share of four-edged vertices."},{"p":"retopology.html","a":"retopo-commands","page":"Retopology","t":"From scripts","x":"Everything is a command (see Commands ): retopo_zone_line (lines from 3D points), retopo_zones and retopo_zone (list the zones, set a resolution), retopo_stroke (flow strokes), retopo_density , retopo_build , retopo_info , retopo_view (with loops and loop_at , and preview for the Retopology preview button), retopo_export , retopo_send_to_blender , retopo_use_as_model , retopo_symmetry (the deadzone), retopo_rings (rings between two lines, on or off), retopo_fewer_poles (on or off), retopo_flow_loops (strokes make edge loops), retopo_snap (snapping line ends), retopo_confirm_lines , retopo_view lines_behind , retopo_thin_zones (zones for thin parts) and retopo_clear ; retopo_zone_line and retopo_stroke take mirror (\"x\", \"xy\"...). The 0.13 commands for painted patches ( retopo_patch , retopo_paint , retopo_auto_patches , retopo_fill_gaps ) still work: painted patches are zones too, and retopo_build with method: \"patches\" fills each patch on its own as 0.13 did."},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Checking, mending, reducing and exporting models","x":"Checking, mending, reducing and exporting models Before a model goes into a game engine, a printer or a render, it is worth knowing what it is made of and what is wrong with it. The Mesh Check tells you both, shows the problems on the model, and mends the ones that can be mended without touching your paint. It is the first of Patina's tools for finishing models made in Blender, scanned or generated: post-process your models the way you would a photo."},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"the-mesh-check-window","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"The Mesh Check window","x":"Open it with Mesh \u203a Check Mesh\u2026 . The top half lists the numbers: vertices, faces (triangles, quads and n-gons), the triangles an engine draws, separate parts, material slots, UV islands, the size in meters along Blender's X, Y and Z, the surface area and volume, whether the model is closed, and how much of the UV square it uses. A cube with a missing face and a top face that isn't joined to the rest. Below are the problems, worst first. Each says how many there are and what it means. Select selects its faces (for example to make an island of them or to look at them in the UV editor), Look turns the view to the first one, and Fix mends it. Tick Show the problems on the model to color the faces: red for a problem, orange for a warning, blue for a note. The back of any face shows magenta, which makes flipped faces easy to spot. Problem What it is Fix Non-manifold edges Edges shared by three faces or more. Engines, printers and bakers can't tell inside from outside there. Mend it in Blender Flipped faces Faces turned the other way from their neighbors: dark or see-through in engines, broken bakes and smoothing. Turns them like their neighbors Inside-out parts Closed parts whose faces "},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"fixing","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Fixing","x":"Fix everything that can be fixed (also in the Mesh menu) does all the fixes at once, in the right order: it joins open seams before it closes holes, so a seam isn't capped twice. Fix settings sets the weld distance (automatic: a hundred-thousandth of the model's size) and the biggest hole to close. A fix makes a new mesh: UVs, material slots, seams and vertex groups stay with their faces, and Patina moves the layers' paint over, as it does when you update a mesh from Blender. A new face closing a hole takes its border's UVs, so it shows the texture next to it until you unwrap it. Undo after a fix A new mesh clears the undo history. Patina keeps a copy of the project from just before the fix: Back to before the fix (in the window and the Mesh menu) returns to it, layers and paint included. Models linked to Blender After a fix the mesh no longer matches the Blender object it came from, so Patina's textures fit the mended mesh, not the object. Send the mended model back with Send the mended model to Blender (see below ): it becomes a new object with the textures, and Patina links to it."},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"reduce","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Fewer polygons","x":"Mesh \u203a Reduce Polygons\u2026 takes a model down to a share of its triangles, for games, the web and AR. Choose how much to keep; the window says how many triangles that leaves and how far the shape moves (as a share of the model's size) before you click Reduce . The UV layout stays: UV seams and the borders between material slots don't move, and every corner that is left keeps its UV, so the textures fit as they are and nothing is repainted. Keep open borders holds the edges of clothes, planes and cut-offs in place. The result is triangles. Suzanne with tiles at all of its 3936 triangles, at 25% and at 6%. The tiles stay where they were. As with a fix, reducing clears the undo history, and Back to before the reduction returns to the model as it was. A reduced model linked to Blender goes back with Send Model to Blender ."},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"lods","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Levels of detail","x":"In File \u203a Export Model\u2026 , Levels of detail adds lighter copies of the model, each with half the triangles of the one before. They share the model's textures. In glTF they are meshes named name_LOD0 , name_LOD1 \u2026 (the full model carries the MSFT_lod extension, which some engines read); with OBJ they are name_LOD1.obj \u2026 beside the model. Unity, Unreal and Godot all recognize the _LOD names."},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"export-model","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Exporting the model","x":"File \u203a Export Model\u2026 writes the model with its textures, ready for a game engine, a web viewer or another 3D program: GLB : one file with the textures inside. Three.js, Babylon.js, Godot, Unity (with glTFast), Unreal (with its glTF importer), Sketchfab and Blender read it. glTF : the same as a .gltf file with a .bin and PNG files beside it. OBJ : .obj and .mtl with PNG maps (base color, roughness, metallic, normal, glow, and opacity when used). Faces keep their quads and n-gons. STL : the shape alone, for 3D printing, in millimeters with Z up (one meter in Blender is 1000 mm). Mend the model first so it is closed; the 3D print recipe does that, stands it on the ground and exports it. PLY : the model with its base color as vertex colors (one color per corner, read from the textures), with UVs and normals, Z up in meters as Blender reads it. For color 3D printing and programs that use vertex colors. Faces keep their quads and n-gons. Each texture set or UDIM tile becomes a material of its own (glTF has no UDIM tiles, so each tile's UVs are moved back into the square). The glTF material has the base color (with opacity in its alpha: Mask when the opacity is all solid or cut out, Blend"},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"collision","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Collision shapes","x":"A game engine does not collide against the model itself, which is far too detailed: it uses a few simple, convex shapes around it. Mesh \u203a Collision\u2026 makes them and shows them in green over the model; File \u203a Export Model writes them after the model, named the way the engine looks for them. A ring split into twelve convex pieces. One hull would fill its hole. Shapes For Convex pieces Anything that bends in: chairs, arches, rings, characters. The model is split where one hull would hold too much empty space. The closest fit. A hull per separate part Models made of separate parts: kits, furniture, a car and its wheels. One convex hull Rocks, bottles, round props. A box per separate part , One box Crates, books, walls. The cheapest for the engine. A box is upright unless a turned one is clearly smaller. Most shapes limits how many (more fit closer but cost the engine more: 4 to 16 is usual for a prop). Most corners per hull keeps each hull simple (engines like 32 or fewer). For convex pieces, Empty space allowed sets when a piece is split again (lower: more, closer pieces), and Detail how finely the model is looked at. After Make , the window says how many shapes there are and how much "},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"lightmap","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Lightmap UVs","x":"Game engines bake their lighting (shadows, light bouncing between surfaces) into a lightmap , a texture laid over the model through a second UV map. That map has stricter rules than the one the paint uses: no face may share space with another (mirrored and stacked islands, fine for painting, are not allowed), the detail should be the same everywhere, and the islands need a gap between them so light does not bleed across. Mesh \u203a Lightmap UVs\u2026 makes it. New islands made for a lightmap of 512 pixels, four pixels apart. Lightmap size : the size the engine will bake at (Unity and Unreal often use 64 to 1024 per object). The gap is measured in its pixels. Gap : pixels between islands. 2 to 4 is usual; a small lightmap needs the larger gaps. Islands : The model's own UV islands , repacked, keeps the shapes the paint uses (best for models with clean UVs; islands that fold over themselves are unwrapped again). New ones made for lightmaps asks xatlas, the unwrapper Godot uses, for new islands: for scans and generated models whose UVs are a mess. It takes a while on dense models (half a minute for 100,000 faces): reduce them first. The paint's UVs do not change. The lightmap UVs are saved wit"},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"to-blender","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"Sending the model to Blender","x":"Blender \u203a Send Model to Blender as a New Object gives Blender the model as it is in Patina, for example after a fix: a new object, name_patina , next to the linked one in its place and collections (or at the world origin for a model opened from a file), with its UVs, material slots, seams and vertex groups, and Patina's material on it. Patina links to the new object from then on, so painting and Send Textures to Blender go there. Replace the Linked Object's Mesh gives the linked object Patina's mesh instead (its modifiers stay; Undo in Blender goes back). After a fix on a linked model, the Mesh Check window has a Send the mended model to Blender button. Both need Blender's live link and the 0.9 add-on."},{"p":"mesh.html","a":"from-claude-and-scripts","page":"Mesh check & export","t":"From Claude and scripts","x":"mesh_check returns the numbers and problems (with places in Blender's axes, and each problem's faces on request), mesh_fix mends ( all , or the fixes one by one, with weld_distance , small_part_size and fill_hole_edges ), and mesh_fix_revert goes back. The mesh_check view mode shows the heat map. mesh_reduce reduces (with preview to only count), export_model writes the model with its textures ( lods for levels of detail), and send_model_to_blender sends it to Blender. See Commands ."},{"p":"batch.html","a":"","page":"Recipes & batches","t":"Recipes and batches","x":"Recipes and batches A recipe is a list of steps that finishes a model: mend it, reduce it, texture it, export it. Run it on the model you have open, or on a whole folder of models at once, as a photo editor applies a preset to a whole shoot. A batch runs in a hidden Patina, so you can keep working, and it never changes the files themselves: the results go to an output folder. Two models through the Web and AR recipe."},{"p":"batch.html","a":"the-built-in-recipes","page":"Recipes & batches","t":"The built-in recipes","x":"Recipe Steps Game asset Mend, bake mesh maps (AO and curvature), export a GLB with three levels of detail and 2K textures Web and AR Mend, reduce to a quarter of the triangles, export a light GLB with 1K textures Clean up Mend everything the Mesh Check can, export a GLB 3D print Mend (closing holes up to 512 edges, faces out), stand it on the ground, export an STL in millimeters Auto texture Bake mesh maps, a library material per material slot from the slots' names, export a GLB Worn painted metal Bake mesh maps, the Worn painted metal smart material, export a GLB Centimeters to meters Scale a model made in centimeters to meters, origin at its base, export a GLB"},{"p":"batch.html","a":"running-a-batch","page":"Recipes & batches","t":"Running a batch","x":"Open File \u203a Batch Process\u2026 , pick a recipe, add files or folders (tick Its subfolders too to go deeper) and choose the output folder (by default a patina_out folder beside the first file). Run the batch opens each model in a hidden Patina, runs the steps and moves on. The window shows how far it has got and, for each file, whether it worked; hover a failed one to read why. A file that fails is left, and the rest carry on. Stop after this file ends the batch early. Apply to the open model runs the same steps on the model you have open instead (its exports go beside its file)."},{"p":"batch.html","a":"your-own-recipes","page":"Recipes & batches","t":"Your own recipes","x":"Edit / copy shows a recipe's steps as text: each is a Patina command and its settings, the same ones Claude and scripts use (see Commands ). Change them and Save as my recipe . In the steps, ${name} is the model's name, ${file} its file, ${dir} its folder and ${out} the output folder, so \"path\": \"${out}/${name}.glb\" exports each model under its own name. Your recipes are kept in your Patina folder and can be deleted; a copy of a built-in recipe under its id replaces it for you. [ {\"cmd\": \"mesh_fix\", \"args\": {\"all\": true}}, {\"cmd\": \"mesh_transform\", \"args\": {\"from_units\": \"cm\", \"origin\": \"base\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"mesh_reduce\", \"args\": {\"ratio\": 0.5}}, {\"cmd\": \"apply_smart_material\", \"args\": {\"id\": \"patina_smart_rusted_iron\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"export_model\", \"args\": {\"path\": \"${out}/${name}.glb\", \"texture_size\": 2048, \"lods\": [0.5]}} ]"},{"p":"batch.html","a":"transform","page":"Recipes & batches","t":"Transform","x":"Mesh \u203a Transform\u2026 (and the mesh_transform step) fixes models made in the wrong units or the wrong way round: the units it was made in (centimeters, millimeters, inches, feet, to Blender's meters), a scale, a turn around Blender's X, Y and Z, the origin (the center of its box, or the center of its bottom) and Stand it on the ground . The faces and UVs stay, so painted layers stay too; fill layers projected in 3D follow the new size. As with a fix, Back to before returns to the model as it was."},{"p":"batch.html","a":"from-claude-and-scripts","page":"Recipes & batches","t":"From Claude and scripts","x":"recipe_list , recipe_save , recipe_delete and recipe_apply work with recipes; batch_run starts a batch (a recipe or steps, files and folders, an output folder), batch_status follows it and batch_cancel stops it. Outside Patina, patina run job.json runs a job with \"files\" and \"per_file\" steps the same way; --progress writes a line per finished file (see Automation )."},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Texture set, baking and export","x":"Texture set, baking and export The texture set holds the output maps of a project. This page covers their resolution, the mesh maps that smart masks use, baking a high-poly sculpt, exporting to other tools, and saving projects."},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"resolution","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Resolution","x":"Texture Set \u203a Resolution can be 512 to 8192 (2048 by default), per texture set or UDIM tile when the model has several (see Texture sets and UDIM tiles ). Changing it resamples every layer, so you can paint at 2K and export at 4K. Higher resolutions use a lot more memory: a fully painted paint layer takes about 85 MB of GPU memory at 2K, 330 MB at 4K and 1.3 GB at 8K. Fill and adjustment layers are much cheaper. The Base material (color, roughness, metallic) is what shows where no layer covers the model. Height\u2192normal sets how strongly the height channel becomes normal-map detail. Dilate seams pads the space around UV islands, so mipmaps and texture filtering in other programs don't pick up background color at the seams. Leave it on."},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"mesh-maps-baking","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Mesh maps (baking)","x":"Mesh maps are worked out once from the model and stored in the project. Smart masks are built from them, and some are exported for game engines. Use the buttons in the Texture Set panel, or the Bake menu ( Bake \u203a All mesh maps bakes everything). Smart masks bake what they need the first time you use them, so you rarely have to think about it, but after large mesh or UV changes, bake again so the masks follow. The four mesh maps, each shown with its view mode: ambient occlusion, curvature, thickness (dark is thin) and bent normals. Map What it holds Used by Ambient occlusion How much surrounding light reaches each point: dark in corners and crevices. AO samples trades quality for time: 64 is fast, 256 is smoother on complex models. Baked on the GPU. Occlusion and Cavity smart masks; exported as AO. Bent normals The average direction light arrives from at each point (object space), baked together with the AO. Exported for engines that use them for specular occlusion and better ambient lighting. Curvature Gray on flat areas, brighter on convex edges, darker in creases. Edges and Cavity smart masks. Thickness How thick the model is behind each point: white is thick, dark is thin. Rays "},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"baked-layers","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Fixing a bake: baked map layers","x":"A bake is not always right: too few AO samples leave it blotchy, a crease comes out too dark, a part that touches another picks up shadow it should not have. Every baked map has a layer of its own for that: ambient occlusion, curvature and thickness each get one in the Baked maps section under the layer stack when they are baked (and when you open a project from before 0.41). What the layer makes is the map the rest of Patina reads: smart masks, the 3D view and exported maps all use the fixed one. The layer adds nothing to the color, roughness or other channels. The Baked maps section, under the stack. fix : fixes are painted on it; fx : it has adjustments. The AO layer's properties: strength, the value the brush paints, the adjustments, Bake again. Select the layer and the 3D view shows its map (the view comes back when you select another layer). Then: Paint on the model to fix the map. The brush paints the Paint value in the layer's properties (for the AO, 1 is no shadow and 0 is fully shadowed; the curvature's flat is 0.5). The picker takes the value under the cursor. Erase brings the bake back under the brush. Reset the Fixes takes all of them away. Blur smooths the map, Smudge"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"high-poly","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Baking from a high-poly mesh","x":"A game model is usually a light, low-poly mesh with the detail of a heavy sculpt baked into its textures. Bake \u203a From a High-poly Mesh\u2026 (or Bake from high-poly\u2026 in the Texture Set panel) does that: choose the sculpt ( .glb , .gltf , .fbx , .obj , .stl or .3mf ) and press Bake . A 48 \u00d7 24 sphere with the dimples of a 160,000-triangle sculpt baked in. Option What it does Normal map The sculpt's surface direction for every texel. It is combined with the normals of every layer (so fill layers don't hide it), shown in the viewport and exported with the textures. Ambient occlusion AO of the sculpt's surface instead of the low-poly one. Smart masks use it too. Curvature (automatic) When the normal map is baked, the curvature is baked again with the normal map's detail added, so edge and cavity masks follow the sculpt's detail. Material slots The material slots of the sculpt, for the Material slot mask and the fill tool. Points per texel Each texel is measured at this many points spread over it (1, 4, 9 or 16), and what they find is averaged. More points give smoother edges to the detail, without stair steps. 4 is the default. Distance How far along the low-poly's normals, both ways, Patin"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"bake-down","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Baking down to a low-poly model","x":"Painting the sculpt itself is often easier: the detail is all there, and smart masks find every crease. But a game needs the light model. Bake \u203a Bake Down to a Low-Poly Model\u2026 moves every layer of the model you have open, the painted high-poly, onto its low-poly version, which then becomes the model. The low-poly can be a file (from Blender's retopology, or any other tool) or the quads of Patina's own retopology . The layer stack stays as it was: you go on painting, in layers, on the light model. After a bake-down, the compare view: the painted high-poly as it was (its triangles) left of the divider, and its retopology quads with every layer baked down onto them right of it. Drag the divider across the model. Paint and masks , and the fixes painted over a baked map, are measured texel by texel. Each texel of the low-poly is sampled at several points spread over it ( Points per texel : 9 by default). Each point looks along the low-poly's normal, both ways, for the high-poly's surface, as the high-poly bake does. What the points find there is averaged, weighted by how much paint covers it, so paint next to bare surface keeps its color. Painted normals (a normal stamp, a material with"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"material-slots","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Material slots","x":"Models from Blender often already have their parts separated by material slots (rubber, metal, glass\u2026). Patina keeps them: the Material Slots view mode shows them in colors, the Fill tool's Material slot mode fills every face of the clicked slot, UV \u203a Select \u203a Whole Material Slot extends a face selection to its slots, and the Material slot smart mask turns a fill layer on for one slot only. When you bake from a high-poly mesh with Material slots on, the sculpt's slots are used."},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"texture-sets","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Texture sets and UDIM tiles","x":"Characters and props often give each part its own textures: the body, the clothes and the hair each laid out over the whole 0\u20131 square, or a hero asset spread over several UDIM tiles (1001, 1002, 1011\u2026). Texture Set \u203a Texture sets chooses how Patina stores the paint: Setting What gets its own textures One texture Nothing: every face shares one set of maps (as before 0.9). Per material slot Each material slot, with its own 0\u20131 UV space. Slots may overlap in UV space. UDIM tiles Each UV tile the islands sit in: 1001 is 0\u20131, 1002 the square to its right, 1011 the one above. Per slot and UDIM Each UDIM tile of each material slot. Two material slots laid out over the same UV square. With a texture set each, the skin and the denim never share texels. Patina picks the layout when it opens a model: per material slot when two slots cover the same part of the UV square, and UDIM tiles when the islands sit in several unit tiles. Islands in other tiles are no longer moved into 0\u20131 for UDIM models. You can switch at any time. The paint stays where it is on the model, but the undo history is cleared. When the slots overlap and you choose One texture , they share texels again, so their paint mixe"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"new-set","page":"Texture set & export","t":"A texture set of its own: the head and the body","x":"To give part of the model its own texture, for example one for the head and one for the body, select its faces and choose UV \u203a Texture Sets \u203a New Texture Set from Selection . The same is in the UV editor's right-click menu, at the bottom of its list of sets, and under Texture Set \u203a New set from selection . Select the faces. Select the head in the window picks everything above the neck in one click; otherwise use the Select tool in the viewport or click islands in the UV editor. Give the set a name, such as Head . The name of an existing set moves the faces into that set instead. Leave Pack each set to fill its space on: the new set's islands grow to fill its whole square (pinned islands move too and stay pinned), and the set the faces came from is packed again, unless it has pinned islands. The faces go into a new material slot, and One texture becomes Per material slot . The paint moves with the faces, and the undo history is cleared. With the Blender link, the next Send Textures to Blender adds the slot to the object in Blender and assigns the faces to it (add-on 0.6.1 or later), so the head gets its own material there too. With UDIM tiles , the faces' islands move to the next fr"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"export","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Exporting textures","x":"Open the dialog with File \u203a Export Textures\u2026 ( Ctrl + E ). Pick a folder, a preset, a size and how the files are named. Under the settings the dialog lists every file the export will write, as they are now: each file's name, what its channels hold ( R occlusion, G roughness, B metallic ), its bits (8 or 16), and whether it is a color map ( sRGB ) or a data map ( Non-Color , as Blender calls it; an engine's \"sRGB\" off). With texture sets or UDIM tiles it lists one set's files and says how many sets there are. Files that are already there are replaced. Preset Files ( Name_\u2026png ) Notes Blender (Principled BSDF) basecolor, roughness, metallic, normal, height (16-bit), emission, ao Normal is OpenGL style. Connect it through a Normal Map node set to Tangent Space. glTF (metallicRoughness) baseColor, metallicRoughness (G = roughness, B = metallic), normal, emissive, occlusion Ready for glTF / three.js / Babylon.js. Unreal (ORM, DirectX normal) BaseColor, OcclusionRoughnessMetallic, Normal (green flipped), Height, Emissive Import the ORM texture with sRGB off. Unity (MaskMap) BaseMap, MaskMap (R metallic, G AO, B detail mask = 0, A smoothness), Normal, Height, Emission For HDRP and URP Lit"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"layered-psd","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Layered PSD","x":"Set Format to Layered PSD to hand the layer stack to Photoshop, Krita, GIMP or Affinity Photo instead of flat maps. Patina writes one .psd file per map: base color, roughness, metallic, height (16-bit), normal and emission. In each file: every Patina layer is a Photoshop layer with the same name, opacity, blend mode and visibility; its mask (painted, smart mask or effect stack, as Patina uses it) becomes a layer mask you can keep editing; folders become layer groups; a paint layer holds its paint, a fill layer its material over the whole canvas (the mask cuts it down, as in Patina), and an adjustment layer the result of the adjustment on the layers below, marked (adjustment, baked) ; the bottom layer, Base , holds the base material (and the high-poly normal map, if baked); the flattened image is Patina's own result, so programs that ignore layers still show the right texture. PSD files are written at the texture set's size. With texture sets or UDIM tiles , each set or tile gets its own PSD files, named like the PNG maps ( Hero_Body_basecolor.psd , Hero_basecolor.1002.psd ). The normal file uses the preset's convention (DirectX for Unreal). Blend modes Photoshop doesn't have are ma"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"file-names","page":"Texture set & export","t":"File names","x":"File names is a template. The default $mesh_$map gives Suzanne_basecolor.png . The dialog shows an example name as you type. Token Becomes $mesh The $mesh field, or the mesh name when that is empty $project The project file name without .patina ( untitled if not saved yet) $map The map name of the preset, for example basecolor , ORM \u2026 or mask $size The output size in pixels, for example 2048 $preset blender , gltf , unreal or unity $layer The exported layer's name, or all For example, T_$mesh_$map_$size gives T_Suzanne_BaseColor_2048.png , a common Unreal naming style. Characters that are not allowed in file names are replaced with _ . Templates cannot create folders."},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"exporting-one-layer-or-a-mask","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Exporting one layer or a mask","x":"The Export combo chooses what to write: All layers : the finished textures. Only the selected layer : the maps as they look with that layer or folder soloed, over the base material. Use it to hand a decal layer or a dirt pass to another tool. The selected layer's mask : the painted mask as a grayscale PNG ( $map is mask ), at the chosen size. Right-click a layer \u25b8 Export this layer\u2026 or Export its mask\u2026 opens the dialog set up for it. If an export fails (for example, the folder is read-only or the disk is full), Patina shows an error dialog, and nothing that was there before is half-overwritten. Each file is written to a temporary name first and then renamed. Export again ( File \u203a Export Again , Ctrl + Shift + E ) repeats the last export with the same folder, preset, size and names, without the dialog. The settings are stored in the project, so it works after reopening it too. When you work with the Blender bridge, Ctrl + B exports straight into the linked Blender material instead, as described in Blender bridge ."},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"projects","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Projects","x":"File \u203a Save ( Ctrl + S ) writes a single .patina file. It holds the mesh, UVs, seams, every layer and mask, the bakes and the settings, so you can move it to another computer and carry on. Projects open the same way on Linux and Windows. While the window is open, Patina autosaves every 5 minutes after you stop painting ( Preferences \u203a Saving changes how often, or turns it off). The autosave is written in the background, so painting carries on while it saves. It goes to the cache folder (see where files live ), and File \u203a Open Autosave recovers it. Autosaving never changes the path your project is saved to. Save Incremental ( File \u203a Save Incremental , Ctrl + Alt + S ) saves a new numbered version next to the current one: crate.patina \u2192 crate_v002.patina \u2192 crate_v003.patina , never overwriting an older version. Crash recovery: if Patina didn't close properly, the next start offers to restore the autosave. Several Patina windows can be open at once without setting this off. The mesh changed on disk: when the .glb or .obj you opened is saved again by another program (you edited it in Blender and exported over it), Patina offers to update the mesh . Your layers are kept: paint follows c"},{"p":"texture-set.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Texture set & export","t":"Gotchas","x":"Colors look different in another program Base color and emission are sRGB. Roughness, metallic, height, normal and AO are linear (non-color) data. If an engine imports them as sRGB, roughness looks too glossy and normals look wrong. Untick sRGB, or set the color space to Non-Color in Blender, for everything except base color and emission. Normal map looks inverted (lit from below) The green channel convention differs between programs. Patina writes OpenGL style (Blender, glTF, Unity), except for the Unreal preset, which writes DirectX style. If you import into another DirectX-style tool, flip the green channel there or use the Unreal preset. High-poly bake: flat patches or \u201cmany texels missed\u201d The two meshes are not in the same place, or the cage distance is too small for how far the sculpt sticks out. Check that both were exported with the same transform (apply scale in Blender), then raise Distance . Detail that appears on a part where it doesn't belong means the distance is too large: lower it. Height isn't displacement Height is exported as a map (16-bit for precision) and shown in Patina as bump detail through the normal map. It doesn't move the geometry in the viewport. For r"},{"p":"blender.html","a":"","page":"Blender bridge","t":"The Blender bridge","x":"The Blender bridge You model in Blender and paint in Patina. With the bridge, the textures appear on the Blender model by themselves, and UV changes made in Patina go back too. .b{fill:var(--panel);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2}.t{font:600 15px system-ui,sans-serif;fill:var(--fg)}.s{font:13px system-ui,sans-serif;fill:var(--dim)}.a{stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:2.5;fill:none;marker-end:url(#ar2)} Blender sidebar (N) \u203a Patina tab Send to Patina \u00b7 Pull Textures Update Mesh \u00b7 Live Link material \u201cPatina_<object>\u201d Patina paints, edits UVs and seams Ctrl+B or auto-send keeps layers on mesh updates mesh + UVs + seams (.ptmesh) textures (PNG) + edited UVs (.ptuv)"},{"p":"blender.html","a":"setting-up","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Setting up","x":"Install patina_bridge.zip in Blender (see Install ). Open the 3D view sidebar ( N ) and find the Patina tab. Click Launch Patina , or start Patina yourself. The add-on finds it on port 7860. If Blender can't find the program, set Patina Executable in the add-on preferences to the full path, for example /home/you/.local/bin/patina or C:\\Users\\you\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Patina\\patina.exe ."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"the-round-trip","page":"Blender bridge","t":"The round trip","x":"Send to Patina with a mesh selected. Patina opens it with the exact face-corner order, UVs and seams. If its materials show textures or colors, Patina starts from them, as the layer From the model (add-on 0.13). A color attribute (vertex paint, add-on 0.10) comes in too, where a slot has no image on Base Color. One that is a single flat color is skipped, unless a material shows it. If you painted the object before and saved the project, Patina opens that project instead (see After a restart ). Paint. Press Ctrl + B in Patina, use Blender \u203a Send Textures to Blender , or turn on Auto-Send After Changes to keep Blender up to date as you paint. Blender reloads the images Patina_<object>_<map> and builds or updates a material Patina_<object> . It contains a Principled BSDF with base color, roughness, metallic, normal, emission and AO, and optionally displacement. Base color and emission are sRGB; everything else is Non-Color. Keep modeling in Blender, then click Update Mesh in Patina . Patina keeps your layers. If only the UVs changed, the paint is moved to the new UVs. If the topology changed (added loops, extrusions, sculpting), the paint is transferred through 3D onto the nearest poi"},{"p":"blender.html","a":"scene","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Several objects","x":"Select several meshes in Blender (a character's body, clothes, eyes and hair) and press Send to Patina . The active one starts the scene and the others come in beside it, each an object with its own layers (Patina 1.2 and the add-on 0.14). Add to the Patina Scene adds the selected meshes to what Patina has open. Each comes in where Blender has it (its matrix_world ), and its sculpt face sets come along as face sets. An update goes to its own object, and the one you paint stays the one painted. Send Textures to Blender sends every linked object's textures. Auto Update Mesh only updates the objects Patina's scene has: an object you removed from the scene stays out. See Scenes ."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"auto-update","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Auto Update Mesh","x":"Turn on Auto Update Mesh under Update Mesh in Patina and you don't have to click it any more: after you change a mesh that was sent to Patina (modeling, sculpting, UV edits in Blender), the add-on sends it again by itself once you pause for the Settle Time (1.5 s by default; sculpting waits three times as long). Patina keeps the layers and moves the paint, as with the button. Only objects already sent are watched, nothing is sent while Patina is still busy with the last one, and UVs that Patina sends back don't trigger a send of their own. It is off by default; the setting is also in the add-on preferences."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"reopen","page":"Blender bridge","t":"After a restart","x":"Your layers live in Patina's project, not in Blender. Blender only gets the finished textures. So save the project ( Ctrl + S ) after you paint. When Blender sends an object that Patina doesn't have open, for example after you closed or restarted Patina, Patina looks for the project it was painted in (0.44). That is the last project saved or opened for that object, or a project in your recent files that is linked to it. Patina opens it and keeps its layers. If the mesh changed since, the paint moves across, as with Update Mesh in Patina . The status bar names the project it opened. Patina leaves a project alone when the object is another model with the same name: other faces, in another place, at another size. It also starts a new project when you send with Keep Layers off. The switch is Blender: reopen an object's project in Preferences \u203a Features. With no saved project, the object starts from what its Blender materials show (add-on 0.13). For each material slot, Patina reads the Principled BSDF's colors and values and the images behind Base Color, Roughness, Metallic, Normal (through a Normal Map or Bump node), Emission, Alpha, Coat and Sheen. They come in as the layer From the m"},{"p":"blender.html","a":"symmetry","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Select Asymmetric Vertices","x":"With a mesh sent to Patina, Select Asymmetric Vertices runs Patina's symmetry check and selects the vertices whose mirror image misses the other side by more than the tolerance (and those with no other side at all), in Object or Edit Mode. The Patina panel shows the verdict and the worst distance. Fix them with Blender's own tools (Symmetrize, snapping, the shape key and weight tools) so the rig and shape keys stay in step, then Update Mesh in Patina and check again."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"texture-sets","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Texture sets and UDIM tiles","x":"The add-on sends the names of the object's material slots, and Patina names its texture sets after them. When Patina paints a texture set per material slot, Blender gets a material per slot, Patina_<object>_<set> , each with its own images, and each slot shows its own material. With UDIM tiles, every map becomes one tiled image (Blender's UDIM images) that reads Patina_<object>_basecolor.<UDIM>.png and so on, with a tile for each of Patina's tiles. The replaced materials are kept, and Restore Materials puts them back, as with a single texture set."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"surface","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Opacity, sheen, clearcoat and subsurface","x":"When the texture set uses them (see Materials ), Patina sends their maps too, and the add-on (0.8) connects them to the Principled BSDF's Alpha , Sheen Weight , Coat Weight and Subsurface Weight . With an opacity map the material is set to Dithered , which shows lace and cut-outs without sorting problems. When the texture set stops using them, the links go again."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"uv-edits-go-back","page":"Blender bridge","t":"UV edits go back","x":"If you unwrap or move UVs in Patina, the next export also sends the UVs and seams. Blender writes them into the active UV map, through BMesh if the object is in Edit Mode, so you don't have to leave it. Blender does this only when the mesh still has the same face corners as the one that was sent."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"retopology","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Retopology goes back as a new object","x":"With a linked object, Retopology \u203a Send the Quads to Blender (or Send to Blender in the Retopology window) adds the quads made over it as a new object, name_retopo , in the same collections and the same place. Sending again replaces its mesh, so you can repaint the patches in Patina and send once more. This needs the 0.7 add-on. See Retopology ."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"materials","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Patina's materials in Blender","x":"All of Patina's materials can sit in Blender's Asset Browser , ready to drag onto any object. In Blender, open the Material Library part of the Patina tab and click Add Patina Materials to Blender (or, in Patina, the Add Patina's materials to Blender button in the Blender Link window). Choose: Map size : 512, 1024 or 2048 pixels. 1024 needs about 300 MB for all of them. Mapping : Box (like Patina) projects the material from the sides, top and front, as Patina does, so it needs no UVs and is the same size on every object (the Scale node is in tiles per meter). UV follows the object's UV map and uses Patina's normal maps. Materials : Patina's own, or Patina's and yours (the materials you made in Patina's material editor or from your images). Patina bakes every material (a hidden Patina does it when Patina isn't running), and Blender builds the library in the background, which takes about a minute. It goes into Documents/Patina Materials (change it with Material Library Folder in the add-on's preferences) and is added to Preferences \u203a File Paths \u203a Asset Libraries as Patina Materials . In the Asset Browser the materials are in Patina's categories, with Patina's previews, under Patina ;"},{"p":"blender.html","a":"model-to-blender","page":"Blender bridge","t":"The model back to Blender","x":"Blender \u203a Send Model to Blender as a New Object sends the model as it is in Patina (mended in the Mesh Check , say) to Blender as a new object with its textures, and Patina links to it. See Sending the model to Blender . This needs the 0.9 add-on."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"without-the-add-on","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Without the add-on","x":"If you prefer exporting files, Patina still keeps up with Blender: export the model as .glb or .obj , open it in Patina, and when you export over the same file after a change in Blender, Patina offers to update the mesh with your layers kept (see Projects ). For game assets, export the sculpt from the same scene and bake it with Bake \u203a From a High-poly Mesh ( high-poly baking ). Material slots come along in both cases."},{"p":"blender.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Blender bridge","t":"Gotchas","x":"\u201cUVs not applied: \u2026 loops\u201d The mesh changed in Blender after you sent it, for example faces were added or deleted, or the mesh was sorted. Click Update Mesh in Patina , then pull again. Modifiers Apply Modifiers When Sending sends what you see. Modifiers that only move vertices, such as Armature, Shrinkwrap or shape keys, keep every face, so edited UVs still come back. Modifiers that add or remove faces, such as Subdivision Surface or Mirror, give a mesh that no longer matches the object: UVs edited in Patina can't be written back then, and neither can textures painted on them. Paint on the final mesh, or apply those modifiers in Blender first. \u201c\u2026 not updated: Patina changed its UVs\u201d Textures painted on Patina's UV layout only fit that layout. When those UVs can't be written into Blender (the mesh changed, the UVs were edited in Blender after sending, or faces were added by modifiers), the add-on leaves the old textures in place rather than show the new ones scrambled. The message says why (in Blender's status bar). Usually Update Mesh in Patina , then pushing again, fixes it. \u201cBlender's UVs kept as UV map 'UVMap before Patina'\u201d An object sent to Patina without UVs gets Patina's UV"},{"p":"automation.html","a":"","page":"Claude & automation","t":"Claude and automation","x":"Claude and automation Every action in Patina is also a command. Claude can call the commands through MCP, scripts can call them over a local socket, other applications over HTTP (see HTTP API and batch mode ), and you can type them in the Console. Claude can set up layer stacks, paint, unwrap, export, and look at the result."},{"p":"automation.html","a":"claude-app","page":"Claude & automation","t":"The Claude desktop app","x":"Open the Blender Link panel and click Connect to Claude app , or run patina --setup-claude . Then quit the Claude app completely and open it again. Patina adds itself to the app's MCP servers in claude_desktop_config.json ( ~/.config/Claude/ on Linux, %APPDATA%\\Claude\\ on Windows), with the full path to this copy of Patina. Other servers, such as Blender MCP, and other settings are kept. The previous file is saved next to it as claude_desktop_config.json.bak . On Windows the entry points to patina-cli.exe , because the app talks to Patina over stdin/stdout. Once it is set up, the panel shows The Claude app can drive this window . \"Patina isn't connected to this chat\" The Claude app reads its MCP servers only when it starts, so restart it after connecting. Keep a Patina window open if you want Claude to work in it. Otherwise Claude starts its own Patina without a window. If the button reports that the file is not valid JSON, fix or remove the file first: Patina never overwrites a configuration it can't read."},{"p":"automation.html","a":"claude-code-and-other-mcp-clients","page":"Claude & automation","t":"Claude Code and other MCP clients","x":"claude mcp add patina -- ~/.local/bin/patina --mcp # Linux claude mcp add patina -- \"%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Patina\\patina-cli.exe\" --mcp # Windows Any other MCP client works with the same command. For example, in a JSON configuration: { \"mcpServers\": { \"patina\": { \"command\": \"/home/you/.local/bin/patina\", \"args\": [\"--mcp\"] } } } patina --mcp chooses one of two modes when it starts: Mode When What Claude sees Live A Patina window is already open Claude works in your window. You see every stroke, and screenshot_window returns exactly what is on your screen. Headless No window is open Patina runs off-screen. render_view and render_uv return pictures. open_gui_window hands the project to a real window at any time, and later calls then drive that window."},{"p":"automation.html","a":"guides","page":"Claude & automation","t":"What Claude gets to work with (0.34)","x":"Every tool comes with its settings: their type, their range, their default, and a sentence on what moving them does ( \u201cLower: more, closer pieces\u201d ). On top of the tools, the MCP server gives agents: What For Guides (the patina_guide tool, and the resources patina://guide/\u2026 ) workflow : the order of the tools from opening a model to exporting it; mesh : checking, mending, transforming, reducing and collision, setting by setting; develop : the adjustment layer's settings and how to tune them by numbers; export : formats, sizes, levels of detail and collision names per engine; batch : recipes and batches; painting ; and parameters : every setting of every tool, written from the tools themselves so it is always up to date. Prompts Finish a model (for a game, the web, printing or rendering), Develop the textures , Make it game ready (Unreal, Godot or Unity) and Batch a folder . In Claude Code they are slash commands such as /mcp__patina__finish_model . Numbers to check by mesh_check (problems), mesh_reduce with preview (how far the shape would move), mesh_collision ( fit ), and texture_check : how much of the color is out of a real material's range, the color cast and spread, with hint"},{"p":"automation.html","a":"things-to-ask-claude","page":"Claude & automation","t":"Things to ask Claude","x":"\u201cFinish ~/Downloads/robot.glb for Godot: mend it, keep it under 20,000 triangles, convex collision, two levels of detail.\u201d \u201cThe textures of this scan are too dark and orange, and the creases are baked in. Develop it to a neutral daylight look and tell me the settings.\u201d \u201cRun the Game asset recipe on every model in ~/assets/props and tell me which failed.\u201d \u201cOpen ~/models/crate.glb , give it worn painted metal with rust on the edges and dirt in the cavities, and show me.\u201d \u201cThe UVs of this mesh overlap. Unwrap it with smart project, pack it, and check that the paint stayed in place.\u201d \u201cApply the rusted iron smart material, put a snow cover on top, and solo the snow folder so I can see it.\u201d \u201cPaint a 2 cm red stripe around the middle of the barrel, then export for Unreal at 4K into ~/exports .\u201d \u201cCompare three rust variations using an adjustment layer and take a screenshot of each.\u201d Claude can read the full list of tools with their parameters. Images (renders, screenshots, textures) are returned to it as pictures."},{"p":"automation.html","a":"the-console","page":"Claude & automation","t":"The console","x":"The Console tab takes command {json} , for example: add_layer {\"type\": \"fill\", \"material\": {\"id\": \"patina_rust_heavy\"}, \"generator\": {\"type\": \"edges\", \"level\": 0.55}} paint_stroke {\"points\": [[0.4, 0.5], [0.6, 0.5]], \"brush\": {\"size\": 40}} export_textures {\"directory\": \"/tmp/out\", \"preset\": \"gltf\"}"},{"p":"automation.html","a":"the-command-server","page":"Claude & automation","t":"The command server","x":"Patina listens on 127.0.0.1:7860 (change it with --port or PATINA_PORT ). The protocol is newline-delimited JSON: one request per line, and one response per line with the same id . import json, socket s = socket.create_connection((\"127.0.0.1\", 7860)); f = s.makefile(\"rwb\") def call(cmd, **args): f.write((json.dumps({\"id\": 1, \"cmd\": cmd, \"args\": args}) + \"\\n\").encode()); f.flush() return json.loads(f.readline()) print(call(\"get_state\")[\"result\"][\"mesh\"]) call(\"set_paint_material\", id=\"patina_gold_polished\") call(\"paint_stroke\", points=[[0.3, 0.5], [0.7, 0.5]]) To repeat a sequence of commands, record it with the macro recorder : every command a script or Claude runs while it records becomes a step, and the saved macro is a job file for patina run . To run the same steps on many models, save them as a recipe and use a batch. patina --headless runs only the server, with no window. scripts/patina_client.py in the source is a small ready-made client. For other applications, other machines, background jobs and job files, use the HTTP API and batch mode ."},{"p":"automation.html","a":"coordinates","page":"Claude & automation","t":"Coordinates","x":"paint_stroke points are normalized viewport coordinates by default: 0\u20131, with the origin at the top left. \"space\": \"uv\" gives texture coordinates (origin at the bottom left) and \"space\": \"viewport\" gives pixels. paint_world_points paints at 3D positions on the model, so you can work without the camera. pick tells you what is under a viewport point: the 3D position, the UV and the current values. The command reference lists every command with its parameters. The desktop window adds a few of its own: screenshot_window , pen_info (what the pen tablet sends) and set_pen_settings (the pressure curve and the pen buttons)."},{"p":"automation.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Claude & automation","t":"Gotchas","x":"Windows: use patina-cli.exe MCP clients talk to the server over standard input and output, which only console programs have. Point the client at patina-cli.exe , not at patina.exe . Two windows, one port A second Patina window can't open port 7860 while the first one has it. It takes the next free port instead (never 7861, which is the Blender add-on's), but Claude and Blender talk to the first window. Start the second window with --port 7862 if you want to choose. The MCP server and your window When Claude starts patina --mcp and no Patina window is open, it works on a hidden Patina of its own. That hidden Patina leaves port 7860 free, so a window you open afterward gets it, and from then on Claude's tools drive that window. (Until 1.2.6 the hidden Patina kept the port: the window moved to 7861, and Blender's add-on got the hidden Patina's sphere.) Headless needs a GPU driver too Headless mode renders off-screen with the same OpenGL 4.5 code. Linux uses EGL, so no display server is needed. Windows uses a hidden window, so it needs a normal desktop session with a GPU driver. Security The server accepts connections only from this computer (127.0.0.1). Any program you run locally can"},{"p":"api.html","a":"","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"HTTP API and batch mode","x":"HTTP API and batch mode Other programs can use Patina as a service: an asset pipeline that bakes every model checked in, a render farm that textures hundreds of props overnight, an in-house tool with a \"make textures\" button. They talk to Patina over HTTP with JSON, on this machine or across your local network, but never from the internet. For scripts and CI there is also a batch mode that runs a job file and exits."},{"p":"api.html","a":"starting-the-api","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Starting the API","x":"patina --headless --http # this machine only, port 7870 patina --headless --lan --workspace /srv/patina # the local network, with a token In the desktop application, turn it on in Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a HTTP API ( Enable , and Local network for other machines). The window then takes requests while you work, and you can watch them happen. On a server, run it headless: Linux needs no display (EGL); Windows needs a logged-in desktop session with a GPU driver. Option Meaning --http , --http-port N Start the API (default port 7870). On its own it answers this machine only. --lan Also answer other machines of the local network (see who can connect ). --bind ADDR Listen on one network interface only, for example the internal network card of a server. --allow CIDR Accept only these address ranges (plus this machine), for example --allow 10.20.0.0/16 . Repeatable. --token T The access token (or PATINA_TOKEN ). With --lan one is generated if you give none. --workspace DIR The folder for uploads and results. Relative paths in commands are inside it. Default: api in Patina's cache folder. --sandbox Refuse any path outside the workspace. Recommended for shared servers. --max-upload-mb N The large"},{"p":"api.html","a":"reach","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Who can connect","x":"This machine (the default): Patina listens on 127.0.0.1 only. Requests whose Host header names another site are refused, so a web page can't reach the API through DNS tricks. Local network ( --lan ): Patina listens on the network but answers only clients with private addresses: 10.x, 172.16\u201331.x, 192.168.x, link-local 169.254.x, IPv6 unique-local (fc00::/7) and link-local (fe80::/10), and this machine. Anything else, including every internet address, gets 403 and a line in the log. --allow narrows this to your own ranges. Token: on the network every request needs Authorization: Bearer <token> (or an X-Patina-Token header). If you don't give one, Patina generates it once, stores it in api_token in the settings folder (on Linux readable only by you), and prints it at start-up. Preferences shows and copies it, and New token replaces it. Only /api/v1/health works without the token, for load balancers and monitoring. Browsers: requests that carry an Origin header (sent by web pages) are refused unless that origin is listed with --cors-origin . Treat the token like a password Whoever has it can run any command, which includes reading and writing files the Patina process can reach. Use --"},{"p":"api.html","a":"endpoints","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Endpoints","x":"All under http://host:7870/api/v1 . Replies are JSON with \"ok\": true or \"ok\": false and an \"error\" message. Request Does GET /health Version, whether a job is running, queue length. No token needed. GET /info Version, workspace, limits, the current state (mesh, layers, resolution\u2026). GET /commands Every command with its description and JSON Schema (the same list Claude sees). GET /openapi.json An OpenAPI 3 description of everything, for client generators and API tools. POST /commands/{name} Runs one command; the body is its arguments. Answers when it is done. POST /jobs Queues a job (several commands in order) and answers at once with its id ( 202 ). GET /jobs , GET /jobs/{id}?wait=S Job status, progress and results. wait holds the reply up to S seconds until the job finishes. DELETE /jobs/{id} Cancels a job. PUT /files/{path} Uploads a file into the workspace (the raw body). GET /files?dir=D&recursive=1 Lists the workspace. GET /files/{path} , DELETE /files/{path} Downloads or deletes a workspace file or folder. Every command works: opening meshes and projects, layers and smart materials, painting, UV tools, bake_mesh_maps , bake_from_high_poly , export_textures , render_image , sa"},{"p":"api.html","a":"jobs","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Jobs","x":"Long work (bakes, high resolutions, many assets) belongs in jobs. Jobs run one at a time in the order they arrive. A job has the engine to itself while it runs, so no other client can change the project between its steps. POST /api/v1/jobs { \"name\": \"crate\", \"stop_on_error\": true, \"steps\": [ {\"cmd\": \"open_file\", \"args\": {\"path\": \"in/crate_low.glb\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"set_resolution\", \"args\": {\"resolution\": 4096}}, {\"cmd\": \"bake_from_high_poly\", \"args\": {\"path\": \"in/crate_high.glb\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"apply_smart_material\",\"args\": {\"id\": \"patina_smart_worn_painted_metal\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"export_textures\", \"args\": {\"directory\": \"out/crate\", \"preset\": \"unreal\"}} ] } The status ( GET /jobs/{id} ) looks like this while it runs: {\"id\": \"j7-3fa2c1\", \"name\": \"crate\", \"status\": \"running\", \"steps\": 5, \"current_step\": 2, \"progress\": 0.52, \"label\": \"Baking from the high-poly mesh\", \"results\": [ ... ]} status is queued , running , done , failed or cancelled . progress runs from 0 to 1 over the whole job. Bakes, renders and exports report their own progress. results has one entry per finished step: cmd , ok , seconds , and result or error . With stop_on_error (the default), the first failing step ends the job as fai"},{"p":"api.html","a":"files-and-paths","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Files and paths","x":"Relative paths in arguments ( path , directory , \u2026_path ) are inside the workspace, so a client on another machine uploads its inputs, runs commands with relative paths, and downloads the results: curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T\" -X PUT --data-binary @crate_low.glb http://render-07:7870/api/v1/files/in/crate_low.glb curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T\" \"http://render-07:7870/api/v1/files?dir=out/crate\" curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $T\" -o crate_BaseColor.png http://render-07:7870/api/v1/files/out/crate/crate_low_BaseColor.png Absolute paths work too when the server and the client share a disk (a network share), unless the server runs with --sandbox . The file endpoints never reach outside the workspace."},{"p":"api.html","a":"the-python-client","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"The Python client","x":"clients/python/patina_api.py (standard library only) wraps all of this: from patina_api import Patina pt = Patina(\"http://render-07:7870\", token=\"\u2026\") pt.upload(\"crate_low.glb\", \"in/crate_low.glb\") pt.upload(\"crate_high.glb\", \"in/crate_high.glb\") job = pt.run([(\"open_file\", {\"path\": \"in/crate_low.glb\"}), (\"bake_from_high_poly\", {\"path\": \"in/crate_high.glb\"}), (\"export_textures\", {\"directory\": \"out/crate\", \"preset\": \"gltf\"})], name=\"crate\", on_progress=lambda j: print(j[\"progress\"], j.get(\"label\"))) for f in pt.files(\"out/crate\"): pt.download(f[\"path\"], \"textures/\" + f[\"path\"].split(\"/\")[-1]) pt.call(\"set_brush\", size=40) runs a single command. Errors raise PatinaError (with the HTTP status) and failed jobs raise JobFailed (with the job's results). For other languages, generate a client from /api/v1/openapi.json . A small render farm: examples/api/farm_bake.py takes a folder of name_low.glb / name_high.glb pairs and a list of Patina servers, gives each server the next asset as soon as it is free, and collects the textures. Run one Patina per GPU; each one works on one job at a time. python3 examples/api/farm_bake.py --token $PATINA_TOKEN \\ --server http://render-01:7870 --server http"},{"p":"api.html","a":"batch","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Batch mode","x":"patina run runs a job file on this machine without a window or a server, then exits. It suits CI, cron and shell loops: patina run bake_job.json --set low=crate_low.glb --set high=crate_high.glb --set asset=crate for f in props/*_low.glb; do n=$(basename \"$f\" _low.glb) patina run bake_job.json --set low=\"$f\" --set high=\"props/${n}_high.glb\" --set asset=\"$n\" --quiet || echo \"$n failed\" done The job file has the same steps as an API job, plus optional default vars ( examples/api/bake_job.json is a complete one): { \"name\": \"Bake and export\", \"vars\": {\"resolution\": \"2048\", \"preset\": \"unreal\"}, \"steps\": [ {\"cmd\": \"open_file\", \"args\": {\"path\": \"${low}\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"set_resolution\", \"args\": {\"resolution\": \"${resolution}\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"bake_from_high_poly\", \"args\": {\"path\": \"${high}\"}}, {\"cmd\": \"export_textures\", \"args\": {\"directory\": \"out/${asset}\", \"preset\": \"${preset}\"}} ] } ${name} is replaced by --set name=value , else by vars . A value that is a whole number or true / false keeps that type when it stands alone ( \"${resolution}\" becomes 2048). ${job_dir} and ${cwd} are always defined. A name without a value stops the run before anything happens. Relative paths are next to the job file. E"},{"p":"api.html","a":"gotchas","page":"HTTP API & batch mode","t":"Gotchas","x":"\"Patina only answers requests from the local network\" The client's address isn't private. That happens through NAT gateways, some VPNs (Tailscale uses 100.64.0.0/10, which is not private) and port forwarding. Add the range with --allow if it is yours. get_state is slow while a job runs Read-only commands are answered between a job's steps, so during a long bake they wait for it. Use GET /jobs/{id} or /health to watch a job; those never wait. One project per server A Patina server holds one project at a time, and jobs replace it with whatever they open. Start each job with open_file or new_project , and run several Patina processes (different --http-port , ideally one per GPU) to work in parallel. The local command server is still there The newline-JSON server on port 7860 ( Claude and automation ) keeps working for local scripts, Blender and MCP. The HTTP API is separate and off unless you start it."},{"p":"web.html","a":"","page":"Patina Web","t":"Patina Web (three.js)","x":"Patina Web Patina Web is a lightweight version of the painter built with three.js. It runs in any modern browser, with nothing to install and no internet connection needed. Patina Web: a fill layer of chipped paint, a gold stroke and some rust, on the sample Suzanne."},{"p":"web.html","a":"opening-it","page":"Patina Web","t":"Opening it","x":"From Patina: Help \u203a Patina Web (Browser Painter) . Directly: open web/index.html from the download (on Linux after install.sh : ~/.local/share/patina/web/index.html ). Double-clicking the file works, and no web server is needed. To share it, copy the whole web folder to any static web host. It needs a browser with WebGL 2: Firefox, Chrome, Edge or Safari 15+."},{"p":"web.html","a":"what-it-does","page":"Patina Web","t":"What it does","x":"Paints the same 270 patina_* library materials, projected in 3D independently of the UVs, just like the desktop app. + Image in the library turns any photo or texture into a material of your own. Tools: Paint , Erase , Blur , Fill and Pick , plus the Island brush. The brush has size, hardness, flow, spacing, round / square / spray / grunge tips, pen pressure, paint-through, channel toggles (color, rough/metal, height) and Mirror X / Y / Z . Layers : paint layers, fill layers and adjustment layers , each with opacity, a blend mode and a mask. Fill and adjustment layers can use a smart mask . A texture / UV view that shows the maps with the UV wireframe. You can paint there in texture space. Opens .glb , self-contained .gltf , .obj , .fbx (FBX 7, from 2011 on; the desktop app also reads the older FBX 6), .stl and .3mf . Includes built-in shapes and the Suzanne sample. The Island brush : paint over a part of the model and it becomes one UV island, with mirroring, orientation, texel density and pinning, plus a right-click menu in the UV view with Relax and shape packing. Unique UVs : one click gives a model that has no UVs, or overlapping UVs, a clean per-triangle layout. Your paint is"},{"p":"web.html","a":"tools","page":"Patina Web","t":"Tools","x":"Blur ( R ) softens what is already painted on the current layer, under the brush. Blur radius sets how far it reaches. Fill ( P ) fills with a click. Fill chooses how much: one Triangle , its UV island , the Connected part of the mesh, or the Whole model . With Mirror X / Y / Z on, the other side is filled too. On a fill or adjustment layer it fills the mask. Pick ( I ) reads the color, roughness and metallic under the cursor into the flat color. Hold I and click to pick without leaving the brush; tap I to pick once and return. Mirror X / Y / Z paints the mirrored position too, across the model's own axes. It works with Paint, Erase, Blur and Fill."},{"p":"web.html","a":"smart-masks","page":"Patina Web","t":"Layers and smart masks","x":"+ Paint , + Fill and + Adj add layers. An adjustment layer changes everything below it: hue, saturation, brightness, contrast, and roughness and metallic offsets. Every layer has an opacity and a blend mode: normal, multiply, screen, overlay, add, soft light, darken or lighten. Fill and adjustment layers can use a smart mask , computed from the shape of the model: A fill layer of mud with the Cavities smart mask, over brushed steel. Mask Covers Edges (wear) Convex edges and corners, where paint wears first Cavities (dirt) Creases and hollows, where dirt gathers Facing up (dust, snow) Faces that point up, where dust and snow settle Height gradient A fade from the bottom of the model to the top Noise Cloudy patches in 3D Amount sets how much it covers, Softness blurs its edge, Breakup roughens the edge with noise and Invert flips it. The smart mask is multiplied with the painted one, so erasing on the layer hides parts of it and painting brings them back. Curvature is per vertex The web version measures edges and cavities on the mesh's vertices, so they follow the polygons. A low-polygon model gives broad, soft edges. The desktop app bakes curvature and ambient occlusion per texel."},{"p":"web.html","a":"projects","page":"Patina Web","t":"Projects","x":"Save project ( Ctrl + S ) downloads a .patinaweb file with the model, its UVs, the island pins and mirror links, every layer with its settings, and the image materials you added. Open it again with Open ( Ctrl + O ) or by dropping it on the window; the layers come back exactly as they were saved. The file is a zip, with the layers as PNG files. Desktop .patina projects and .patinaweb projects are separate formats. To move work between them, export the maps or a GLB."},{"p":"web.html","a":"island-brush","page":"Patina Web","t":"Island brush","x":"The Island brush works like the one in the desktop app (see UV editing ). Pick Island ( L ), paint over a part of the model, and press Enter or Make island . The painted triangles become one UV island: Unwrapped flat (least-squares conformal). A closed part, such as a whole head, is split into pieces first. Oriented : Upright turns the model's up direction to the texture's up. Longest side lines it up with the texture's width. Keep leaves it as unwrapped. Scaled to a texel density: Automatic or Match the layout gives it the density of the rest of the UVs, Fit the texture makes it as big as the free space allows, and Custom sets texels per unit. Placed in free space . If nothing fits, the unpinned islands are packed again around the pinned ones. Mirrored with Mirror X / Y / Z : the other half is selected too, and it gets the same UVs, stacked on the first half, so both sides share the texels. The halves stay linked: moving either moves both. Pinned ( Pin new islands ), so later edits leave it where it is. Pin and Unpin under Make island pin or unpin the painted region's islands, and Alt + P unpins over the 3D view. While the Island brush is chosen, pinned islands have blue stripes o"},{"p":"web.html","a":"islands-in-the-uv-view","page":"Patina Web","t":"Islands in the UV view","x":"Switch the UV view's mode from Paint to Islands . A click selects an island, Shift adds and Ctrl removes, and dragging on empty space draws a box. Dragging a selected island moves it, along with its mirrored half. Pinned islands don't move: P pins and Alt + P unpins. Right-click for everything else: rotate and flip, orient, set the density, move into free space, select the other half, re-stack or unstack mirrored halves, select pinned islands, and pack all islands. The right-click menu in the UV view."},{"p":"web.html","a":"controls","page":"Patina Web","t":"Controls","x":"Action Input Paint / erase / blur Left mouse \u00b7 B / E / R Fill P , then click Pick a color Hold I and click, or tap I for one pick Orbit Alt + left drag, or middle drag Pan / zoom Right drag / mouse wheel Look along an axis Click X, Y or Z in the axis gizmo (top right corner); the end facing you again for the other side; drag there to orbit Brush size [ ] , or Ctrl + wheel Straight line Shift +click Undo / redo Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Shift + Z Open / save project Ctrl + O / Ctrl + S UV view / frame U / F Island brush / make the island L / Enter ( Ctrl + paint removes) Island brush: all / none / grow / shrink A / Alt + A / Ctrl + = / Ctrl + - Pin / unpin islands (UV view, Islands mode) P / Alt + P , right-click for more Unpin the painted islands (3D view, Island brush) Alt + P"},{"p":"web.html","a":"compared-with-the-desktop-app","page":"Patina Web","t":"Compared with the desktop app","x":"Patina Web is the lighter of the two. It has the same painting model and most of the everyday tools, in a simpler interface. Desktop Web Material painting, independent of the UVs \u2713 \u2713 Paint, fill and adjustment layers, blend modes, painted masks \u2713 \u2713 Smart masks Baked per texel: AO, curvature, thickness Per vertex: edges, cavity, up, gradient, noise Blur, polygon fill, color picker, mirror painting \u2713 \u2713 Clone, smudge \u2713 \u2013 Island brush: mirroring, orientation, density, pinning \u2713 \u2713 Island brush along the surface (Surface) \u2713 \u2013 (the faces seen in the circle) Relax, shape packing \u2713 \u2713 Seam brush, unwrap methods (cylinder, sphere, smart project), weld, align, stretch maps \u2713 \u2013 Texture sets per material slot, UDIM tiles \u2713 \u2013 Your own materials Material editor, smart materials From an image Projects .patina .patinaweb Blender bridge, MCP \u2713 \u2013 Export 4 presets, up to 8K 3 presets or GLB, up to 4K"},{"p":"web.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Patina Web","t":"Gotchas","x":"Nothing is saved between visits Patina Web keeps everything in memory. Reloading the page or closing the tab loses the painting, so use Save project first. Pins live in the project Island pins and mirror links are saved in the .patinaweb file. The exported GLB has the new UVs, but no pins. 4096 needs memory At 4096 each layer uses about 200 MB of graphics memory. On laptops and phones, stay at 2048 unless you need the detail. Overlapping UVs paint twice A model whose UVs overlap (mirrored halves, or a status bar warning about overlap) shares paint between those parts. Click Unique UVs to separate them. The exported GLB then uses the new UVs. Unique UVs have many seams The automatic layout gives each quad its own spot. Paint stays continuous because it is projected in 3D, but at low resolutions you may see faint lines along polygon edges, especially in the normal map. Use 2048, or unwrap the model properly in the desktop app or Blender. Original textures are ignored Patina Web paints over a neutral base. The model's existing textures and materials aren't imported. Privacy Everything runs on your computer. Models and textures are never uploaded."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Gotchas and troubleshooting","x":"Gotchas and troubleshooting Things that surprise people, why they happen, and what to do about them. The other pages have their own gotchas too; this page gathers the important ones in one place."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"overlapping-uvs","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Paint shows up in two places","x":"Two parts of the model use the same area of the texture. That happens with mirrored halves, with stacked or duplicated islands, and with \u201cUV tiles\u201d that were collapsed into one. Each texel can hold only one color, so painting one part also paints the other. Patina warns about it when it loads the model. Fix: give the overlapping parts their own space with UV \u203a Unwrap \u203a Smart UV Project , then Pack Islands . Existing paint is carried over. In Patina Web, use Unique UVs . If the mirroring is intentional, for example to save texture space, paint symmetrical details only, or paint on a mirrored copy in Blender."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-stroke-stops-at-a-hidden-edge-or-goes-through-the-model","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A stroke stops at a hidden edge or goes through the model","x":"Painting respects visibility: only surfaces you can see under the brush get paint. Turn on Paint through ( Occlusion off) to reach hidden surfaces, and remember to turn it back off. Very thin geometry (cloth, leaves) may need paint-through on purpose."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"strokes-are-soft-or-blocky-up-close","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Strokes are soft or blocky up close","x":"That part of the model has too few texels. Raise the resolution, or scale its islands up in the UV editor. The UV checker view shows texel density."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"faint-lines-along-uv-seams","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Faint lines along UV seams","x":"Seams are where the texture is cut, and filtering can pick up a little color from the other side. Keep Dilate seams on (Texture Set), and use resolutions of 2048 and up for final textures. Normal-map seams are smallest when islands follow the model's natural edges."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-material-looks-stretched-or-blurred-on-one-side","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The material looks stretched or blurred on one side","x":"Tri-planar projection blends three directions. Where a surface faces diagonally, the directions meet. Raise Tri-planar blend , rotate the material, or switch to UV projection on a well-unwrapped model."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-pattern-shows-a-faint-second-copy-of-itself-ghosting","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A pattern shows a faint second copy of itself (ghosting)","x":"Two causes, both fixed in 0.7.4. Where tri-planar projections met, they were cross-faded, so two patterns showed at half strength (see tri-planar seams ). On a symmetric island, both halves wrote their own values into the texels they share, leaving a patchwork of triangles with the left and right half's pattern; now the half that is unwrapped decides and the stacked half shows the same texels mirrored. Projects from older versions pick both fixes up when opened: fill layers and smart masks are recomputed. Paint strokes keep what they were painted with, so repaint strokes from an older version that still look doubled."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-smart-mask-puts-wear-in-odd-places","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A smart mask puts wear in odd places","x":"The AO / curvature bake is stale (re-bake after mesh or UV changes), or the mesh has no bevels, so all edges are equally sharp. Lower Level , add Noise , or paint the mask by hand."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-layer-or-the-whole-stack-seems-to-have-vanished","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A layer or the whole stack seems to have vanished","x":"A layer is soloed : look for the yellow Solo: \u2026 button above the layer list and click it. Solo is not saved in the project, and exports ignore it."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"painting-on-a-folder-hid-everything-in-it","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Painting on a folder hid everything in it","x":"The brush paints a folder's mask , and a new mask starts black, so only the painted spot shows. Undo, then use Add White Mask on the folder and erase where it should be hidden."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"undo-went-further-back-than-expected","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Undo went further back than expected","x":"Each slider change is its own step, recorded when you let go. A long session of tweaks becomes many small steps. The History panel shows them all. Click the step you want instead of pressing Ctrl + Z many times. Each object keeps 100 steps by default; set how many, and how much memory undo may use, in Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Undo (see How much undo keeps )."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-smart-material-with-paint-looks-scrambled","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A smart material with paint looks scrambled","x":"Painted textures in a smart material follow the UV layout of the mesh they were painted on. Save smart materials without paint to use them on other models."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"after-unwrapping-the-model-shows-no-paint","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"After unwrapping, the model shows no paint","x":"UV \u203a Keep Paint on UV Edits was off, so the paint stayed where it was in the texture while the UVs moved. Undo ( Ctrl + Z ), turn the option on, and unwrap again."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-island-brush-made-several-small-islands-or-the-whole-layout-moved","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The Island brush made several small islands, or the whole layout moved","x":"Each connected piece of the painted area becomes its own island; paint across gaps, or grow the selection ( Ctrl +Numpad + ). If the new island didn't fit into free space, all islands were repacked at an equal density, and the status bar says so. Paint moves with them, so the model looks the same. Undo, and pick a lower density in UV \u203a Islands \u203a Texel Density if you want the old layout kept."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-texel-density-heat-map-is-mostly-blue-or-red","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The texel density heat map is mostly blue or red","x":"The heat map compares every face with the target ( UV \u203a Islands \u203a Texel Density ). A model whose UVs come from a projection (like the Suzanne sample) varies a lot from face to face. Islands made with the Island brush, or Pack , even it out."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"uv-tiles-udims","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"UV tiles / UDIMs","x":"A model whose islands each sit inside one unit tile, across several tiles, opens with UDIM tiles: one texture per tile (see Texture sets and UDIM tiles ). Check Texture Set \u203a Texture sets if a model opens differently from what you expect."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"paint-shows-up-on-two-parts-of-the-model-at-once","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Paint shows up on two parts of the model at once","x":"Those parts share UV space. Mirrored halves stacked on purpose do that. If they are different material slots (body and clothes each laid out over the whole texture), choose Texture Set \u203a Texture sets \u203a Per material slot . Otherwise use UV \u203a Fix Overlapping UVs ."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"wrong-colors-or-shading-in-another-program","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Wrong colors or shading in another program","x":"Only base color and emission are sRGB color images. All other maps are data: import them as Non-Color / linear. If the normal map makes bumps look like dents, the program expects the other green-channel convention. Use the Unreal preset (DirectX) or flip green."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-export-file-names-are-not-what-i-typed","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The export file names are not what I typed","x":"Unknown $tokens stay as typed, and characters that are not allowed in file names ( / \\ : * ? \" < > | ) are replaced with _ . The example under the template shows the real name before you export."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-baked-high-poly-detail-has-flat-patches","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The baked high-poly detail has flat patches","x":"Some texels didn't find the sculpt within the cage Distance . Make sure both meshes were exported from the same Blender scene with the same transform, then raise Distance in Bake \u203a From a High-poly Mesh . The status bar reports how many texels found the sculpt."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"export-again-is-grayed-out","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Export Again is grayed out","x":"Nothing has been exported from this project yet. Export once with Ctrl + E ; after that Ctrl + Shift + E repeats it, also after reopening the project. Exports to Blender through the bridge don't count."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"textures-exported-but-blender-didn-t-update","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Textures exported but Blender didn't update","x":"The bridge isn't linked (see the status bar), or Blender's listener is off. In Blender, check that Live Link is running, then press Ctrl + B in Patina or Pull Textures in Blender."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"no-pressure-from-the-tablet","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"No pressure from the tablet","x":"Open Help \u203a Pen Tablet Test . If it says No pen events yet while you move the pen, the driver hands the pen to applications as a mouse: on Windows enable Windows Ink in the Wacom / Huion settings; on Linux check sudo libinput debug-tablet . Details in Pen tablets ."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-pen-s-buttons-do-the-wrong-thing","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The pen's buttons do the wrong thing","x":"Set them in Preferences \u203a Pen tablet (right click, middle click, swap colors, undo, nothing). If they still send keys or something else, the tablet driver has remapped them; set them to the default (right / middle click) there."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-sbsar-or-spsm-spp-won-t-open","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A .sbsar (or .spsm, .spp) won't open","x":"Those are Adobe's own formats: an .sbsar needs Adobe's Substance engine to render. Export the material's outputs as images from Substance Designer, Sampler or Player, then import that folder (see Materials from Substance ). Exported texture folders, including packed ORM / MaskMap maps and EXR files, import directly."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"another-machine-can-t-reach-the-http-api","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Another machine can't reach the HTTP API","x":"Check, in order: Patina was started with --lan (or Local network is ticked in Preferences); the firewall lets port 7870 in from your network; the client's address is private (10.x, 172.16\u201331.x, 192.168.x), otherwise add its range with --allow ; the client sends the token. The Patina log names every refused address. See who can connect ."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-api-answers-409-busy","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The API answers 409 \"busy\"","x":"A job is using the engine. Queue your work as a job too ( POST /api/v1/jobs ), or wait longer with ?busy_wait=120 ."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"a-key-stopped-doing-what-it-used-to","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"A key stopped doing what it used to","x":"In 0.3, X swaps the paint colors and the X mirror is on Shift + X . Tab hides all panels (press it again). Any shortcut can be changed back in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts . Two actions on the same key are shown in red there, and only the first one fires."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"u-makes-an-island-instead-of-selecting-blur","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"U makes an island instead of selecting Blur","x":"Since 0.4, U in the Blender keymap does what Blender's Unwrap does: it makes an island from the painted faces. Blur has no key there; give it one in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts . In the Patina keymap U is still Blur."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"patina-asks-to-recover-work-or-says-the-mesh-changed","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Patina asks to recover work, or says the mesh changed","x":"\u201cRecover your work?\u201d appears when Patina didn't close properly last time and an autosave exists. \u201cMesh changed on disk\u201d appears when another program saved the .glb / .obj you opened. Updating keeps your layers. Both are described in Projects ."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"s-f-or-tab-do-something-unexpected","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"S, F or Tab do something unexpected","x":"The Blender keymap is on: S samples a color, F resizes the brush and Ctrl + Space hides the panels. Edit \u203a Preferences shows which keymap is active and switches back to the Patina one. See the Blender keymap reference ."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"patina-starts-with-odd-brush-settings-or-a-tiny-ui","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Patina starts with odd brush settings or a tiny UI","x":"Brush, colors, UI scale and other choices are restored from session.json in the settings folder ( Help \u203a About shows where). Use View \u203a UI Scale \u203a 100% , or quit and delete that file to get the defaults back. keymap.json next to it holds the shortcuts."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"could-not-create-an-opengl-4-5-context","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"\u201cCould not create an OpenGL 4.5 context\u201d","x":"The graphics driver is missing or too old, or you are in a VM or remote session that provides only basic OpenGL. Update the GPU driver. On Fedora, sudo dnf install mesa-dri-drivers ; for NVIDIA, use the RPM Fusion driver. On Windows, install the driver from your GPU vendor, not the generic Microsoft one."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"wayland-and-pen-pressure","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Wayland and pen pressure","x":"The prebuilt Linux binary runs through XWayland. Tablets work through XInput2, but some desktops report pressure only to native Wayland apps. Build from source with SDL3-devel installed for native Wayland."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"file-dialogs-don-t-open-linux","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"File dialogs don't open (Linux)","x":"Patina uses zenity (or kdialog ) for file dialogs. Install one with sudo dnf install zenity . Dragging and dropping files always works."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"windows-windows-protected-your-pc","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Windows: \u201cWindows protected your PC\u201d","x":"The download isn't code-signed. Choose More info \u203a Run anyway . Install it from a folder you own. Don't use Program Files , so Patina can update its files without administrator rights."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"file-names-with-accents-or-non-latin-characters","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"File names with accents or non-Latin characters","x":"These are supported on Linux. On Windows they need Windows 10 1903 or later, because Patina relies on the system's UTF-8 support."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"port-7860-is-already-in-use","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Port 7860 is already in use","x":"Another Patina window (or another program) has the port. The second window still works, but automation talks to the first one. Start it with --port 7870 , and set the same port in the Blender add-on and the MCP command if you want those to use it."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"the-app-is-slow-at-4k-8k","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"The app is slow at 4K / 8K","x":"Every stroke updates the whole texture set on the GPU. At 4K and above, with many layers, an integrated GPU will struggle. Paint at 2K, then switch the resolution up before exporting. Layers are resampled."},{"p":"gotchas.html","a":"windows-build-status","page":"Gotchas & troubleshooting","t":"Windows build status","x":"Windows build status The Windows version is cross-compiled from Linux. It is built from the same code as the Linux app, which passes its automated tests, but the Windows binaries themselves haven't been run on Windows yet. If something behaves differently on Windows, run patina-cli.exe from a terminal: it prints the log, including the OpenGL driver it found."},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Keyboard and mouse reference","x":"Keyboard and mouse reference These are the defaults of the Patina keymap. If you come from Blender, switch to the Blender keymap in Edit \u203a Preferences ; it has its own reference page. Every key in the Tools , Layers and Application tables can be changed in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts , and View \u203a Navigation switches the mouse navigation to Blender, Maya or ZBrush style (see The interface )."},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"viewport","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Viewport","x":"Action Input Paint with the current tool Left mouse Orbit Alt + left drag \u00b7 middle drag Pan Shift + middle drag \u00b7 Alt + Shift + left drag \u00b7 Alt + Shift + right drag Zoom Wheel \u00b7 Alt + right drag \u00b7 Ctrl + middle drag \u00b7 Alt + Ctrl + left drag Brush size / hardness [ ] \u00b7 Ctrl + wheel \u00b7 Ctrl + right drag (x = size, y = hardness) Straight line from the last stroke Shift +click Clone source Ctrl +click (Clone tool) Quick color pick Hold I and click \u00b7 Alt + right-click Erase with the Fill tool Alt +click Pen tablet Tip paints \u00b7 eraser end erases \u00b7 lower / upper barrel button: right / middle click (change in Preferences \u203a Pen tablet ) Topology lock / island lock on or off Alt + T / Alt + L Stencil: move / rotate / scale Hold T : left drag / Shift + left drag / wheel Stencil on or off Shift + T Split / compare view on or off \u00b7 take a snapshot \\ \u00b7 Shift + \\ (drag the divider to wipe) New note / edit a note Click the model / a pin with the Note tool ( N ); Ctrl + Enter saves Frame the model F Frame the selection (the model when nothing is selected; also the view pie, down right) Shift + F Rotate the lighting Shift + right drag Front / right / top (opposite with Ctrl ) Numpad 1 3 7 Orthographi"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"sliders","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Sliders","x":"Action Input Type a slider's value ( Enter keeps it, Esc goes back) Right-click the slider \u00b7 Ctrl +click Drag it / set it where you click Left drag / left click"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"tools","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Tools","x":"B Paint E Erase U Blur C Clone P Polygon fill I Pick S Select faces M Seams L Island brush J Smudge N Note X Swap colors Shift + X X mirror"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"island-brush-and-face-selection","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Island brush and face selection","x":"Action Input Paint the area that becomes an island / remove from it Left drag \u00b7 Ctrl + left drag (Island tool) Make the island Enter Select all faces / none (any tool, 3D view and UV editor) A (none when some are selected) / Alt + A Grow / shrink the selection Ctrl +Numpad + / Ctrl +Numpad - Unpin the selected islands Alt + P (also in the UV editor)"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"layers","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Layers","x":"Action Input New paint layer / group into a folder Ctrl + L / Ctrl + G Duplicate / delete Ctrl + D / Delete Make an instance (linked copy) Alt + D Solo a layer Alt +click its eye \u00b7 Alt + S for the selected layer Rename Double-click the name Move into a folder / reorder Drag onto the middle of a folder row / onto the top or bottom half of a row Everything else Right-click a layer"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"uv-editor","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"UV editor","x":"Action Input Select / add / remove Click \u00b7 Shift +click \u00b7 Ctrl +click Box select Drag on empty space Select all / none A (toggles) \u00b7 Alt + A none Move / rotate / scale Drag the selection (it starts moving after a few pixels, so a click never moves it) \u00b7 G / R / S ( Ctrl snaps) Context menu (every UV operation) Right-click Pin / unpin the selected islands P / Alt + P Frame the selection F Confirm / cancel a transform Click or Enter / right-click or Esc Pan / zoom Middle drag or Alt + left drag / wheel Reset the view Home"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"application","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Application","x":"Ctrl + O Open Ctrl + S Save Ctrl + Shift + S Save as Ctrl + E Export textures Ctrl + B Send to Blender Ctrl + L New paint layer Ctrl + Z Undo Ctrl + Shift + Z Redo F1 Shortcut sheet Ctrl + Alt + S Save incremental Ctrl + Shift + E Export again F12 Render image Ctrl + Shift + P Command palette Esc Close help / about / export / shortcuts and other windows Ctrl + Q Quit Tab Hide / show all panels"},{"p":"shortcuts.html","a":"command-line","page":"Shortcuts (Patina keymap)","t":"Command line","x":"patina [mesh.glb | .gltf | .fbx | .obj | .stl | .3mf | project.patina] --headless automation server without a window --mcp Model Context Protocol on stdin/stdout (Windows: patina-cli.exe) --setup-claude add Patina to the Claude desktop app's MCP servers, then exit --port N command server port (default 7860, or $PATINA_PORT) --no-server do not open the command server --resolution N default texture resolution (default 2048) --verbose debug logging --version Setting PATINA_FRAME_STATS to a file name makes the window write a line every 120 frames: the frame rate, the time each part of a frame takes on the processor, and the time the 3D view and the whole frame take on the graphics card. Useful when Patina feels slow on a machine. Setting PATINA_UI_ID_CHECK (to anything) makes Patina check each frame for two buttons or options in one window that Dear ImGui can't tell apart (they would fight over clicks), log each one, and list them for the ui_id_conflicts command. For testing Patina itself."},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Blender keymap reference","x":"Blender keymap reference If you paint in Blender, switch Patina to the Blender keymap : S samples a color, X flips colors, F resizes the brush, Shift+A adds, Shift+D duplicates, and the middle mouse button orbits. This page lists every key in that keymap. The default Patina keymap has its own page."},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"turning-it-on","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Turning it on","x":"Edit \u203a Preferences\u2026 Open Edit \u203a Preferences\u2026 Choose Blender keymap . The navigation switches to Blender's as well (middle mouse orbits, Shift + middle pans, Ctrl + middle zooms), and Hold Ctrl while painting to paint with the secondary color is turned on. Both can be changed back separately in the same window. The choice is saved and kept on the next start. F1 shows the keys of the active keymap. You can also switch from Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts or from the F1 window. Switching keymaps replaces any keys you changed yourself. Individual keys can still be changed afterward in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts ."},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"painting","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Painting","x":"The Blender column shows what the key does in Blender's texture paint mode. Where Blender has no key for an action, Patina's own key is kept, marked Patina . Action Key Blender Sample the color under the cursor S Same (Sample Color) Swap primary and secondary color X Same (Flip Colors) Paint with the secondary color Hold Ctrl while painting Same Brush size F , move the mouse, click (or Enter ) to confirm; right-click or Esc cancels Same (radial control) Brush opacity (Blender's strength) Shift + F , then the same Same Brush flow G , then the same Patina (Blender has no flow) Brush hardness Shift + G , then the same Patina Brush angle Ctrl + F , move the mouse round ( Shift : steps of 15 degrees) Same (Blender turns the brush texture) Brush smaller / bigger [ / ] Same Clone source Ctrl +click with the Clone tool Same (Ctrl+click sets the clone point) Straight line from the last stroke Shift +click Patina (Blender uses a Line stroke method) Toggle X mirror Shift + X Patina Topology lock: stay on the connected piece of mesh Alt + T Patina (Blender's face mask limits painting to selected faces instead) Island lock: stay inside the UV island Alt + L Patina Command palette: search everyt"},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"sliders","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Sliders","x":"Action Input Type a slider's value ( Enter keeps it, Esc goes back) Right-click the slider \u00b7 Ctrl +click Drag it / set it where you click Left drag / left click"},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"tools","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Tools","x":"Blender's paint tools have no single-key shortcuts, so the Patina keys stay, except where Blender uses the key for something else. B Paint E Erase C Clone P Polygon fill M Seams L Island brush J Smudge N Note Blur, Pick and Select faces: tool strip Blur has no key in the Blender keymap since 0.4, because U unwraps, as in Blender. Give it one in Edit \u203a Keyboard shortcuts if you use it a lot. Picking colors In the Blender keymap the Pick tool has no key, because S already samples straight away, without a click. Alt + right-click also picks with any tool."},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"uvs-and-face-selection","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"UVs and face selection","x":"Action Key Blender Island brush: paint the area, Ctrl removes L , then left drag Similar ( L selects linked faces) Make the painted area an island U Same idea ( U opens the Unwrap menu) Select all faces / none (any tool) A (none when some are selected) / Alt + A Same Grow / shrink the selection Ctrl +Numpad + / - Same (Select More / Less) Unpin the selected islands Alt + P Same (in Blender's UV editor)"},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"layers","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Layers","x":"Action Key Blender Add menu: paint, fill, fill with black mask, adjustment, folder, smart material Shift + A (opens at the mouse) Same (Add) Duplicate the selected layer Shift + D Same (Duplicate) Make an instance (linked copy) Alt + D Same (Duplicate Linked) Group into a folder Ctrl + G Same (Make Group) Solo the selected layer (toggle) Numpad / Same idea (Local View) Delete the selected layer Delete Same ( X is Flip Colors while painting) Solo any layer Alt +click its eye Patina (Blender's outliner uses Ctrl+click)"},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"view","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"View","x":"Action Key Blender Orbit / pan / zoom Middle drag / Shift + middle / Ctrl + middle or the wheel Same Frame the model Home Same (View All) Frame the selection (the model when nothing is selected; also the view pie, down right) Numpad . Same (View Selected) Front / right / top (opposite with Ctrl ) Numpad 1 3 7 Same Orthographic on/off Numpad 5 Same Snap to the nearest side view while orbiting Alt + middle drag, or hold Alt during one Same Back to your view (again: the side view) Numpad 0 Patina (Blender's Numpad 0 is the scene camera) View pie Hold Z Same idea (Blender's shading pie) Channel pie Hold Shift + Z Patina Edit mode pie (the tools) Hold ` Patina (Blender's ` is the view pie; its Ctrl + Tab is the mode pie) Quick Favorites Hold Q Same idea (Blender's Quick Favorites, as a pie) Hide all panels Ctrl + Space Same idea (Toggle Maximize Area) View modes 1 \u2013 9 Patina Shortcut window F1 Patina (Blender's F1 opens its manual) Split / compare view \u00b7 take a snapshot \\ \u00b7 Shift + \\ Patina"},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"files-and-editing","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Files and editing","x":"Ctrl + O Open Ctrl + S Save Ctrl + Shift + S Save as Ctrl + Z Undo Ctrl + Shift + Z Redo Ctrl + Q Quit Ctrl + Alt + S Save incremental (same as Blender) F12 Render image (same as Blender) Shift + right drag Rotate the lighting (Patina) Ctrl + E Export textures (Patina) Ctrl + Shift + E Export again (Patina) Ctrl + B Send to Blender (Patina) The UV editor keys ( G / R / S , A / Alt + A , P / Alt + P to pin and unpin, F to frame, click and box select) already follow Blender in both keymaps. See the UV editor table ."},{"p":"shortcuts-blender.html","a":"gotchas","page":"Shortcuts (Blender keymap)","t":"Gotchas","x":"S samples where the mouse is As in Blender, S picks the color under the cursor immediately, and the brush then paints that flat color instead of the library material. Click a material again to go back to it. F no longer frames the model In the Blender keymap F resizes the brush. Use Home to frame the model, and Numpad . or the view pie ( Z , down right) to frame the selection. Tab does not hide the panels In Blender Tab switches modes, so the Blender keymap uses Ctrl + Space for the distraction-free view. U no longer selects Blur In 0.3.1 U was the Blur tool in both keymaps. The Blender keymap now uses it to make an island, as Blender's Unwrap does. Saved Blender keymaps from 0.3.1 are updated on the first start, and keys you changed yourself are kept. Mixing The keymap and the mouse navigation are separate settings. For example, you can keep Patina's keys and use Blender navigation, or the other way round."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Translating Patina","x":"Translating Patina Patina's interface can be shown in any language. A translation is one text file: every text of the interface in English, each with its translation beside it. You don't need to know any programming to make one, and the tools translators already use (Poedit, Weblate, Crowdin, Lokalize) read and write it."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"choose","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Choosing a language","x":"Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Language lists English, the translations that come with Patina, and yours. Pick one and the interface changes at once. Patina remembers it. The list also has a test language . It shows every translatable text with accents and brackets, like [P\u00e5\u00ef\u00f1t\u00b7\u00b7] , a third longer than the English. Text that still shows plain English can't be translated yet. Longer text shows where a translation will need more room."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"start","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Starting a translation","x":"In Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Language , choose Make a new translation\u2026 . Type the language's code: two letters for the language, with a region after an underscore if it matters ( fr , de , es , pt_BR , zh_CN , ja ). Then its name in that language ( Fran\u00e7ais , Deutsch ). Choose Make it . Patina copies the template to <code>.po in your languages folder and opens the folder. An existing file is never replaced. Open the file in Poedit (free, for Windows, macOS and Linux) or any text editor, and translate. Save it, then choose your language in Preferences, or press Reload to see the changes while you work. Your languages folder is ~/.local/share/patina/user/languages on Linux and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Patina\\user\\languages on Windows. Open the languages folder in Preferences opens it. The template, patina.pot , is in the languages folder next to Patina's materials."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"file","page":"Translating Patina","t":"What the file looks like","x":"Each text is an entry: msgid is the English, msgstr is your translation. The #: line says where in Patina's code the text is, which helps when a word could mean two things. #: src/ui/Toolbars.cpp:120 msgid \"Paint\" msgstr \"Peindre\" #: src/ui/LanguageUI.cpp:66 #, c-format msgid \"%d of %d texts translated\" msgstr \"%d textes traduits sur %d\" An entry you leave empty stays English. Poedit shows how many are done and lets you jump to the next one."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"rules","page":"Translating Patina","t":"The rules","x":"Keep the % codes , in the same order: %d and %zu are whole numbers, %s is a text (such as a layer's name), %.1f is a number with one decimal, %% is a percent sign. You may move the words around them, but not swap two codes. A translation whose codes don't match the English is not used. Preferences says how many, and lists them when you rest the pointer on the warning. Keep the line breaks ( \\n ) in long tooltips, or put them where your sentence breaks well. Don't translate keys such as Ctrl + Z , Alt +click or Numpad 1: they are the names on the keyboard. Words around them are fine to translate. Fuzzy entries (Poedit's \"Needs work\") are not used, the way gettext does it. Save the file as UTF-8 (Poedit always does)."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"plurals","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Plurals","x":"Some texts change with a number: 1 face , 2 faces . They have a msgid_plural and one msgstr[n] for each plural form of your language: msgid \"%d translation is not used: its %% codes are not the same as the English.\" msgid_plural \"%d translations are not used: their %% codes are not the same as the English.\" msgstr[0] \"...\" msgstr[1] \"...\" How many forms there are, and which number takes which, is in the file's header line Plural-Forms . Patina fills it in for the common languages when it makes your file (French, Russian, Polish, Czech, Japanese, Chinese, Korean...). For other languages, the gettext manual lists them, and Poedit sets it from the language."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"context","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Words with two meanings","x":"A few short English words mean two things in Patina. Those entries have a msgctxt line that says which one it is (for example layer or tool ); translate each on its own."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"fonts","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Fonts and scripts","x":"Latin, Greek and Cyrillic letters work with the fonts Patina uses on every system. For Chinese, Japanese and Korean, Patina uses a system font that has the characters (Microsoft YaHei, Yu Gothic, Meiryo or Malgun Gothic on Windows; Droid Sans Fallback or Noto Sans CJK on Linux). To use another font, name it in the file's header: \"X-Patina-Font: NotoSansJP-Regular.otf\\n\" A name without a folder is looked for next to the .po file. Patina only loads the characters your translation uses, so a large font costs little. Not yet Right-to-left scripts (Arabic, Hebrew) and scripts that join or stack their letters (Hindi, Thai) don't show correctly yet: the interface toolkit draws each letter on its own."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"check","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Checking your work","x":"Reload in Preferences reads your file again after you save it. Save the untranslated text\u2026 writes the texts Patina showed since you chose the language and that have no translation yet, as <code>-untranslated.po beside your file. Translate those first: they are the ones people see. Look around with the language on: menus, tooltips (rest the pointer on things), the right-click menus and the tool windows. Watch for text that is cut off or too wide."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"update","page":"Translating Patina","t":"After a new Patina version","x":"New versions add texts and change some. The template that comes with each version has all of them. In Poedit, choose Translation \u203a Update from POT File\u2026 and pick patina.pot : the new texts are added, the changed ones are marked fuzzy for you to check, and the rest stay as you translated them. With gettext's tools it is msgmerge -U fr.po patina.pot ."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"english","page":"Translating Patina","t":"What stays in English","x":"Names : the materials, brushes, smart materials and recipes that come with Patina, and the names you give layers and files. Messages from commands : the console, the MCP tools, the HTTP API and their results, which scripts and agents read. An error message from such a command can show in an error window in English. The Assistant : its model works in English, so what you type to it and its examples stay English (its window is translated). This manual , Patina Web and the Blender add-on."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"share","page":"Translating Patina","t":"Sharing a translation","x":"A translation is the one .po file: send it to others, and they put it in their languages folder. To have it come with Patina, it goes in the languages folder of Patina's source."},{"p":"translating.html","a":"agents","page":"Translating Patina","t":"For scripts and agents","x":"language_list lists the languages and how much of the current one is translated, language_set switches the interface (the results of commands stay English), language_new starts a translation from the template, and language_untranslated lists (or saves) the texts shown without a translation. See the command reference ."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"","page":"Command reference","t":"Command reference","x":"Patina command reference Every command is available as an MCP tool ( patina --mcp ), over TCP as JSON lines ( {\"id\":1,\"cmd\":\"<name>\",\"args\":{...}} on 127.0.0.1:7860) and in the in-app Console ( <name> {json args} ). Generated by scripts/gen_commands_doc.py from the live command registry. Tool results that contain images ( render_view , render_uv , screenshot_window , get_texture , get_material with preview ) are returned to MCP clients as PNG image content. MCP-only tools: open_gui_window \u2014 when running headless, opens the desktop window with the current project and switches all further tools to drive it live; patina_guide {topic} \u2014 the guides (workflow, mesh, develop, export, batch, painting, parameters), also MCP resources patina://guide/<topic> . MCP prompts: finish_model, develop_textures, game_ready, batch_folder."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"ping","page":"Command reference","t":"ping","x":"Health check."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"get-state","page":"Command reference","t":"get_state","x":"Full state snapshot: mesh, layer stack, brush, paint material, tool, camera, view, undo, Blender bridge."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"get-log","page":"Command reference","t":"get_log","x":"Recent log lines (warnings, errors, info). argument type range, default description count integer 1 to 1000, default 50 Number of lines, the newest last"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"open-file","page":"Command reference","t":"open_file","x":"Opens a mesh (.obj .gltf .glb .fbx .stl .3mf .ply .ptmesh) or a Patina project (.patina). argument type range, default description path (required) string Absolute file path"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"open-mesh","page":"Command reference","t":"open_mesh","x":"Loads a mesh and starts a new texture set (or keeps layers when topology matches). argument type range, default description keep_layers boolean Keep painted layers if the mesh has the same topology path (required) string Mesh path (.obj .gltf .glb .fbx .stl .3mf .ply .ptmesh) resolution integer 0 to 8192, default 0 Texture set resolution per texture set / UDIM tile (512 to 8192, a power of two). 0 = the default (2048); a textured model's texture set grows to its textures' size unless this is given separate boolean (1.2) A file with several meshes (a character's body, clothes, eyes, hair): an object each, with its own layers (true), or one object (false). Default: an object each when there are at most 24"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"new-project","page":"Command reference","t":"new_project","x":"Starts a new project on a primitive mesh (sphere, cube, plane, torus). argument type range, default description primitive sphere \\ cube \\ plane \\ torus Primitive mesh resolution integer 512 to 8192 Texture set resolution: 512, 1024, 2048, 4096 or 8192 (default: the preference, 2048)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"save-project","page":"Command reference","t":"save_project","x":"Saves the project (.patina single file). argument type range, default description path string Destination; default: current project path"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"save-incremental","page":"Command reference","t":"save_incremental","x":"Saves the project as the next version next to it: name_v002.patina, _v003..."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-resolution","page":"Command reference","t":"set_resolution","x":"Changes the texture set resolution (layers are resampled). argument type range, default description resolution (required) integer 512 to 8192 Resolution per texture set / UDIM tile: 512, 1024, 2048, 4096 or 8192. Layers are resampled. Higher is sharper and takes more memory (a full paint layer: about 85 MB at 2K, 330 MB at 4K)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"texture-tiles","page":"Command reference","t":"texture_tiles","x":"Texture sets per material slot and / or UDIM tiles. Without 'mode' it reports the layout. mode: 'single' (one texture), 'material_slots' (a texture set per slot), 'udim' (a texture per UDIM tile) or 'material_slots_udim'. Paint moves with the layout; undo history is cleared. Every tile has the texture set resolution. argument type range, default description faces boolean Report each tile's face indices too mode single \\ material_slots \\ udim \\ material_slots_udim The layout"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"export-textures","page":"Command reference","t":"export_textures","x":"Exports the final texture maps as PNG files, or as layered PSD files (format psd: one per map, a layer per Patina layer with its mask, opacity and blend mode; a set per texture set / UDIM tile). Sizes above the texture set render in tiles at full detail (up to 32768). argument type range, default description base_name string Value of $mesh (default: mesh name) directory (required) string Output folder dry_run boolean (0.47) Write nothing: answer plan , the files it would write, each with what its channels hold, its bits, size and color space (srgb false: data, Blender's Non-Color) format png \\ psd png (default) or layered psd include_ao boolean Write the AO map when baked (default true) include_bent_normal boolean Write the bent normal map when baked (default true) include_thickness boolean Write the thickness map when baked (default true) layer integer 0 to 100000 Export only this layer / folder (its index), as if soloed mask boolean With layer: export that layer's painted mask as a grayscale PNG name_template string File name template, default \"$mesh_$map\". Tokens: $mesh $project $map $size $preset $layer objects active \\ all (1.2) A scene of several objects: the active object's "},{"p":"commands.html","a":"export-again","page":"Command reference","t":"export_again","x":"Exports the textures again with the settings of the last export (also stored in the project)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"reload-mesh","page":"Command reference","t":"reload_mesh","x":"Reloads the mesh file the project came from (after it was re-exported, e.g. from Blender), keeping the layers: paint is moved to changed UVs, or transferred through 3D when the topology changed. argument type range, default description path string Mesh file (default: the current mesh's file)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"import-model-textures","page":"Command reference","t":"import_model_textures","x":"Brings in the model's own materials (textures and colors of each material slot, and vertex colors, from a .glb, .gltf, .obj with its .mtl, .fbx or .ply) as a paint layer, From the model, at the bottom of the stack (one undo step). Opening a model does this by itself when it has textures, vertex colors or materials that are more than plain gray. (1.2.2) An OBJ or FBX color texture's alpha becomes the opacity channel when the file says nothing about opacity and the alpha cuts out (hair cards, leaves, lace). argument type range, default description color_alpha auto \\ opacity \\ ignore OBJ / FBX: the color texture's alpha as the opacity. auto (default): when it has see-through and solid parts (a cut-out); opacity: whenever the image has alpha; ignore: never (alpha that holds gloss, as in some Unity assets) path string The model file (default: the open model's file)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-materials","page":"Command reference","t":"list_materials","x":"Lists library materials (id, name, category, type). argument type range, default description category string Optional category filter"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"get-material","page":"Command reference","t":"get_material","x":"Full definition of a library material, optionally with a preview image. argument type range, default description id (required) string Material id preview boolean Include a 128px sphere preview"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"create-material","page":"Command reference","t":"create_material","x":"Creates or updates a user material in the library. type 'procedural' uses a generator (flat two_tone brushed_metal scratched_metal rust concrete wood stone leather fabric tiles plastic car_paint dirt chipped_paint carbon_fiber marble cells emissive_grid diamond_plate hammered corrugated woven bark terrazzo sand moss asphalt camo perforated panels bricks parquet hex_tiles scales grille quilted knit knurling stucco twill skin hair brush_strokes painted_stone painted_wood painted_metal stripes checks dots animal_print cracked_earth flagstone pebbles strata crackle chainmail damascus circuit stained_glass mosaic sequins paper fur feathers leaves rope damask ice crosshatch lace net hair_cards velvet) with params {color_a,color_b,color_c (sRGB 0-1 or #hex), roughness [a,b], metallic [a,b], scale, detail, seed, height, variation, normal_strength, extra[4], emissive, and for every generator warp, warp_scale (wobble), grime, grime_color (dirt in the low parts), wear, wear_color (worn raised edges), opacity, sheen, coat (clearcoat), subsurface (0-1 each), cutout (true: the holes of net, lace, leaves, hair cards, grilles... are see-through)}; list_generators says what each generator's colors "},{"p":"commands.html","a":"import-material-folder","page":"Command reference","t":"import_material_folder","x":"Imports a folder of PBR texture maps as library materials. Understands ambientCG, Poly Haven, Quixel and Substance export naming, packed maps (Unreal OcclusionRoughnessMetallic / ORM / ARM, glTF metallicRoughness, Unity MaskMap and MetallicSmoothness), OpenGL / DirectX normals, EXR, HDR and 16-bit maps. A Substance Painter export of several texture sets in one folder gives one material per texture set. .sbsar / .spsm files are not readable (Adobe's engine only): export their outputs as images first. argument type range, default description name string Material name (with several texture sets: the name prefix) normal auto \\ opengl \\ directx Normal map convention (auto: from the file name, or DirectX for Substance's Unreal preset) path (required) string Folder path"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"import-image-material","page":"Command reference","t":"import_image_material","x":"Imports a single image (logo, decal, photo) as a color material. Paint it with mapping 'screen' to project it from the view, or 'uv'. argument type range, default description path (required) string Image file (png, jpg, tga)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-paint-material","page":"Command reference","t":"set_paint_material","x":"Sets what the brush paints: a library material (id) and/or flat values, plus projection settings. argument type range, default description coat number 0 to 1 Flat clearcoat: a smooth varnish (lacquer, car paint) color array Flat sRGB color [r,g,b] 0-1 or '#rrggbb' emissive array Flat emissive sRGB color height number 0 to 1 Flat height: 0.5 neutral, above raises (bumps), below lowers (dents) height_scale number 0 to 4 Scales the height relief: 0 flat, 2 twice as deep id string Library material id; empty string = flat values mapping triplanar \\ uv \\ screen Material projection metallic number 0 to 1 Flat metallic: 0 non-metal, 1 metal (in between only along borders) metallic_override number -1 to 1 Forces the metallic value everywhere; -1 keeps the material's own normal_strength number 0 to 4 Scales the normal detail: 0 none, 2 twice as strong opacity number 0 to 1 Flat opacity: 1 opaque, 0 invisible (lace, cut-outs) rotation number -180 to 180 Turns the projected pattern, degrees roughness number 0 to 1 Flat roughness: 0 mirror-smooth, 1 fully matte roughness_offset number -1 to 1 Added to the material's roughness roughness_scale number 0 to 4 Multiplies the material's roughness: be"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"export-material-maps","page":"Command reference","t":"export_material_maps","x":"Bakes library materials and writes their tiling maps (<id>_color, _rmh [r rough, g metal, b height], _normal [OpenGL], _emissive, _opacity [see-through materials], _thumb) plus manifest.json (with each material's average values, sheen, clearcoat and subsurface among them). Used to build the web painter library and Blender's Patina asset library; handy for using Patina materials in other tools. argument type range, default description directory (required) string Output folder format png \\ jpg Color, normal and glow maps as PNG (default) or JPEG; the rmh map is always PNG height16 boolean Also write the height alone as a 16-bit PNG (<id>_height; smooth bump mapping) ids array Material ids (default: see which) quality integer 1 to 100, default 92 JPEG quality: lower is smaller and softer size integer 32 to 4096, default 512 Map size in pixels thumbnails boolean Also write sphere previews (default true) which builtin \\ user \\ all Without ids: the built-in materials (default), your own, or both"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-brushes","page":"Command reference","t":"list_brushes","x":"Lists brush presets: Patina's, saved ones, and (1.2) a brush for each image in the user's brushes folder (and its folders, a category each) and each tip of a Photoshop .abr file there (ids file:<path>)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-brush","page":"Command reference","t":"set_brush","x":"Selects a brush preset and/or overrides parameters. Sizes are in viewport pixels (diameter); for UV strokes in texels. argument type range, default description alpha string Tip shape: round square star splatter noise scratches grunge dots hatch cracks bristle spray leaf ring cloud rust angle number -180 to 180 Tip angle in degrees angle_falloff number 0 to 0.8 Fades the paint on surfaces seen at a grazing angle: 0 off, 0.1 to 0.6 typical angle_jitter number 0 to 1 Random tip rotation per dab: 1 = any angle backface_culling boolean Do not paint faces pointing away flow number 0.01 to 1 How much each dab adds: low flow builds up gradually within a stroke flow_jitter number 0 to 1 Random flow per dab follow_path boolean Rotate dabs along the stroke hardness number 0 to 1 Edge: 0 soft falloff, 1 hard edge lazy_radius number 0 to 100 Lazy mouse: the stroke trails the cursor by this many pixels, for smooth lines lock_face_set boolean (1.2) Lock Face Sets: a stroke only paints the face set it starts on lock_island boolean Island lock: a stroke only paints the UV island it starts on lock_set boolean Set lock: a stroke only paints the texture set (or material slot) it starts on lock_topolog"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"save-brush-preset","page":"Command reference","t":"save_brush_preset","x":"Saves the current brush settings as a user preset. argument type range, default description category string Category id (required) string Preset id name string Display name"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-tool","page":"Command reference","t":"set_tool","x":"Selects the active tool (in the window: also the tools that are not brushes, such as select, seams, island, note and the retopology tools). argument type range, default description blur_radius number 1 to 64, default 6 Blur tool radius in texels: larger smooths more fill_mode face \\ uv_island \\ connected \\ mesh \\ selection Polygon fill mode seam_aware boolean Blur and smudge reach across UV seams (default true) tool (required) paint \\ erase \\ fill \\ picker \\ blur \\ clone \\ smudge \\ select \\ seams \\ island \\ note \\ zone_lines \\ zones \\ flow \\ density Tool"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-paint-channels","page":"Command reference","t":"set_paint_channels","x":"Selects which material channels strokes write. Channel keys: basecolor, roughness, metallic, height, normal, emissive, opacity, sheen, coat (clearcoat), subsurface (or 'all') argument type range, default description channels (required) array Channel keys: basecolor, roughness, metallic, height, normal, emissive, opacity, sheen, coat (clearcoat), subsurface (or 'all') opacity object Optional per-channel opacity, e.g. {\"height\":0.5}"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-symmetry","page":"Command reference","t":"set_symmetry","x":"Symmetric painting through the object origin: any combination of X/Y/Z mirrors, plus radial symmetry (radial = number of copies around radial_axis; 1 = off). argument type range, default description radial integer 1 to 32 Radial copies around the symmetry axis: 1 = off, 8 for a wheel radial_axis x \\ y \\ z Axis for radial symmetry x boolean Mirror X y boolean Mirror Y z boolean Mirror Z"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-clone-source","page":"Command reference","t":"set_clone_source","x":"Sets the Clone tool source: 'uv' [u,v] or 'point' [x,y] (normalized viewport, picks the surface). Aligned (default) keeps the offset between strokes. argument type range, default description aligned boolean Keep the source offset across strokes point array [x,y] normalized viewport tile integer -1 to 1023, default -1 Texture sets / UDIM tiles: the tile of 'uv'; -1 = its UDIM, else 0 uv array [u,v] (with texture tiles: as authored, in 'tile')"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"paint-stroke","page":"Command reference","t":"paint_stroke","x":"Paints one stroke with the current brush/material/tool onto the selected layer (or its mask). Points are [x,y] or [x,y,pressure]. space 'normalized' = 0..1 of the 3D viewport (origin top-left), 'viewport' = pixels, 'uv' = texture coordinates (0..1, origin bottom-left; brush size in texels). Optional brush/material/tool objects are applied first (persistently). argument type range, default description brush object set_brush arguments material object set_paint_material arguments points (required) array List of [x,y] or [x,y,pressure] space normalized \\ viewport \\ uv Coordinate space (default normalized) tile integer -1 to 1023, default -1 space uv with texture sets / UDIM tiles: the tile (index from texture_tiles); -1 = the UDIM of the first point, else 0 tool paint \\ erase \\ blur \\ clone \\ smudge Tool for this stroke"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"paint-world-points","page":"Command reference","t":"paint_world_points","x":"Paints a stroke through 3D points (object space). Points are projected through the current camera; hidden points are skipped. argument type range, default description brush object set_brush arguments material object set_paint_material arguments points (required) array List of [x,y,z]"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"fill","page":"Command reference","t":"fill","x":"Polygon fill with the current material: mode face | uv_island | connected | mesh | selection. Target faces via 'faces', or 'point' [x,y] (normalized viewport) to pick a face. argument type range, default description erase boolean Erase the faces instead (like Alt+click with the Fill tool) faces array Face indices material object set_paint_material arguments mode face \\ uv_island \\ connected \\ mesh \\ selection \\ material Fill extent (material = every face of the clicked face's material slot) point array [x,y] normalized viewport position"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"pick","page":"Command reference","t":"pick","x":"Samples the composite (color, roughness, metallic, height) at a normalized viewport point. argument type range, default description apply boolean Use as flat paint values point (required) array [x,y] normalized"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-stencil","page":"Command reference","t":"set_stencil","x":"Stencil: a picture laid over the 3D view that strokes paint through (mode mask: white / opaque lets paint through) or whose colors they paint (mode decal). It stays fixed on screen while the camera moves. Give an image path, or text to make a text stencil. Returns the stencil state. argument type range, default description align left \\ center \\ right Alignment of text lines clear boolean Remove the stencil color array Text color, sRGB [r,g,b] (decal mode paints it) enabled boolean Strokes use the stencil fit boolean Center the stencil and fit it into the view font string Font: sans, sans-bold, serif, mono (bundled), a system font name (list_fonts) or a file path font_size number 16 to 512, default 128 Text line height in pixels of the stencil image invert boolean Paint where the stencil is dark / transparent instead letter_spacing number -64 to 256, default 0 Extra pixels between letters (negative tightens) line_spacing number 0.5 to 4, default 1.15 Line spacing factor mode mask \\ decal mask: the stencil only shapes the paint; decal: paints its colors outline number 0 to 64, default 0 Text outline width in pixels (0 = none) outline_color array Outline color, sRGB overlay_opacity nu"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"project-stencil","page":"Command reference","t":"project_stencil","x":"Paints the whole stencil onto the surface seen from the current camera in one step (decal projection), with the paint material and channels (decal mode: the stencil's colors). erase=true erases through the stencil instead. argument type range, default description erase boolean Erase through the stencil"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-fonts","page":"Command reference","t":"list_fonts","x":"Fonts for text stencils: the bundled ones, then fonts found on this computer."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-note","page":"Command reference","t":"add_note","x":"Pins a note (comment) to the model, saved in the project. Place it by a 3D position, or by a point in the view (normalized [x, y], 0,0 = top left) that hits the model. argument type range, default description author string Who wrote it (default: the user name) point array View point [x, y] in 0..1 (or pixels with space=viewport) position array Object-space point [x,y,z] (snapped to the surface) space normalized \\ viewport Coordinates of point (default normalized) text (required) string The note"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-notes","page":"Command reference","t":"list_notes","x":"The notes pinned to the model. argument type range, default description include_resolved boolean Also list resolved notes (default true)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"update-note","page":"Command reference","t":"update_note","x":"Edits a note: text, resolved, position. argument type range, default description id (required) integer 1 to 4294967295 Note id (from list_notes) position array Move it to the surface point nearest [x,y,z] resolved boolean Mark resolved / open text string New text"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"delete-note","page":"Command reference","t":"delete_note","x":"Removes a note. argument type range, default description id (required) integer 1 to 4294967295 Note id (from list_notes)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"go-to-note","page":"Command reference","t":"go_to_note","x":"Turns the camera to a note (the view it was written from). argument type range, default description id (required) integer 1 to 4294967295 Note id (from list_notes)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-compare","page":"Command reference","t":"set_compare","x":"Split / compare view: the left side of the 3D view shows a snapshot of the textures or another view mode, the right side the live result. snapshot=true takes the snapshot now. argument type range, default description enabled boolean Show the split view mode snapshot \\ view \\ high_poly Left side: the snapshot, another view mode, or (1.1) the high-poly model as it was before the last bake-down snapshot boolean Take a snapshot of the current textures split number 0 to 1 Divider position, 0 left edge to 1 right edge view string View mode for mode=view (basecolor, roughness, metallic, normal, height, ...)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"undo","page":"Command reference","t":"undo","x":"Undo the last operation."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"redo","page":"Command reference","t":"redo","x":"Redo."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"history","page":"Command reference","t":"history","x":"Undo history: every step with its label; 'position' = number of steps currently applied."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"history-jump","page":"Command reference","t":"history_jump","x":"Undoes or redoes until 'position' steps of the history are applied (0 = before all). argument type range, default description position (required) integer 0 to 1000000 Target position (from history; 0 = the start)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-layers","page":"Command reference","t":"list_layers","x":"Layer stack (index 0 = bottom)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"add_layer","x":"Adds a paint layer, or a fill layer covering the mesh with a material (optionally masked by a smart generator: edges, cavity, occlusion, gradient_y, noise). argument type range, default description adjust object Adjustment layer settings (any of them; the rest stay). Order: de-light, white balance, exposure, highlights / shadows in linear light, then vibrance, saturation, hue, brightness, contrast. At the defaults nothing changes.<br> brightness (0 to 3, default 1): Multiplies the color values (sRGB): 1.2 = 20% brighter. exposure is the gentler, linear-light control.<br> contrast (0 to 3, default 1): Spreads the colors away from (above 1) or toward (below 1) mid gray.<br> delight (0 to 1, default 0): Takes shadows baked into the color out (scans, generated models): divides the color by the ambient occlusion (floored at 0.25). 1 = fully: a texel with AO 0.5 becomes twice as bright. Bakes the AO if needed. Use texture_check before and after.<br> exposure (-5 to 5, default 0): In stops, in linear light as a camera: +1 = twice the light, -1 = half. The first control to fix a texture that is too dark or too bright overall.<br> height (-0.5 to 0.5, default 0): Added to the height (0.5 is"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"remove-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"remove_layer","x":"Deletes a layer (undoable). argument type range, default description index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"select-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"select_layer","x":"Selects the layer that strokes affect; edit_mask paints its mask instead. argument type range, default description edit_mask boolean Paint the layer mask index (required) integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"update-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"update_layer","x":"Changes layer properties: name, visible, opacity, blend (normal multiply screen overlay add subtract darken lighten add_signed soft_light), channels, material (fill), generator (smart mask). argument type range, default description adjust object Adjustment layer settings (any of them; the rest stay). Order: de-light, white balance, exposure, highlights / shadows in linear light, then vibrance, saturation, hue, brightness, contrast. At the defaults nothing changes.<br> brightness (0 to 3, default 1): Multiplies the color values (sRGB): 1.2 = 20% brighter. exposure is the gentler, linear-light control.<br> contrast (0 to 3, default 1): Spreads the colors away from (above 1) or toward (below 1) mid gray.<br> delight (0 to 1, default 0): Takes shadows baked into the color out (scans, generated models): divides the color by the ambient occlusion (floored at 0.25). 1 = fully: a texel with AO 0.5 becomes twice as bright. Bakes the AO if needed. Use texture_check before and after.<br> exposure (-5 to 5, default 0): In stops, in linear light as a camera: +1 = twice the light, -1 = half. The first control to fix a texture that is too dark or too bright overall.<br> height (-0.5 to 0.5, defau"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"move-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"move_layer","x":"Moves a layer in the stack. argument type range, default description from (required) integer 0 to 100000 Current index (0 = bottom) to (required) integer 0 to 100000 New index (0 = bottom)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"move-layer-into","page":"Command reference","t":"move_layer_into","x":"Moves a layer (with its contents if it is a folder) into a folder, or out to the top level with folder = -1. argument type range, default description folder (required) integer -1 to 100000 Folder index; -1 = top level index (required) integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"group-into-folder","page":"Command reference","t":"group_into_folder","x":"Puts a layer into a new folder that takes its place in the stack. argument type range, default description index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers). Default: the selected layer"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"duplicate-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"duplicate_layer","x":"Duplicates a layer. argument type range, default description index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"solo-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"solo_layer","x":"Shows only this layer (or folder) in the viewport and exports nothing else until turned off. index -1 = off. argument type range, default description index integer -1 to 100000, default -1 Layer index; -1 turns solo off"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"copy-layer-settings","page":"Command reference","t":"copy_layer_settings","x":"Copies opacity, blend mode, channels, smart mask and (between layers of the same type) the fill material or adjustment from one layer to others. argument type range, default description from (required) integer 0 to 100000 Source layer index to (required) array Target layer indices"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"clear-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"clear_layer","x":"Clears painted channels (and/or mask) of a layer. argument type range, default description channels array Channel keys: basecolor, roughness, metallic, height, normal, emissive, opacity, sheen, coat (clearcoat), subsurface (or 'all') index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers) mask boolean Remove the mask"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-layer-mask","page":"Command reference","t":"set_layer_mask","x":"Adds (filled with value) or removes a layer mask. argument type range, default description from_selection boolean Mask = the selected faces (white), replacing any mask index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers) remove boolean Remove the mask value number 0 to 1, default 1 Fill value of a new mask: 1 white (the layer shows), 0 black (hidden until painted)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-mask-effect","page":"Command reference","t":"add_mask_effect","x":"Adds an effect to a layer's mask effect stack (the simple mask is converted first: painted mask, then the smart mask multiplied). Returns the stack. argument type range, default description effect (required) object The effect, as in update_layer mask_effects index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers). Default: the selected layer position integer 0 to 1000 Where in the mask's effect stack (0 = the bottom); default: on top"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-instance","page":"Command reference","t":"add_instance","x":"Adds an instance of a layer right above it: the same content (paint, fill material or adjustment) with its own opacity, blend mode, channels and mask. Painting either one changes both. argument type range, default description index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers). Default: the selected layer"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"make-independent","page":"Command reference","t":"make_independent","x":"Turns an instance into an ordinary layer with a copy of the content. argument type range, default description index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom; see list_layers). Default: the selected layer"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-smart-materials","page":"Command reference","t":"list_smart_materials","x":"Smart materials (saved layer stacks) from the built-in set and your library."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"apply-smart-material","page":"Command reference","t":"apply_smart_material","x":"Adds a smart material above the selected layer (or into the selected folder), optionally limited to a region of the model (parts from mesh_parts) and a smart mask stack. argument type range, default description id (required) string Smart material id or name (see list_smart_materials), or a file path mask array Smart mask stack, multiplied together: cavity (creases, pores), edges (worn edges), occlusion (sheltered places), facing_up (where dust settles), facing_down, facing (direction), gradient_up (stronger toward the top), gradient_down, gradient (along direction), noise (breakup; kind clouds, cells, cracks, ridges or streaks), thin (thin parts), drips (streaks running down the sides), pattern (a library material's height or brightness: material, source). level = threshold 0..1 (higher = less coverage), contrast 0 soft .. 1 hard region object Where the layer shows: a part id from mesh_parts (slot:<name>, piece:<n>, group:<name>, region:head, region:body, selection), several parts, and parts to leave out<br> except : <br> part : <br> parts :"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"save-smart-material","page":"Command reference","t":"save_smart_material","x":"Saves a layer or folder (with everything in it) as a smart material that can be applied to any project. include_paint also stores painted textures and masks (they only fit meshes with the same UVs). argument type range, default description description string Short description include_paint boolean Store painted channels and masks too (default false) index integer 0 to 100000 Layer / folder index. Default: the selected one name string Name"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-camera","page":"Command reference","t":"set_camera","x":"Orbit camera: yaw/pitch (radians), distance, target [x,y,z], fov, ortho; view preset front back left right top bottom persp; frame=true frames the mesh. A side, top or bottom view remembers the view before it: view=previous goes back to that one (and a second time to the side view again), like Numpad 0 in the app. argument type range, default description distance number 0.001 to 1000000 Distance from the target, object units: smaller is closer fov number 0.05 to 2.5 Vertical field of view, radians: 0.6 normal, smaller flattens the perspective frame boolean Frame the mesh first ortho boolean Orthographic pitch number -1.55 to 1.55 Look from above (+) or below (-), radians target array [x,y,z] view front \\ back \\ left \\ right \\ top \\ bottom \\ persp \\ previous Preset yaw number -6.2832 to 6.2832 Turn round the model, radians: 0 front, 1.5708 a quarter turn"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-viewport-size","page":"Command reference","t":"set_viewport_size","x":"Size of the 3D viewport used for normalized stroke coordinates and renders (headless). argument type range, default description height (required) integer 16 to 16384 Pixels width (required) integer 16 to 16384 Pixels"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-view","page":"Command reference","t":"set_view","x":"Viewport display: mode, wireframe, seams. Checks among the modes: mesh_check (problems), pbr_range (colors no real material has), texel_density, uv_stretch, symmetry, thickness, bake_check (1.1: where the last bake-down found the high-poly only by the nearest point, amber, or guessed, red). argument type range, default description axis_gizmo boolean The X/Y/Z axis gizmo in the corner of the app's 3D view (not in renders) isolate_set boolean Draw the other texture sets as dim ghosts (texture set tools) mode material \\ basecolor \\ roughness \\ metallic \\ normal \\ height \\ emissive \\ ao \\ curvature \\ uv_checker \\ mask \\ world_normal \\ uv_islands \\ texel_density \\ uv_stretch \\ material_ids \\ thickness \\ bent_normal \\ symmetry \\ opacity \\ sheen \\ coat \\ subsurface \\ mesh_check \\ pbr_range \\ solid \\ path_traced \\ bake_check \\ face_sets View mode: material (lit), a single channel, a bake, or a check (mesh_check, pbr_range, texel_density, uv_stretch, symmetry) seams boolean Show UV seams texture_set integer -1 to 1023 The texture set to show or isolate (index from texture_tiles; -1 = all) wireframe boolean Wireframe overlay"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-environment","page":"Command reference","t":"set_environment","x":"Lighting: optional .hdr equirect path, intensity, rotation (degrees), exposure. argument type range, default description exposure number 0.05 to 10 Viewport exposure multiplier (display only: exports are not affected) hdr_path string Equirectangular .hdr intensity number 0 to 10 Strength of the lighting rotation number -360 to 360 Turns the lighting round the model, degrees studio boolean Back to procedural studio lighting"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"render-view","page":"Command reference","t":"render_view","x":"Renders the 3D view offscreen and returns a PNG (does not change the live window). Optional camera overrides and view mode. argument type range, default description camera object set_camera arguments, e.g. {view: \"front\", frame: true} height integer 16 to 4096, default 768 Image height in pixels mode material \\ basecolor \\ roughness \\ metallic \\ normal \\ height \\ emissive \\ ao \\ curvature \\ uv_checker \\ mask \\ world_normal \\ uv_islands \\ texel_density \\ uv_stretch \\ material_ids \\ thickness \\ bent_normal \\ symmetry \\ opacity \\ sheen \\ coat \\ subsurface \\ mesh_check \\ pbr_range \\ solid \\ path_traced \\ bake_check \\ face_sets View mode: material (lit), a single channel, a bake, or a check (mesh_check, pbr_range, texel_density, uv_stretch, symmetry) samples integer 1 to 4096, default 64 Path traced mode: samples per pixel (more: less noise, slower) width integer 16 to 4096, default 1024 Image width in pixels"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"render-image","page":"Command reference","t":"render_image","x":"Renders the 3D view (current camera, lighting and view mode, without overlays) to a PNG. Optional transparent background and turntable frames (a full turn, files name_0001.png ...). argument type range, default description height integer 16 to 16384, default 1080 Image height in pixels path (required) string PNG file path_traced boolean Path traced: real shadows, reflections and light bouncing between the model's parts, lit by the environment (a shadow on the ground with path_trace's ground). Slower; no supersampling needed samples integer 1 to 8192, default 256 Path traced: samples per pixel (more: less noise, slower) supersample integer 1 to 4, default 2 Rendered this many times larger and scaled down: smoother edges, slower transparent boolean Transparent background turntable_frames integer 0 to 720, default 0 Frames of a turntable round the model (0 = one image) width integer 16 to 16384, default 1920 Image width in pixels"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"screenshot-window","page":"Command reference","t":"screenshot_window","x":"Captures the live Patina window (what the user sees). In headless mode falls back to render_view. argument type range, default description max_width integer 64 to 8192, default 1600 Downscale to at most this width"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"render-uv","page":"Command reference","t":"render_uv","x":"Renders the UV layout over a texture: channel basecolor | roughness | metallic | height | normal | emissive | opacity | sheen | coat | subsurface | ao | curvature | checker. argument type range, default description channel string Texture under the wireframe distortion boolean Show area distortion heat map height integer 16 to 4096, default 1024 Image height in pixels tile integer 0 to 1023 Texture sets / UDIM tiles: which tile to show (default: the UV editor's) width integer 16 to 4096, default 1024 Image width in pixels"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"get-texture","page":"Command reference","t":"get_texture","x":"Returns a final texture map as PNG (basecolor roughness metallic height normal emissive opacity sheen coat subsurface ao). argument type range, default description channel (required) string Map size integer 0 to 8192, default 512 Downscale to this size in pixels (0 = full size) tile integer 0 to 1023, default 0 Texture sets / UDIM tiles: which tile (index from texture_tiles)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"texture-check","page":"Command reference","t":"texture_check","x":"Measures the finished textures (0.34), so an agent can tune an adjustment layer's develop settings by numbers: how much of the base color no real material has (non-metals darker than sRGB 30 or brighter than 240, metals darker than 180, metallic half-way: the PBR Range view), the base color's brightness (percentiles of its sRGB luminance) and color cast, roughness spread, and hints naming the setting to move. Run it, change a setting with update_layer, run it again. argument type range, default description tile integer -1 to 1023, default -1 Texture sets / UDIM tiles: only this one (index from texture_tiles); -1 = all"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"bake-mesh-maps","page":"Command reference","t":"bake_mesh_maps","x":"Bakes mesh maps: ambient occlusion (with bent normals), curvature (with the high-poly normal map's detail when one is baked) and thickness. Smart masks use them; AO, thickness and bent normals export too. argument type range, default description ao boolean Bake AO and bent normals (default true) ao_samples integer 4 to 1024, default 64 AO directions: 64 is fast, 256 smoother on complex models curvature boolean Bake curvature (default true) curvature_detail boolean Add the high-poly normal map's detail to the curvature (default true) thickness boolean Bake thickness (default false: it casts rays, so it takes longer) thickness_distance number -1 to 1000000, default -1 How deep thickness looks, object units; -1 = half the model's radius. Lower it to find thinness at a smaller scale thickness_rays integer 4 to 512, default 32 Rays per texel for thickness: more is smoother and slower"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"bake-from-high-poly","page":"Command reference","t":"bake_from_high_poly","x":"Bakes a high-poly mesh (sculpt) onto the low-poly one: tangent-space normal detail (becomes the base normal of the stack and is exported with it), ambient occlusion and material slots. Every texel is sampled at several points; each looks along the low-poly's normal both ways out to 'distance' (the nearest point of the sculpt when the rays find nothing). Both meshes must be in the same place. Returns the shares found along the normals (hit_fraction), by the nearest point and guessed. argument type range, default description ao boolean Bake ambient occlusion from the high-poly mesh (default true) ao_samples integer 8 to 1024, default 64 AO directions: more is smoother and slower clear boolean Remove the high-poly bake instead distance number -1 to 1000000, default -1 How far above and below the low-poly surface to look for the sculpt, object units; -1 = measured from the two meshes (1.1). Too small misses detail, too large catches the wrong side of thin parts material_ids boolean Take material slots from the high-poly mesh (default true) normal boolean Bake the normal map (default true) path string High-poly mesh file (.glb .gltf .fbx .obj .stl .3mf .ply) samples integer 1 to 16, def"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"baked-maps","page":"Command reference","t":"baked_maps","x":"(0.41) The baked ambient occlusion, curvature and thickness and their layers. Each baked map has a layer at the bottom of the stack (type baked): paint on it (select it, set_brush mask_value, paint_stroke) to fix the bake, the erase tool brings the bake back, blur / smudge / clone work on it; its mask effects (update_layer mask_effects: levels, blur, fill, noise...) adjust the bake with the fixes; strength scales the result. What it makes is the map smart masks, the 3D view (view modes ao, curvature, thickness) and exports read. (0.44.2) Its layer hidden (update_layer visible false) switches the map off: the 3D view no longer shades with the ambient occlusion and exports leave it out (shown false); smart masks keep the bake. Lists the maps (mean values: as baked, and now; out_of_date: baked for other UVs, so rebake); with map: select its layer, add it again after a delete, take its painted fixes away or its adjustments off, set its strength. argument type range, default description add boolean Give the map a layer again (after the layer was deleted) map ao \\ curvature \\ thickness The baked map rebake boolean (0.44.2) Bake again the maps that are out_of_date (made for other UVs: UV "},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-make-island","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_make_island","x":"Island brush: turns faces into one UV island. Cuts seams around them, unwraps (LSCM), orients it (upright = object up is texture up), scales it to the target texel density and places it in free UV space without moving other islands (repacking everything only if there is no room). Paint is kept. argument type range, default description density string auto (default) layout fit screen, or a number of texels per unit faces array Face indices margin number 0 to 0.1, default 0.005 Gap between islands in UV units: 0.005 = half a percent of the square. More avoids color bleeding between islands at small texture sizes; less uses the space better orient upright \\ longest_axis \\ none Island orientation (default upright) pin boolean Pin the new island(s) so later UV edits leave them in place (default false) repack_if_full boolean Repack all islands when there is no free space (default true) reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true) selection boolean Use the selected faces (default when no faces) symmetry array Mirror axes, e.g. [\"x\"]: the mirrored half gets the same (mirrored) UVs, stacked in one place; its faces are added when missing"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-density","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_density","x":"Texel density: targets (fit the texture, 1 texel per pixel on screen, the existing layout) and the selection's density; with set, scales the islands of the faces / selection to a density. With scale, the islands get that many times their texel density (2: twice the texture detail, 0.5: half) and the layout is packed again so they fit (pack: false leaves it), for example more texture space for the head. argument type range, default description faces array Faces whose islands to scale (default selection) pack boolean With scale: pack every island again so they fit (default true) reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true) scale number 0.25 to 4 Times the islands' texel density (0.25..4) selection boolean Use the selected faces set string auto layout fit screen, or texels per unit (omit to only read)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-orient","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_orient","x":"Orients UV islands: upright (object up = texture up) or along their longest side. (1.2.7) changed is false when the islands already were (nothing moved by a quarter of a texel): no undo step then. argument type range, default description faces array Faces (default selection, or all) mode (required) upright \\ longest_axis Orientation reproject boolean Keep paint (default true) selection boolean Use the selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-fix-overlaps","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_fix_overlaps","x":"Finds islands that overlap (paint would appear twice) and moves them into free UV space; repacks everything if there is no room. With check_only, just reports them. argument type range, default description check_only boolean Only report margin number 0 to 0.1, default 0.005 Gap between islands in UV units: 0.005 = half a percent of the square. More avoids color bleeding between islands at small texture sizes; less uses the space better reproject boolean Keep paint (default true)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-unwrap","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_unwrap","x":"Unwraps UVs: lscm (respects seams), smart (angle-based charts), box, planar (view direction), cylinder (unrolled around an axis, ends planar; for limbs, barrels, tubes), sphere (longitude / latitude). Painted layers are reprojected onto the new layout unless reproject=false. argument type range, default description angle number 1 to 89, default 66 Smart project: faces bending more than this many degrees start a new island. Lower: more, flatter islands; higher: fewer, more stretched ones axis auto \\ x \\ y \\ z Cylinder / sphere axis (default auto: the longest extent; sphere: y) faces array Only these faces (default all) margin number 0 to 0.1, default 0.005 Gap between islands in UV units: 0.005 = half a percent of the square. More avoids color bleeding between islands at small texture sizes; less uses the space better method (required) lscm \\ smart \\ box \\ planar \\ cylinder \\ sphere Unwrap method reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true) selection boolean Only selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-pack","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_pack","x":"Packs UV islands into 0..1 (optionally rotating and equalizing texel density). Islands are packed by their outlines, nesting into each other's gaps (quality \"tight\", default), or by bounding boxes (\"fast\"); with \"tight\" the better of the two is kept. argument type range, default description faces array Only islands of these faces free_space boolean Place only the islands of faces / selection into free space; others stay margin number 0 to 0.1, default 0.005 Gap between islands in UV units: 0.005 = half a percent of the square. More avoids color bleeding between islands at small texture sizes; less uses the space better normalize_density boolean Equal texel density quality tight \\ fast tight: island outlines (default); fast: bounding boxes reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true) rotate boolean Allow rotation"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-rectify","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_rectify","x":"Straightens islands made of quads (strips and grids: belts, pipes, trims, planks) into rectangles on an axis-aligned grid, each quad as long and wide as on the model. Islands with triangles or n-gons, or that are not a clean grid, are left alone. Paint is kept. argument type range, default description faces array Faces whose islands to straighten (default selection, or all) reproject boolean Keep paint (default true) selection boolean Use the selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-mirror","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_mirror","x":"Mirrors UVs across the model's symmetry: faces on the negative side of the axes take the (mirrored) UVs of their mirror image on the positive side, so both halves share one place in the texture. argument type range, default description axes array Mirror axes, e.g. [\"x\"] (default x) faces array Only these faces (either side; default all) reproject boolean Keep paint (default true; the negative half then shows the positive half's paint) selection boolean Use the selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-pin","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_pin","x":"Pins (or unpins) UV islands: pinned islands keep their UVs through every UV edit (moving, unwrapping, packing, fixing overlaps, the Island brush); packing and unwrapping place other islands around them. argument type range, default description all boolean Every island faces array Faces whose islands to pin (default: the selection) islands boolean Whole islands and their stacked mirror halves (default true) pin boolean true pins (default), false unpins selection boolean Use the selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-restack","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_restack","x":"Puts mirrored halves (from symmetric islands or Mirror UVs) back exactly on the half they copy, if they were moved apart. argument type range, default description reproject boolean Keep paint (default true)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-unstack","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_unstack","x":"Makes mirrored halves independent: the copies among the faces (or the copies of the faces) stop following the other half and move into free UV space, so each side can be painted on its own. argument type range, default description faces array Faces (default: the selection) reproject boolean Keep paint (default true) selection boolean Use the selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-islands","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_islands","x":"Lists the UV islands: face count, UV bounds [u0, v0, u1, v1], pinned, and mirrored halves (an island that is the stacked copy of another, or that has one stacked on it). With faces, only the islands of those faces. argument type range, default description faces array Only the islands of these faces limit integer 1 to 100000, default 1000 At most this many islands in the result selection boolean Only the selected faces' islands"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-transform","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_transform","x":"Moves/rotates/scales the UVs of faces (or whole islands containing them). Paint follows (reprojected). argument type range, default description faces array Face indices islands boolean Expand to whole UV islands pivot array [u,v] (default bounds center) reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true) rotate number -360 to 360 Turns the islands, degrees counter-clockwise scale array [su,sv] or number selection boolean Use selected faces translate array [du,dv]"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-seams","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_seams","x":"Seam editing: from_islands, by_angle, clear, mark or unmark edges (edge indices), or path: a seam along the shortest chain of edges through the given vertices or points (like the Seams brush; vertices at one position count as one). argument type range, default description angle number 1 to 179, default 60 by_angle: edges sharper than this many degrees become seams edges array Edge indices for mark/unmark mode (required) from_islands \\ by_angle \\ clear \\ mark \\ unmark \\ path Operation points array path: [x,y,z] model-space points (nearest vertex each), in order unmark boolean path: clear the seam along the path instead vertices array path: mesh vertex indices to pass through, in order"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-info","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_info","x":"UV statistics: islands, coverage of 0..1, overlaps, texel density, seam count, pinned faces, mirrored (stacked) faces and how many of those have drifted off their other half, and faces with no UV area (collapsed onto a point or a line: they get no paint)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"select-faces","page":"Command reference","t":"select_faces","x":"Face selection for UV tools, the island brush and fills: set, add, remove, clear, all, invert, by island, by material slot, grow or shrink. (0.45) visible: of the faces this selects (or, with remove, takes off), only those the camera sees (set_camera; turned toward it, on screen, not behind other faces), like the Select tool's Visible only: an island that wraps inside the model, or a skin under clothes, gives just what is seen. (0.47) no_uv_area adds the faces whose UVs collapsed onto a point or a line (they get no paint; uv_info counts them): unwrap them with uv_unwrap selection. (1.2.7) mirror adds the mirror images of the faces (default: of the selection) across the model's symmetry (axes, default x), and the halves stacked on them: one half of a face's UVs selects the other half, side by side or stacked. argument type range, default description axes array mirror: the symmetry axes, e.g. [\"x\"] (default x) faces array Face indices material integer 0 to 255 Material slot (mode material; adds to the selection) mode (required) set \\ add \\ remove \\ clear \\ all \\ invert \\ island \\ material \\ grow \\ shrink \\ no_uv_area \\ mirror Operation visible boolean default false Only the faces the"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"island-brush","page":"Command reference","t":"island_brush","x":"One dab of the Island brush at a normalized viewport point: the faces it takes join the selection (remove: leave it), with their mirror images under set_symmetry. reach surface (default): the faces within the brush's radius along the surface from the face under the point, so never a layer behind (a cloth with thickness) or a part only near in space, whatever the angle; screen: the faces seen inside the circle. Faces turned away from the camera are left out either way. One undo step. Then uv_make_island. argument type range, default description list boolean Also return the indices of the faces the dab took point (required) array [x,y] normalized viewport point (0..1, y down) reach surface \\ screen What a dab takes (default surface) remove boolean Take the faces out of the selection instead size number 1 to 2000 Brush diameter in viewport pixels (default: the brush size)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"pick-face","page":"Command reference","t":"pick_face","x":"Returns the face/UV/position under a normalized viewport point. argument type range, default description point (required) array [x,y] normalized"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"send-to-blender","page":"Command reference","t":"send_to_blender","x":"Exports textures (and edited UVs) and tells the Blender add-on to apply them to the linked object. argument type range, default description auto_push boolean Also push automatically after each change objects all \\ active (1.2) Every object of the scene linked to Blender (default), or the active one"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"send-model-to-blender","page":"Command reference","t":"send_model_to_blender","x":"Sends the model as it is in Patina (mended, with its UVs, material slots, seams and vertex groups) to Blender as an object with its textures: a new object (name, default <object>_patina) next to the linked one, in its place and collections, or with replace the linked object's own mesh. Patina links to that object from then on. Needs Blender's live link and the Patina Bridge add-on 0.9 or newer. argument type range, default description name string The new object's name (default: the linked object's name + _patina) port integer 0 to 65535, default 0 Blender's live link port; 0 = the linked object's, else 7861 replace boolean Replace the linked object's mesh instead (its modifiers stay)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"blender-material-library","page":"Command reference","t":"blender_material_library","x":"Puts Patina's material library in Blender's Asset Browser. Blender (Patina Bridge add-on 0.8 or newer, live link on) has Patina bake every material and builds a library of ready materials in Documents/Patina Materials (or the add-on's folder setting), added to its asset libraries. Run it again to update it. argument type range, default description mapping box \\ uv box (default): projected like Patina, no UVs needed; uv: the object's UV map port integer 1 to 65535 Blender's live link port (default: the linked object's, else 7861) size integer 512 to 2048, default 1024 Map size: 512, 1024 or 2048. Bigger is sharper, slower to build and larger on disk which all \\ builtin Patina's materials and your own (default), or Patina's only"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-check","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_check","x":"The Mesh Check: what the model is made of (vertices, faces, triangles, parts, material slots, UV islands, size in Blender's axes, area, volume, closed or not, UV coverage) and what is wrong with it: holes, non-manifold edges, bowtie vertices, flipped faces, inside-out parts, degenerate and duplicate faces, open seams (vertices at one place, not joined), loose vertices, specks and floaters, faces without UV space, overlapping UVs, odd scale. Each problem says how many, where (Blender axes) and which mesh_fix mends it. show: the Mesh Check heat map in the viewport; select: select a problem's faces. argument type range, default description faces boolean default false List each problem's faces (up to 20000 each) select string Select the faces of this problem (its id from the list), e.g. to mask or delete them show boolean default false Switch the viewport to the Mesh Check heat map (red problem, orange warning, blue note)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-fix","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_fix","x":"Mends the mesh (see mesh_check): weld (join vertices closer than weld_distance), remove_degenerate, remove_duplicate_faces, remove_loose, remove_small_parts (smaller than small_part_size, default 3% of the model), fix_winding (faces like their neighbors, closed parts facing out), fill_holes (up to fill_hole_edges edges, default 64; the new faces take their border's UVs), or all. The paint moves to the new mesh. It clears the undo history, so a copy of the project is kept first: mesh_fix_revert goes back. argument type range, default description all boolean default false Every fix below, with its default settings (the usual first step for a downloaded or generated model) fill_hole_edges integer 0 to 100000, default 0 Only holes with up to this many edges. 0 = automatic (64). Raise it for big openings (to print: 512); real openings (a cup's top) also close fill_holes boolean default false Close holes with a face (the new faces take their border's UVs) fix_winding boolean default false Turn faces like their neighbors; closed parts face out remove_degenerate boolean default false Remove faces with no area remove_duplicate_faces boolean default false Remove a face on the same corners as"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-fix-revert","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_fix_revert","x":"Goes back to the project as it was before the last mesh_fix, mesh_reduce or mesh_transform (mesh, layers and paint)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-reduce","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_reduce","x":"Fewer polygons (decimation): keeps ratio of the triangles (or triangles), the UV layout (seams, material borders and every corner's UV stay, so the textures fit as they are) and, with keep_borders (default), open borders. preview: only say how many triangles it would leave. The undo history is cleared, so a copy of the project is kept first: mesh_fix_revert goes back. argument type range, default description keep_borders boolean default true Open borders (a cloth's edge, a cut) don't move max_error number 0 to 1, default 1 Stop early rather than let the shape move more than this share of its size: 0.01 = 1%. 1 = always reach the target preview boolean default false Only say how many triangles it would leave and the error, without changing the model ratio number 0.001 to 1, default 0.5 Share of the triangles to keep: 0.5 halves them, 0.1 keeps a tenth. Lower is lighter but rounder shapes go faceted; the result's error says how far the shape moved triangles integer 0 to 100000000 Keep this many triangles instead of a ratio"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-transform","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_transform","x":"Sizes, turns and places the model: scale (or from_units: the units it was made in, cm, mm, in or ft, to Blender's meters), rotate [x, y, z] in degrees around Blender's axes, origin (keep, center: the middle of its box, base: the middle of its bottom) and ground (the lowest point at height 0). The faces and UVs stay, so the paint does too. A copy of the project is kept first: mesh_fix_revert goes back. argument type range, default description from_units m \\ cm \\ mm \\ in \\ ft The units it was made in; it is scaled to Blender's meters (cm: x0.01). Default m (no change) ground boolean default false Move it so its lowest point is at height 0 origin keep \\ center \\ base Where the origin goes: center = the middle of its box, base = the middle of its bottom (for props that stand on the floor). Default keep rotate array [x, y, z] degrees around Blender's axes, applied in that order: [90, 0, 0] stands up a model lying on its back scale number 1e-06 to 1000000, default 1 Multiply its size (after from_units)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-collision","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_collision","x":"Makes collision shapes for game engines and shows them over the model (green): box (one oriented box), boxes (a box per separate part), hull (one convex hull), parts (a hull per separate part) or pieces (the model split into convex pieces where one hull would hold too much empty space). export_model with collision writes them after the model. clear removes them; show switches the view. argument type range, default description clear boolean default false Remove them concavity number 0 to 1, default 0.05 Pieces: a piece is split again while its hull holds more than this share of empty space. Lower: more, closer pieces (0.02); higher: fewer, rougher ones (0.15) max_shapes integer 1 to 256, default 16 Most shapes (boxes, parts, pieces). More fits concave models closer but costs the physics engine more; 4-16 is usual for game props max_vertices integer 8 to 255, default 32 Most corners per hull. Fewer are cheaper and a little smaller than the model; engines like 32 or fewer (PhysX allows 255) mode box \\ boxes \\ hull \\ parts \\ pieces box: one oriented box (crates, simple props; cheapest). boxes: a box per separate part. hull: one convex hull (rocks, bottles, round props). parts: a hull p"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-lightmap-uvs","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_lightmap_uvs","x":"Makes a second UV map for baked lighting (Unity, Unreal and Godot lightmaps): every face gets space of its own (mirrored and stacked islands too), the same texel density everywhere, and a gap of padding texels at the lightmap's size. It is saved with the project and exported as the glTF's second UV map (TEXCOORD_1); Blender gets it as the UV map Lightmap. The paint's own UVs do not change. A fix or a reduction drops it (make it again); a transform keeps it. argument type range, default description clear boolean default false Remove the lightmap UVs lightmap_size integer 32 to 8192, default 512 The lightmap resolution the engine will bake at (128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048). The padding is measured at this size: a smaller lightmap needs more room between islands padding number 0 to 64, default 4 Texels between islands (and from the border) at lightmap_size. Too little lets light bleed between islands; 2 to 4 is usual source uv \\ auto uv: the model's own UV islands, repacked (keeps their shapes; best for models with clean UVs; islands that fold are unwrapped again). auto: new charts made for lightmaps by xatlas (for models whose UVs are missing, stretched or a mess, such as scans and gene"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"export-model","page":"Command reference","t":"export_model","x":"Exports the model with its textures: .glb (one file, textures inside), .gltf (with a .bin and PNG files) or .obj (with a .mtl and PNG files). A material per texture set / UDIM tile; glTF gets base color (with opacity as alpha: MASK or BLEND), metallic-roughness, normal, emissive, occlusion when baked, and the KHR_materials_sheen / KHR_materials_clearcoat extensions when used. lods adds levels of detail that share the textures: glTF nodes name_LOD0, name_LOD1... (with MSFT_lod on LOD0), or name_LOD1.obj... beside the .obj. .stl: 3D printing (mm, Z up, no textures); .ply: the base color as sRGB vertex colors, with UVs and normals (Z up, meters, faces as they are). argument type range, default description collision none \\ unreal \\ godot \\ plain Collision shapes after the model (glTF, OBJ): the ones made with mesh_collision, else made now; named UCX_ / UBX_ (Unreal), -convcolonly (Godot) or _collision_ lods array Levels of detail besides the model: the share of its triangles each keeps, e.g. [0.5, 0.25, 0.125]; they share the textures (glTF nodes name_LOD0, name_LOD1... with MSFT_lod; OBJ files name_LOD1.obj...) objects all \\ active (1.2) A scene of several objects: every object shown "},{"p":"commands.html","a":"recipe-list","page":"Command reference","t":"recipe_list","x":"Recipes (develop presets): the built-in ones and yours, each with its steps."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"recipe-save","page":"Command reference","t":"recipe_save","x":"Saves a recipe (a list of Patina commands, steps: [{cmd, args}], run in order) among your recipes. Strings may use ${name} (the model's name), ${file}, ${dir} (its folder) and ${out} (the output folder). An id of a built-in recipe saves your own version of it. argument type range, default description description string What it is for id string Id (default from the name) name (required) string Name steps (required) array [{cmd, args}]"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"recipe-delete","page":"Command reference","t":"recipe_delete","x":"Deletes one of your recipes. argument type range, default description id (required) string Recipe id"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"recipe-apply","page":"Command reference","t":"recipe_apply","x":"Runs a recipe's steps on the open model, in order (a step that fails stops it). output: the folder ${out} stands for (default: the model's folder). argument type range, default description id string Recipe id output string The output folder (${out}) steps array Or these steps"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"batch-run","page":"Command reference","t":"batch_run","x":"Runs a recipe on many model files in a hidden Patina (the open project and the files themselves are not changed): each file is opened, the recipe's steps run, and the results go to output (${out}; default a patina_out folder beside the first file). files: model files; folders: every model in them (recursive: their subfolders too). A file whose step fails is left and the next goes on. batch_status follows it. argument type range, default description files array Model files folders array Folders of models output string Output folder recipe string Recipe id recursive boolean Folders' subfolders too resolution integer 512 to 8192, default 2048 Texture set size for models opened without a project (a textured model may grow to its textures' size) steps array Or these steps"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"batch-status","page":"Command reference","t":"batch_status","x":"The running (or last) batch: how many files are done, each file's result (ok, the error, its steps), and the report when it has finished."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"batch-cancel","page":"Command reference","t":"batch_cancel","x":"Stops the running batch after the file it is on."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-density","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_density","x":"Retopology: the density of quads. quads_per_m2 sets it for the whole mesh (model units are meters); value multiplies it on the given faces (2: twice the quads there, 0.5: half). argument type range, default description faces array Face indices part string A part from mesh_parts (region:head, slot:<name>, piece:<n>, group:<name>) quads_per_m2 number 1 to 10000000 Quads per square meter for the whole mesh selection boolean The selected faces value number 0.05 to 50 A multiplier on the given faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-zone-line","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_zone_line","x":"Retopology: a zone line on the surface through the given points (zones are the parts of the model the lines and the painted patches split it into; each line is an edge loop, where the flow changes). With clear, the lines near the points are taken away; clear_all removes every zone line. One undo step. argument type range, default description clear boolean Take away the lines near the points instead clear_all boolean Remove every zone line loop boolean The last point joins the first mirror string Mirror it across the model's middle on these axes (\"x\", \"xy\"...); within the deadzone of the middle it joins its mirror image points array [[x,y,z], ...] on (or near) the surface, in order"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-zones","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_zones","x":"Retopology: the zones (parts of the model between the zone lines and painted patches' borders) with their size and resolution."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-zone","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_zone","x":"Retopology: a zone's resolution, a multiplier on the quads per m\u00b2 there (2: twice as many quads). The zone is given by its number (retopo_zones), a face, or a point on it. argument type range, default description face integer 0 to 100000000 A face in the zone point array [x,y,z] on the zone resolution number 0.05 to 50 Multiplier on the quads per m\u00b2 zone integer 0 to 100000 The zone (from retopo_zones)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-stroke","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_stroke","x":"Retopology: a stroke that sets which way the flow runs where it is drawn (the quads follow it), or clear them all. With edge, an edge loop runs exactly along it. argument type range, default description clear boolean Remove every stroke edge boolean An edge loop runs exactly along it (default: it only sets the flow's direction) loop boolean The last point joins the first mirror string Mirror it across the model's middle on these axes (\"x\", \"xy\"...); within the deadzone of the middle it joins its mirror image points array [[x,y,z], ...] on the surface, in order"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-build","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_build","x":"Retopology: makes the quads at the density set (retopo_density) on the surface of the open mesh: along a smooth flow over the whole model, with an edge loop along each zone line (retopo_zone_line) and painted patch border, turned by the flow strokes (retopo_stroke), each zone at its own resolution (retopo_zone) and joined to its neighbors by rows of 3-to-1 steps (method flow); or filling each painted patch on its own (method patches). Returns the quad count, the share of vertices with four edges and anything left out. argument type range, default description fresh boolean Make all the quads again (default: only those near what changed since the last ones; the rest kept as they were) method flow \\ patches \\ layout flow: quads along the model's shape, zone lines as edge loops (default); patches: each painted patch filled on its own; layout: (in progress) a quad layout of the lines, its patches' counts solved at once relax integer 0 to 500, default 30 Smoothing passes of the quads' inner vertices (patches method): more evens the quads out, too many pulls them off sharp features"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-info","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_info","x":"Retopology: the patches, the density, the lines and strokes, and the last result. quads_asked: about how many quads the settings ask for (zones and density counted); over 150,000 the live preview doesn't start (preview_too_big) and only retopo_build makes them. argument type range, default description line_points boolean Also every zone line's points"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-view","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_view","x":"Retopology: what the viewport shows: the zones (in color, with their lines) or the painted density over the model (or neither), and the quads (as lines over the model, or alone). In the app's 3D view they show while preview is on (the top bar's Retopology preview button) or a retopology tool is chosen; render_view shows them either way. argument type range, default description lines_behind shown \\ darkened \\ hidden Zone lines and flow strokes behind the model: shown through it, darkened, or hidden loop_at array Light up the edge loop nearest this point of the model (with loops on) loops boolean Show the quads' poles, and light up the edge loop under the pointer marks boolean Show the red crosses over the quads: parts too thin for them (left out), tight spots patched where zones meet, and (with mirror) where the two sides of the model differ. The build's problems say which. Default true preview boolean The app's Retopology preview: show these in its 3D view whatever tool is chosen, with a quick set of quads following each change quads boolean Show the quads of the last build quads_only boolean Show the quads alone, shaded, instead of the model show zones \\ density \\ none \\ patches O"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-export","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_export","x":"Retopology: writes the quads (the last result) to an .obj file. argument type range, default description groups boolean A group (g) for each patch path (required) string The .obj file"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-send-to-blender","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_send_to_blender","x":"Retopology: sends the quads (the last result) to Blender as a new object next to the linked one (name_retopo), in the same place."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-use-as-model","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_use_as_model","x":"Retopology: the quads (the last result) become the model you paint. They are saved as an .obj next to the high-poly (name_retopo.obj), opened with new UVs, and the high-poly is baked onto them (normals, ambient occlusion). (1.1) The layers come along (bake_down: paint and masks resampled onto the quads' UVs; a new project); keep_layers false starts them afresh. argument type range, default description bake boolean Bake the high-poly onto the quads (default true) distance number -1 to 1000000, default -1 (1.1) The bake-down's distance along the normals, object units; -1: measured keep_layers boolean (1.1) Bake the layers down onto the quads (default true; off when the bake-down feature is) path string Where to save the quads (default next to the high-poly) resolution integer 256 to 8192 (1.1) The bake-down's texture size per texture set (default the high-poly's) samples integer 1 to 16, default 9 (1.1) The bake-down's points per texel: 1, 4, 9 or 16"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-clear","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_clear","x":"Retopology: removes the patches, the painted density and the result. argument type range, default description keep_density boolean Keep the painted density"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-patch","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_patch","x":"Retopology: makes faces of the open (high-poly) mesh a patch, to be filled with quads. Without a label the faces become a new patch; label 0 takes them out of any patch. Patches with the same label that don't touch are separate patches. Where patches meet are the low-poly's main edge loops; where three meet is a corner. argument type range, default description faces array Face indices label integer 0 to 100000 The patch (default: a new one); 0 removes the faces from their patches part string A part from mesh_parts (region:head, slot:<name>, piece:<n>, group:<name>) selection boolean The selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-paint","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_paint","x":"Retopology: paints along a stroke on the open (high-poly) mesh, like the brushes: a patch (label, or a new one), erase (patch 0), or density (value multiplies the quads per m\u00b2 there). Each point paints the faces within radius of it, across the surface. argument type range, default description label integer 0 to 100000 The patch (default: a new one) mode patch \\ erase \\ density What to paint (default patch) points (required) array Stroke points [[x,y,z], ...] on or near the surface (model units) radius (required) number 0.0001 to 100 Brush radius (model units) value number 0.05 to 50 Density multiplier for mode density"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-auto-patches","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_auto_patches","x":"Retopology: covers the mesh (or a part, or the selection) with patches of about the same size to start from (geodesic cells around evenly spread points). Replaces the patches there. argument type range, default description count integer 1 to 2000, default 24 How many patches: more gives smaller patches that follow detail closely; fewer gives broad ones faces array Face indices part string A part from mesh_parts (region:head, slot:<name>, piece:<n>, group:<name>) selection boolean The selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-fill-gaps","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_fill_gaps","x":"Retopology: the unpainted areas of the mesh become patches: a small area one patch, a large one several of about the size of the painted patches (cells that aren't disks are split)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-flow","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_flow","x":"Retopology, for testing: makes the quads as retopo_build does, with settings to try (seed, working shell, field smoothing, Patina's own field). Method trace (experimental) instead traces patches along a smooth field from its singularities, replacing the painted patches, and fills them. argument type range, default description build boolean Make the quads too (default true) curvature number 0 to 10 Trace method: how much the surface's bends steer the flow (default 0.3) field_smoothing integer -1 to 200, default -1 Passes of smoothing the direction field sees: more ignores small detail and gives calmer flow; -1 = about a quad lines_obj string Write the traced lines and singularities to this .obj (for checking) method quads \\ trace quads: one field over the whole model (default); trace: patches traced along it, filled one by one seed integer 0 to 2147483647, default 0 Random seed for the quads method: another seed gives another, equally valid layout shell boolean Work on a voxel shell of the model (default: when it is big or messy)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-symmetry","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_symmetry","x":"Retopology: the symmetry used with the Sym buttons (and the mirror option of retopo_zone_line and retopo_stroke): the planes go through the model's middle; points within the deadzone of a plane count as on it, so lines ending there join their mirror images. argument type range, default description deadzone number -1 to 1000000, default -1 Model units: vertices this close to the mirror plane are put on it; negative = 1% of the model's size (the default)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-rings","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_rings","x":"Retopology: whether a zone between two edge lines (round an eye or a mouth, along an arm) is filled with rings of quads that follow both lines (on by default); small zones inside such rings are closed with rings shrinking in. Off: those zones are made as the others are. argument type range, default description on boolean Rings between two lines"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-thin-zones","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_thin_zones","x":"Retopology: finds the parts too thin for the quads as they are now (fingers, strands of hair: fewer than about four quads would go round them; thin sheets, as locks of hair, are left) and gives each a zone: zone lines round it where it meets the rest, and a resolution for about quads_around quads round it (a power of two, up to max_resolution). One undo step. apply: false only reports them. argument type range, default description apply boolean Add the lines and resolutions (default true) max_resolution number 2 to 64 Parts that need more than this (strands of hair) are left out (default 16) quads_around integer 3 to 64, default 6 Quads round each thin part (a finger, a strap): more keeps them round, fewer is lighter"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-parts","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_parts","x":"The named parts of the model, for the commands that take a part or region: material slots (slot:<name>), connected pieces by size (piece:<n>), Blender vertex groups (group:<name>), the head and body found from the shape (region:head, region:body, only when a neck is found) and the selected faces (selection). argument type range, default description faces boolean Include each part's face indices (default false)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"select-part","page":"Command reference","t":"select_part","x":"Sets, adds to or removes from the face selection by part id (see mesh_parts). One undo step. argument type range, default description mode set \\ add \\ remove What to do with the selection (default set) part (required) string Part id"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-tint-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"add_tint_layer","x":"Adds a fill layer that changes only the base color, limited to a region of the model and to a smart mask stack: for example a soft red in the skin's creases. strength is the layer's opacity. glow: the color lights up instead (emissive), for glowing eyes or lights. One undo step. argument type range, default description blend normal \\ multiply \\ screen \\ overlay \\ add \\ subtract \\ darken \\ lighten \\ add_signed \\ soft_light Blend mode (default normal) color (required) array sRGB color [r, g, b], 0..1 glow boolean The color glows (emissive channel) instead of tinting the base color mask array Smart mask stack, multiplied together: cavity (creases, pores), edges (worn edges), occlusion (sheltered places), facing_up (where dust settles), facing_down, facing (direction), gradient_up (stronger toward the top), gradient_down, gradient (along direction), noise (breakup; kind clouds, cells, cracks, ridges or streaks), thin (thin parts), drips (streaks running down the sides), pattern (a library material's height or brightness: material, source). level = threshold 0..1 (higher = less coverage), contrast 0 soft .. 1 hard name string Layer name region object Where the layer shows: a part id fro"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-material-layer","page":"Command reference","t":"add_material_layer","x":"Adds a fill layer with a library material on every channel (see list_materials), limited to a region and a smart mask stack: for example rust where the armor's edges are. One undo step. argument type range, default description mapping triplanar \\ uv Projection (default triplanar) mask array Smart mask stack, multiplied together: cavity (creases, pores), edges (worn edges), occlusion (sheltered places), facing_up (where dust settles), facing_down, facing (direction), gradient_up (stronger toward the top), gradient_down, gradient (along direction), noise (breakup; kind clouds, cells, cracks, ridges or streaks), thin (thin parts), drips (streaks running down the sides), pattern (a library material's height or brightness: material, source). level = threshold 0..1 (higher = less coverage), contrast 0 soft .. 1 hard material (required) string Library material id name string Layer name (default: the material's name) region object Where the layer shows: a part id from mesh_parts (slot:<name>, piece:<n>, group:<name>, region:head, region:body, selection), several parts, and parts to leave out<br> except : <br> part : <br> parts : scale number 0.05 to 20 Material scale multiplier (default 1)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"update-layer-mask","page":"Command reference","t":"update_layer_mask","x":"Changes where a layer shows: a new region (parts from mesh_parts) and / or a new smart mask stack. What is not given stays: a new region keeps the smart masks, new smart masks keep the region. One undo step. argument type range, default description everywhere boolean Drop the region: the layer covers every part again index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom). Default: the selected layer mask array Smart mask stack, multiplied together: cavity (creases, pores), edges (worn edges), occlusion (sheltered places), facing_up (where dust settles), facing_down, facing (direction), gradient_up (stronger toward the top), gradient_down, gradient (along direction), noise (breakup; kind clouds, cells, cracks, ridges or streaks), thin (thin parts), drips (streaks running down the sides), pattern (a library material's height or brightness: material, source). level = threshold 0..1 (higher = less coverage), contrast 0 soft .. 1 hard region object Where the layer shows: a part id from mesh_parts (slot:<name>, piece:<n>, group:<name>, region:head, region:body, selection), several parts, and parts to leave out<br> except : <br> part : <br> parts :"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-island-per-part","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_island_per_part","x":"One UV island per part: seams on the parts' borders and none inside them, each part unwrapped as one island (closed parts and tubes get a cut along the back, as a head or sleeve would), then everything is packed at an equal texel density. A part made of separate pieces gives an island per piece. Paint is kept. One undo step. argument type range, default description density string auto (default) layout fit screen margin number 0 to 0.1 Gap between islands in UV units (default 0.005) pack boolean Pack all islands afterward (default true) parts (required) array Part ids (mesh_parts); a face in several parts goes to the first reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"layer-region-faces","page":"Command reference","t":"layer_region_faces","x":"Which faces a layer's region covers: the material-slot masks and painted mask at the start of its mask stack (or the painted mask of a simple mask), sampled at each face's center. all = no region. argument type range, default description faces boolean Include the face indices (default true) index integer 0 to 100000 Layer index (0 = bottom). Default: the selected layer"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-ask","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_ask","x":"Asks the Assistant's model for a plan for a request about the open project, without running it: the project description and the grammar are made here, the model writes the plan, and the plan is checked (see assistant_apply to run it). Needs a model file. argument type range, default description request (required) string What the user asked, in plain words timeout number 1 to 600, default 30 Seconds to wait for the local model before giving up"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-apply","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_apply","x":"Runs an Assistant plan ({say, steps: [{cmd, args}]}) as one undo step: only allowlisted commands, at most 16 steps, \"$n.field\" for step n's result. A step that fails undoes the steps before it. argument type range, default description label string Undo step name (default: Assistant + the plan's say) plan (required) object {say, ask, steps: [{cmd, args}]} request string What was asked (remembered for follow-ups)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-check","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_check","x":"Checks an Assistant plan without running it: the command allowlist, argument schemas, $n.field references and the step limit. Returns the steps in plain words and warnings for steps that delete, clear or rebuild. argument type range, default description plan (required) object {say, ask, steps: [{cmd, args}]}"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-context","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_context","x":"The short text description of the open project that the Patina Assistant's model reads with a request: the mesh, its parts, texture layout, layer stack and library materials that fit the request. argument type range, default description request string What the user asked (picks the materials listed)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-model","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_model","x":"The Assistant's model: action load (the file from path, or the configured one), unload or status (default). Status gives the file, its state and where it runs. argument type range, default description action status \\ load \\ unload What to do path string A GGUF model file (load) sha256 boolean status: also give the file's SHA-256 (reads the whole file)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-panel","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_panel","x":"Opens the Assistant window in the desktop app, and asks it a request when one is given (the plan then waits there for the user to preview and keep). Not available headless. argument type range, default description preview boolean Preview the plan as soon as it arrives (default false) request string What to ask (optional)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"assistant-grammar","page":"Command reference","t":"assistant_grammar","x":"The GBNF grammar the Assistant's model is held to for the open project (plan shape, allowlisted commands and their arguments, the part ids, layer indices and materials there are)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"add-object","page":"Command reference","t":"add_object","x":"Adds a model file to the scene as a new object (1.2), next to the ones there: its own layers and texture set, placed where the file puts it (a character's clothes, eyes and hair exported from the same scene fit the body). A file with several meshes adds an object for each (separate: false adds them as one). Its texture set gets as many texels per meter as the active object's (at most its size), unless resolution is given; the model's own materials come in as a layer. The active object stays active unless select is true. Into the startup sphere's scene, the model takes its place. argument type range, default description path (required) string Model file (.obj .gltf .glb .fbx .stl .3mf .ply .ptmesh) resolution integer 0 to 8192, default 0 Texture set resolution per tile, 512 to 8192, a power of two (0: as many texels per meter as the active object, at most its size) select boolean Make the (first) new object the active one (default false) separate boolean A file with several meshes: an object for each (default true)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"auto-texture","page":"Command reference","t":"auto_texture","x":"Guesses a library material for each material slot from its name (Skin, Jeans, M_Boots_Leather, Hair.001...) and adds a fill layer per slot, masked to that slot's faces, in an 'Auto texture' folder at the bottom of the stack. Slots named after parts that want painting by hand (eyes, teeth, glass, decals) or with no confident match are skipped. apply: false only reports the guesses. argument type range, default description apply boolean Add the layers (default true); false = only report the guesses mapping triplanar \\ uv How the materials are projected (default triplanar) overrides object Slot name or index -> material id, to replace a guess (\"\" skips the slot) style any \\ realistic \\ stylized Prefer realistic or stylized materials"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"bake-down","page":"Command reference","t":"bake_down","x":"(1.1) Bakes the layers down from the painted high-poly model (the one open) onto its low-poly version, which becomes the model: every layer comes along. Paint, masks and a baked map's fixes are resampled texel by texel: several points per texel, each looking along the low's normal both ways for the high surface (the nearest point of it when the rays find nothing); normals are turned to the low's surface. Fills, adjustments, folders and smart masks stay as they are, over the low's new bakes: the high's shape baked into the normal map, ambient occlusion and material slots. The result is a new project (no file yet, not linked to Blender); the high stays as its high-poly model. Both models must be in the same place. For the retopology's quads use retopo_use_as_model (it bakes down too). Returns the shares of the texture found along the normals (hit_fraction), by the nearest point and guessed, the distance, the largest gap between the two surfaces; then check it with set_view mode bake_check (amber: by the nearest point, red: guessed) and set_compare mode high_poly (the high as it was beside the low). argument type range, default description bake_high_poly boolean Bake the high-poly's s"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"delete-face-set","page":"Command reference","t":"delete_face_set","x":"Deletes a face set (its faces stay, in no set), or all of them (all: true) (1.2). One undo step. argument type range, default description all boolean Every face set set string The face set: its name or id"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"duplicate-object","page":"Command reference","t":"duplicate_object","x":"A copy of an object (1.2), with its layers and textures, right after it in the list, in the same place (move it with set_object). Not linked to Blender. argument type range, default description object string The object: its name as list_objects gives it, or its index (default: the active one)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"edit-face-set","page":"Command reference","t":"edit_face_set","x":"Changes a face set (1.2): its name, color, hidden (its faces leave the 3D view, the brush and picking; renders and exports keep them), and its faces: add or remove \"selection\" or a list of face indices (faces added leave the sets they were in; (1.2.10) a set left with no faces stays, empty). One undo step. argument type range, default description add \"selection\" or face indices to put in it color array [r,g,b] 0..1 hidden boolean Hidden (true) or shown name string A new name remove \"selection\" or face indices to take out of it set (required) string The face set: its name or id"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"features","page":"Command reference","t":"features","x":"Newer features that can be switched off (Preferences > Features). Without arguments it lists them; with id and on, it switches one. argument type range, default description id string Feature id from the list on boolean Switch it on or off"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"frame-object","page":"Command reference","t":"frame_object","x":"Frames an object in the 3D view (1.2), the whole scene (all: true), or (1.2.11) the active object's selected faces (selection: true, as Shift+F / Blender's View Selected; the whole object when none are selected, framed_selection false then). argument type range, default description all boolean The whole scene object string The object: its name as list_objects gives it, or its index (default: the active one) selection boolean The selected faces of the active object"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"import-brushes","page":"Command reference","t":"import_brushes","x":"Copies brush images (PNG, JPEG, TGA, BMP, GIF, PSD, EXR) or Photoshop brush files (.abr: each sampled tip a brush) into the user's brushes folder, in a folder named category if given (the Brushes panel's group), and makes brushes of them (1.2). A tip that is dark on a light ground paints with its dark part, light on dark with the light part, one with transparency with its opaque part. select: paint with the first one. argument type range, default description category string Its group in the Brushes panel (a folder of that name; default the top) paths (required) array Files to import select boolean Paint with the first brush imported (default true)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"isolate-object","page":"Command reference","t":"isolate_object","x":"Shows only the active object (on) or every object again (off), like Blender's Local view; the brush paints the active object either way (1.2). Hidden objects stay hidden. argument type range, default description on boolean Only the active object (default: switch)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"language-list","page":"Command reference","t":"language_list","x":"The interface's languages: English, the translation files (.po) shipped with Patina and in the user's languages folder, and x-test (every text marked, for checking what is translatable). Also how much of the current one is translated and where the template and the folders are."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"language-new","page":"Command reference","t":"language_new","x":"Starts a translation: copies the template (languages/patina.pot) to <code>.po in the user's languages folder with its header filled in (language, name, plural forms). An existing file is never replaced. Edit it with Poedit or a text editor, then language_set. argument type range, default description code (required) string Language code: fr, de, es, pt_BR, zh_CN, ja... name string The language's name in that language: Fran\u00e7ais, Deutsch..."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"language-set","page":"Command reference","t":"language_set","x":"Shows the interface in a language from language_list (code such as fr or pt_BR; en: English; x-test: the test language). Only the windows change: commands, results and logs stay in English. argument type range, default description code (required) string The language code (the .po file's name)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"language-untranslated","page":"Command reference","t":"language_untranslated","x":"The texts the interface showed this session without a translation in the current language, and optionally a .po file of them to fill in. argument type range, default description path string Write them to this .po file (optional)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"layers-at","page":"Command reference","t":"layers_at","x":"The layers that paint the spot of the model under a viewport point (what the 3D view's right-click menu lists): each with its coverage there (paint or fill x mask x opacity, 0..1) and whether it is shown. Top of the stack first; folders are left out. Use it to find which layer to change for a spot you see in render_view. argument type range, default description point (required) array [x,y] normalized viewport point (0..1, 0,0 top left)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"layout","page":"Command reference","t":"layout","x":"(0.46) The window's layout: guided (the work in five steps along the top, each a screen with what it needs: model, uvs, bake, paint, export), full (every panel docked) or (1.2.7) blender (arranged as Blender is: the steps as workspace tabs, the Outliner and a Properties editor with vertical tabs, an asset shelf), and (1.2.7) its theme: patina (gray and copper) or vivid_dark (KDE Plasma's Vivid Dark: navy and cyan, as the Vivid Dark Blender theme). With no arguments it only reports. Returns the layout, the theme, the Guided step shown, and each step's state (done, look: needs a look, todo) with why, in a few words: the same marks the step bar shows, and (1.2.10) the Blender layout's Properties tab and its pin. Only with the window open (not headless). argument type range, default description mode guided \\ full \\ blender Switch to this layout pin_properties boolean (1.2.10) Pin the Properties tab (the pin above its tabs): true keeps the tab when a tool or a workspace is chosen; false (default) shows the tool's tab (Select: Step, Paint: Layer, a retopology tool: Retopology) properties_tab step \\ object \\ layer \\ texture_set \\ face_sets \\ retopology \\ history \\ notes \\ console (1.2.10)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-face-sets","page":"Command reference","t":"list_face_sets","x":"The active object's face sets (1.2): named, colored groups of faces kept in the project (a face is in one at most), each with its id, color, face count and whether it is hidden. Blender's sculpt face sets come in with the model from the add-on 0.14."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-generators","page":"Command reference","t":"list_generators","x":"The procedural generators (create_material's generator): each one's name, group, what colors A, B and C are for, and what its four extra values do (label, range, the default that 0 stands for, the choices of a style setting). argument type range, default description generator string Only this generator"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"list-objects","page":"Command reference","t":"list_objects","x":"The scene's objects (1.2): each model in the project is an object with its own layers, texture set and history (a body, its clothes, its eyes, its hair). The active one is the one painted, listed by list_layers and changed by the other commands. Each with its name, faces, layers, texture resolution, whether it is shown, its placement (location in meters, rotation in degrees about X, Y, Z, scale) and its bounds in the scene."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"macro-list","page":"Command reference","t":"macro_list","x":"Saved macros (the macros folder)."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"macro-play","page":"Command reference","t":"macro_play","x":"Replays steps: the recorded ones, a saved macro by 'name', a job file by 'path', or 'steps' given here. Stops at the first step that fails and reports it. Each step is its own undo step. argument type range, default description name string Saved macro name path string Job file steps array [{cmd, args}]"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"macro-record","page":"Command reference","t":"macro_record","x":"Macro recorder: action 'start' (clears the steps unless append), 'stop', 'clear', 'status', or 'remove' (index). While recording, every command that changes something is kept as a step - from the menus, scripts or Claude. Viewport brush strokes are not commands and are not recorded. argument type range, default description action start \\ stop \\ clear \\ status \\ remove What to do (default status) append boolean start: keep the steps already recorded index integer 0 to 100000 remove: the step to drop (0 = the first)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"macro-save","page":"Command reference","t":"macro_save","x":"Saves the recorded steps as a job file (the format of patina run ): to 'path', or by 'name' into the macros folder so macro_play and the Macro window find it. argument type range, default description name string Macro name path string Job file path (.json)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"make-face-set","page":"Command reference","t":"make_face_set","x":"Makes face sets (1.2). from selection (default) or faces: one set of those faces, named name (they leave the sets they were in; (1.2.10) a set left with no faces stays, empty, until delete_face_set). from material, island or connected: a set for each material slot, UV island or connected part (replacing the sets there were). One undo step. argument type range, default description color array [r,g,b] 0..1: its color (default: one of its own) faces array from faces: the face indices from selection \\ faces \\ material \\ island \\ connected What the sets are made of (default selection) name string The new set's name (one set)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"mesh-symmetry","page":"Command reference","t":"mesh_symmetry","x":"Measures how far the mesh is from mirror-symmetric across X (Blender's X): every vertex is mirrored and its distance to the surface on the other side is measured. Reports the worst spots (positions in Blender axes), the vertices beyond tolerance (0.1% of the model's size) and a breakdown per material slot and height band. show: true switches the viewport to the Symmetry heat map. argument type range, default description plane_x number -1000000 to 1000000 Mirror plane x (object units); kept until changed; null = automatic (the default) show boolean Switch the viewport to the Symmetry view vertices boolean Also list the vertex indices beyond tolerance and without a match (Blender's vertex order for meshes sent from Blender)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"open-window","page":"Command reference","t":"open_window","x":"Opens one of Patina's windows, set up for what was asked: export (textures, with a preset), render, texel_density, symmetry (the mesh symmetry check), high_poly (bake from a high-poly mesh), bake_down (1.1: the layers onto a low-poly model), new_texture_set (a texture set from the selection), texture_set (the Texture Set panel), blender (the Blender link), preferences, shortcuts, macros, material_editor (a library material to edit or copy, or a new one with a generator), mesh_check (what is wrong with the model, and fixes), export_model (the model with its textures), reduce (fewer polygons), batch (recipes on many files), transform (units, size, turn, origin); and (0.36) a panel brought to the front: layers, properties, materials, brushes, console, history, notes, colors; about, help (shortcuts), welcome, pen_test; and (1.2.12) tutorial: Patina in 5 Minutes, the guided tutorial, in the browser (the result says which page). Only with the window open (not headless). argument type range, default description generator string material_editor: a new procedural material with this generator keymap patina \\ blender preferences: switch to this keymap (and its mouse navigation) material strin"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"path-trace","page":"Command reference","t":"path_trace","x":"(0.39) The Path traced view mode's settings, and how far its samples have got (set_view {mode: path_traced} shows it in the app's 3D view; render_view and render_image {path_traced} use the same settings). argument type range, default description bounces integer 0 to 32, default 6 Light bounces after the first hit: more lights the insides of folds and cavities clamp number 0 to 1000, default 12 The brightest an indirect sample may be: lower removes bright speckles (fireflies) sooner; 0 = off ground boolean A ground under the model that catches its shadow (otherwise invisible; default true) max_samples integer 1 to 65536, default 512 The view stops adding samples here"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"pick-object","page":"Command reference","t":"pick_object","x":"The object under a viewport point (1.2): the first one a ray from the camera meets among those shown, with the face and the point on it (in the object's own space) and the point in the scene. Hidden objects and, in the Local view, the others are left out. argument type range, default description point (required) array [x,y] normalized viewport point (0..1, 0,0 top left)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"reload-brushes","page":"Command reference","t":"reload_brushes","x":"Looks at the user's brushes folder again (1.2): new images and .abr files there become brushes, removed ones go (Patina also looks every few seconds by itself). Returns the folder and how many brushes there are."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"remove-object","page":"Command reference","t":"remove_object","x":"Removes an object and its layers from the scene (1.2). The last object can't go. Not undoable: the object's history goes with it. argument type range, default description object (required) string The object: its name as list_objects gives it, or its index (default: the active one)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-confirm-lines","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_confirm_lines","x":"Retopology: confirms zone lines (or unconfirms them): confirmed lines are drawn darker and new lines don't snap to them; they still make zones and edge loops. One undo step. Without line or point: every line. argument type range, default description confirmed boolean Confirm (true, the default) or unconfirm (false) line integer 0 to 100000 Only this line (its index, as retopo_info line_points lists them) point array Only the line nearest this point of the model"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-fewer-poles","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_fewer_poles","x":"Retopology: whether the quads' counts are solved again after they are made, so that a three- and a five-edged pole close together cancel out (on by default). The new quads are kept only when they have fewer poles and no more folded faces. Off: the quads as first made. argument type range, default description on boolean Fewer poles"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-flow-loops","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_flow_loops","x":"Retopology: whether each flow stroke is an edge loop as well, running exactly along it (on by default). Unlike a zone line, a stroke doesn't make a zone of its own. Off: the strokes only turn the quads their way, and the quads keep their size, so a circle of flow still has poles. argument type range, default description on boolean Strokes make edge loops"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-keep-away","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_keep_away","x":"Retopology: whether a change (a line, a stroke, painted density, a zone's resolution) makes only the quads near it again, the rest kept exactly as they were and joined to the new ones by a row of faces (on by default); off: every build makes all the quads again. retopo_build with fresh makes them all again once. argument type range, default description on boolean Keep the quads away from changes"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-ring-line","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_ring_line","x":"Retopology: a zone line right round the part of the model at the point (an arm, a leg, the neck, a finger), across the way the part runs there, as Shift+click with the Lines tool makes. A line round the top of an arm or a leg keeps its quads in rings. Where there is no part to go round (a head, a chest), or with mode facing: where a plane through the point facing the camera cuts the model (round a breast, the face, a knee). One undo step. argument type range, default description direction array [x,y,z] the way the facing plane faces (default: the camera's, parallel to the screen) mirror string Mirror it across the model's middle on these axes (\"x\", \"xy\"...) mode auto \\ around \\ facing auto (the default): round the part, else facing the camera point (required) array [x,y,z] on (or near) the surface"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"retopo-snap","page":"Command reference","t":"retopo_snap","x":"Retopology: whether a new zone line's ends snap onto the lines near them (on by default; confirmed lines are never snapped to). Off: the ends stay where they are drawn (tight folds: lips, fingers, the crotch). Only the Lines tool's drawing; retopo_zone_line takes the points as given. argument type range, default description on boolean Snap line ends"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"select-linked","page":"Command reference","t":"select_linked","x":"Selects the faces linked to the face under point (or face; neither: to the faces selected) (1.2): connected (the whole connected part, by shared corners), flat (spreading over edges where the surface bends less than angle: a box's side, a panel), island (its UV islands) or material (its material slots). One undo step. argument type range, default description angle number 0 to 180, default 10 by flat: the most the surface may bend from face to face, in degrees by connected \\ flat \\ island \\ material What links them (default connected) face integer 0 to 1000000000 A face instead of point mode set \\ add \\ remove What the faces do to the selection (default set) point array [x,y] normalized viewport point on the active object"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"select-loop","page":"Command reference","t":"select_loop","x":"Selects a band of quads (1.2), as Blender's Alt+click by an edge in face mode: from the face under point (or face), across its edge nearest that point (or edge, the face's corner index), into the next face and out of its opposite edge, both ways, until a face that isn't a quad, a border or the band closes. ring: the band along that edge instead (Ctrl+Alt+click). One undo step. argument type range, default description edge integer 0 to 1000000 With face: its edge (corner index from 0); default the first face integer 0 to 1000000000 A face instead of point mode set \\ add \\ remove What the faces do to the selection (default set) point array [x,y] normalized viewport point on the active object (0..1, 0,0 top left) ring boolean The band along the edge instead of across it (default false)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"select-object","page":"Command reference","t":"select_object","x":"Makes an object the active one (1.2): the one the brush paints, whose layers list_layers gives and the Layers panel shows, whose UVs the UV editor shows, and that the other commands change. Its own undo history comes with it. argument type range, default description object (required) string The object: its name as list_objects gives it, or its index (default: the active one)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"set-object","page":"Command reference","t":"set_object","x":"Changes an object (1.2): its name, whether it is shown, and where it is: location (meters, moved from where its file put it), rotation (degrees about X, then Y, then Z, about the middle of the model) and scale (1 = as it came; one number scales evenly). reset: back to where it came in. by: add the location and rotation to the object's instead of setting them. One undo step in the object's history. argument type range, default description by boolean location and rotation are added to the object's (default false) location array [x, y, z] in meters name string A new name object string The object: its name as list_objects gives it, or its index (default: the active one) on_ground boolean Moved up or down so its lowest point is at height 0 reset boolean Back to where it came in (before location, rotation and scale) rotation array [x, y, z] in degrees scale [x, y, z], or one number for all three visible boolean Shown in the 3D view, renders and exports"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"texture-set-from-faces","page":"Command reference","t":"texture_set_from_faces","x":"Gives faces a texture set (their own texture space) of their own, such as one for the head and one for the body. With one texture or texture sets per material slot, the faces go into the material slot called 'name' (a new slot unless one has that name; Blender gets it with the next send). With UDIM tiles, their islands move to the next free UDIM tile. pack (default true) then packs each set the faces left or joined, so every set fills its own space. Paint moves with the faces. Clears the undo history. argument type range, default description faces array Face indices margin number 0 to 0.1, default 0.005 Gap between islands in UV units: 0.005 = half a percent of the square. More avoids color bleeding between islands at small texture sizes; less uses the space better name string The texture set's name (material slot), such as Head pack boolean Pack the sets the faces left and joined (default true) part string A part from mesh_parts (such as region:head, slot:2, piece:1) selection boolean Use the selected faces"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"ui-id-conflicts","page":"Command reference","t":"ui_id_conflicts","x":"For tests: widgets drawn with the same ID as another in their window (they fight over clicks), each once since Patina started. Only checked when Patina was started with PATINA_UI_ID_CHECK set."},{"p":"commands.html","a":"ui-rect","page":"Command reference","t":"ui_rect","x":"For tests: where a widget was last drawn in the window (x, y, width, height in window pixels), by its name (for example retopo.zone_resolution). argument type range, default description name string The widget prefix string Instead: every widget whose name starts so (for example materials.tile:)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"undo-settings","page":"Command reference","t":"undo_settings","x":"(1.2.7) How much undo keeps, for every object's history together, and what it uses now. Recent steps keep their texture copies on the graphics card (instant undo, up to gpu_mb); older ones are compressed into RAM, and past ram_mb the oldest steps are dropped. Undo is never written to disk. Each value given is set (the user's preferences); without any, it only reports. argument type range, default description gpu_mb integer 256 to 65536, default 1024 Undo copies kept on the graphics card for instant undo, in MB. Less leaves more for textures; more makes older steps undo faster ram_mb integer 1 to 1048576 Compressed undo in RAM, in MB; past it the oldest steps go. Default: a quarter of the machine's memory, 1 to 8 GB steps integer 10 to 1000, default 100 Steps each object's history keeps. More: undo further back; fewer: less memory"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"use-face-set","page":"Command reference","t":"use_face_set","x":"Does something with a face set's faces in one go (1.2): select (the selection becomes them), add_to_selection, (1.2.10) deselect (they leave the selection), hide, show, isolate (hide every other set and the faces in none; again: show_all), show_all, fill (the selected layer gets the paint material on them, as the polygon fill does) or mask (the selected layer's mask: white on them, black elsewhere). One undo step. argument type range, default description action (required) select \\ add_to_selection \\ deselect \\ hide \\ show \\ isolate \\ show_all \\ fill \\ mask What to do set string The face set: its name or id (not needed for show_all)"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"uv-relax","page":"Command reference","t":"uv_relax","x":"Relaxes UV islands: each island of the faces is solved again without stretch (conformal) and fitted back onto where it was, at the same size. Pinned islands are left alone. Paint is reprojected. (1.2.7) changed is false when nothing moved by a quarter of a texel or more (the islands were already relaxed): no undo step then. argument type range, default description faces array Faces whose islands to relax (default all) reproject boolean Keep existing paint (default true) selection boolean Use the selected faces strength number 0 to 1, default 1 How far toward the relaxed layout: 0 keeps the old one, 1 fully relaxed"},{"p":"commands.html","a":"wait-frames","page":"Command reference","t":"wait_frames","x":"Tests: answers once the window has drawn this many more frames (after synthetic input, a click is then handled and drawn). Headless: at once. argument type range, default description count integer 1 to 600, default 2 Frames to wait for"},{"p":"changes.html","a":"","page":"What's new","t":"What's new","x":"What's new What changed in each version of Patina, newest first. The same list comes with Patina as CHANGELOG.md . What changed in each version, newest first. The manual (Help \u203a Manual, or docs/manual ) explains how to use each feature, and docs/ROADMAP.md lists every feature with the version it came in."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"added","page":"What's new","t":"Added","x":"Patina in 5 Minutes , a guided tutorial that comes with Patina. It has sixteen short chapters with real screenshots: connect Blender and Claude, import, add objects, unwrap, bake, paint layers, brushes, your own materials, smart materials and export. Each chapter shows the long way and the pie, and Play runs it like a short video. The welcome window has one button that opens it in your browser. The x beside the button hides it for good. It is always in Help \u203a Patina in 5 Minutes (Tutorial) and in the command palette. The manual's first page links to it. MCP: open_window tutorial ."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"added-2","page":"What's new","t":"Added","x":"Frame the selection in the 3D view , as Blender's View Selected: the selected faces fill the view, or the whole model when nothing is selected. It's Shift+F in the Patina keymap and Numpad . in the Blender keymap (Numpad . framed the whole model before). It's also in the view pie ( Z , down right) and the View menu, as Frame the Selection . MCP: frame_object selection ."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"The view pie's down-right item is now Frame selection. The retopology preview it had is still on the bar over the 3D view and in the View menu. The manual's pie menus section now says \"radial menus\" too, so a search for either finds it."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"added-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Added","x":"A pin for the Properties tabs (Blender layout): the pin above the tabs keeps the tab you chose when you pick a tool or a workspace. Unpinned, as before, the Select tool shows the Step tab and the Paint tool the Layer tab. MCP: layout pin_properties and properties_tab . A selects every face, or none when some are selected, in the 3D view with any tool, as in the UV editor. Before, it worked in the 3D view only with the Select and Island tools."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"The Face Sets panel works like Blender's vertex groups. A click on a set makes it the active set. Under the list, Add Selection and Remove Selection change its faces, and Select , Deselect and Deselect All change the selection. Each row has a trash button that deletes the set. A set whose faces all move to another set now stays, empty, with its name and color. Before, it disappeared, so Create from Selection seemed to rename and recolor it. Clearer names: Create from Selection , Create Sets From... , Show by Color , Lock Face Sets and Show Only These Faces . Show All is always under the Create buttons, and in each set's right-click menu too. MCP: use_face_set deselect ."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"Linux install: install.sh now installs every shipped data folder. Before, it copied only materials, the Blender add-on, Patina Web and the samples, so an installed Patina had no built-in smart materials, recipes, fonts or translations. Running the new install.sh adds them; your own library is left alone."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"added-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Added","x":"Red marks (Retopology window \u203a Show, and the arrow beside the Retopology preview button) hides the red crosses over the quads. Its tooltip says what they mark: parts too thin for the quads, tight spots patched where zones meet, and, with Mirror on, places where the two sides of the model differ. MCP: retopo_view marks ."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"Retopology could take all the memory and close Patina. A zone set to a high resolution over most of a big model (a million quads asked for) ran six tries side by side, each of up to 25 GB, and the live preview started it by itself when a project opened. The tries now share a memory budget: only as many run at once as fit, and a zone that can't fit at all is not tried; the window says why. A zone asking for more than 250,000 quads isn't tried either (QuadriFlow ran 20 minutes on one asking for a million and gave none): the window says to lower its resolution. The live preview doesn't start for more than 150,000 quads (Make quads still makes them), and the window warns when the settings ask for that many. Memory freed after a build goes back to the system. MCP: retopo_info quads_asked . The camera didn't move with the Note tool. A middle drag, Alt + drag and the wheel did nothing while it was chosen. Now only a click on the model or on a pin goes to the notes."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"The bar over the 3D view is easier to read. Each brush setting is one field with its icon inside it, filled up to its value, as Blender's sliders are; thin lines part the groups (the brush, its projection, the locks, the view). The icons stood apart from their sliders, so which icon went with which slider was hard to see. Fields are narrower. A slider or drop-down is at most about 13 characters wide, so its label sits beside it in wide panels (they took 65% of a panel's width). The Blender layout's workspace tabs have their steps' colors again , as the Guided layout's steps, and so do the tool groups in its tool strip. Material Edit is a tab of the Blender layout's sidebar, beside Brush, Colors, View and Blender. The Material Editor's window opened behind the layout there. With names beside the icons, the tool strip of the Blender and Guided layouts is as wide as the names need. A docked strip that is narrower shows the icons alone instead of names too small to read. The Outliner says the layers are the object's, for all of its texture sets (each layer paints every set)."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"The layer buttons ran off the panel with names beside the icons. They go on to a new row now. Retopology: a change remade the whole model. \"Only the quads near a change\" made new quads on the whole model, then kept them only near the change, so every edit cost a full build (19 s on a head, up to a minute and more on a body). The new quads are now made only on the part of the model round the change: on a 190,000-face character, a zone line drawn after the first build updates in about 1 second instead of 21. Builds also ask for the number of quads that worked last time, so most need one pass instead of two."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"new","page":"What's new","t":"New","x":"A Blender layout. Besides Guided and Full, the switch at the right of the menu bar (and Edit \u203a Preferences \u203a Display , the Welcome window, the command palette) has Blender : Patina arranged as Blender arranges its editors, with every panel and button of the Full layout. The five steps are workspace tabs in the menu bar, each with its mark (done, needs a look, to do). A step brings up its tools and its own panel; UVs puts the UV editor beside the 3D view, as Blender's UV Editing workspace, and Paint the asset shelf under it, as Texture Paint. A tool of another step brings that step up. The tools stand at the 3D view's left. Its sidebar is at its right, with vertical tabs as Blender's N panel: Brush, Colors, View (the base material and the lighting) and Blender. The chosen tab again closes it. At the right, the Outliner: the objects, and under the active one its texture sets (a click shows one in the UV editor) and its layers, with the layer buttons, folders, drag and drop and a search. (The layers paint every texture set of their object, so they sit beside the sets, not in each one.) Under it, the Properties editor, with a column of icon tabs: the step's own panel (the Guided layout"},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"One UV menu, the same everywhere. UV in the menu bar, the UV editor's More button and its right-click menu had grown apart: the same command had another name or place in each, and some were only in one. Now all three are the same menu, in the same order, with the same names (the right-click menu adds Move, Rotate and Scale at the top). With faces selected, the commands work on their islands; with nothing selected, on every island. The same menu everywhere, for the other menus too. The menu bar's View and the 3D view's right-click View are now one menu (they named the same commands differently, \"Frame mesh\" and \"Frame the model\", and each had options the other didn't). The new-layer list is the same in Edit \u203a New Layer, the Layers panel's right-click menus and the Add menu: five lists had grown apart, some with the adjustment layer, the white mask, baked maps or smart materials and some without. The 3D view's right-click menu had two Select menus; now it has one, with the same items as the UV editor's Select. Menu items are in Title Case everywhere, as in the menu bar and Blender. Mirrored Halves \u203a Mirror the Selection's UVs (X) : with faces selected, Mirror UVs stacks only their ha"},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"On a big model, only the latest UV edit could be undone. With \"Keep paint\" on, a UV edit copies every layer: 4.9 GB for a character with seven texture sets. Each object's history could hold 1 GB on the graphics card, so every UV edit pushed out the one before. Now that copy is compressed to about 131 MB, and the steps before it stay. The limit also covered each object separately, so a scene of eight objects could hold 8 GB of graphics memory; it now covers them all together. \"Select the Other Half\" was grayed out for a face unwrapped in two halves. It only knew halves stacked on each other. Now it adds the mirror image of the selection by the model's symmetry, wherever it is in the layout (whole islands when you selected whole islands). It is also in Select \u203a The Other Half. Claude uses select_faces with mode: \"mirror\" . UV commands that changed nothing said they had done it. Orient Upright on an island that was already upright said \"Oriented upright\", and Relax on a relaxed island said \"Relaxed\". Now they say \"Already upright: nothing turned\" or \"Already relaxed\", and make no undo step (with \"Keep paint\" on, each step copied every layer). uv_orient and uv_relax return changed . pa"},{"p":"changes.html","a":"blender-add-on-0-14-1","page":"What's new","t":"Blender add-on 0.14.1","x":"\"Send to Blender\" was refused after the mesh changed in Blender, even when Patina hadn't changed a UV. The add-on said \"Patina changed its UVs ... Patina sent 60930 loops but ... has 60916\" and kept the old textures. Now, when Patina sends back the UVs it was given, the textures load: they fit every face you didn't change. A warning says the mesh changed, and that sending it to Patina again paints the changed faces too (the paint carries over). When Patina did change the UVs, the textures still wait, as before."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"Blender got another model's UVs, and textures sent from Patina never arrived. When Claude Desktop (or another MCP client) started patina --mcp before any Patina window was open, that hidden Patina took port 7860. A Patina window opened afterward moved to 7861, which is the port Blender's add-on listens on. So Blender's requests went to the hidden Patina, which answered with its startup sphere: \"Patina sent 8064 loops but ... has 60993\". And the window's \"Send to Blender\" reached itself: \"unknown command 'patina_textures_updated'\". Now: the hidden Patina of patina --mcp leaves the port free, and its tools switch to a Patina window as soon as one is open; a window that finds its port busy never takes 7861; a Patina asked for an object it has no link to says so, instead of sending its own object's textures and UVs. The manual's tip for two windows suggested --port 7861 , Blender's port. It says --port 7862 now."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"A brush set to UV projection could stop painting. After a few strokes, or after the camera moved, strokes in the 3D view put nothing on the layer, and the status line said \"Nothing to paint\". A fill or a Screen stroke made it work again until the next camera move. Such a brush checks which spots the camera sees, but Patina drew the picture of the view it checks against only for Screen brushes, so it used an old one. It draws it for these brushes too now. Symmetry needed the camera to see both sides. With a Screen or UV brush, a mirrored copy the camera couldn't see (facing away, or off screen) was left out, or it landed on whatever the view showed at that spot, like an eyelid's copy on the nose. Those copies now go straight onto the model's surface, so the mirror follows the model, not the view. \"Nothing to paint\" no longer shows after a stroke that did paint with a library material."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"The path tracer showed the triangles of smooth models where light grazes them. Under a hard light, the line between light and shadow on a nose, a cheek or a shoulder ran in triangle-shaped steps, though the Lit view and Blender's viewport were smooth. This is the \"shadow terminator\" problem. Light was cut off at each flat triangle's own horizon, and shadow rays leaving the flat triangles hit their neighbors. Now light fades out at the smooth surface's horizon, and rays leave from the smooth surface the normals describe, as Blender's Cycles does. A normal map's light fades out smoothly too. Renders under soft light don't change."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"new-2","page":"What's new","t":"New","x":"G, R and S in the Object tool , as in Blender's Object Mode. With the pointer over the 3D view, G moves the object, R turns it about the line of sight and S scales it evenly. There's no handle to hit. X, Y or Z keeps to one axis, a typed number sets the amount (S 0.5 halves it), and Ctrl snaps. A click or Enter keeps the change as one undo step, and a right-click or Esc puts it back. The keys are in Edit \u203a Keyboard Shortcuts for both keymaps. In the Object tool they come first: S doesn't pick the Select tool there. A list view for materials : a small picture, the name, and the category and library of each, in the Materials and Smart materials tabs. A size slider for the material tiles (Ctrl+mouse wheel over them does it too). Patina remembers the view and the size."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"Adding a model could replace the one you had. After you opened a project, Patina still took it for the sphere it starts with. Add a Model to the Scene then put the new model in place of a one-object project, and a model dropped on the window opened instead of asking. Blender's Add to the Patina Scene replaced the scene too. Now only the untouched starting sphere gives way. Material tiles ran off the side of the panel. The panel didn't count the space around each tile or the scrollbar, so at some widths the last column was cut off. A long name could also widen its tile. The tiles fit now, and long names end with \"...\". The same fix applies to the smart materials, smart masks and brushes."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"Hair cards, leaves and lace from OBJ and FBX files came in solid. Many game assets keep their cut-outs only in the color texture's alpha, and their file says nothing about opacity. Patina now makes that alpha the Opacity channel of the layer From the model when it has see-through and solid parts, and the status line says so. An alpha that is even all over is left out, because some assets keep gloss there. For a model you already brought in, select it and use File \u203a Bring In the Model's Textures again. See-through parts drew in the wrong order. Back layers of hair cards could land on top of front ones, and an object that was not the one you paint lost its see-through parts in front of the one you do. Every object's opaque parts are drawn first now, then the see-through parts, and the nearest layer of cards hides the ones behind it."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"The layer's Opacity channel box and its Channel opacity \u203a Opacity slider explain themselves: the see-through channel and its strength, not the slider that says how strongly the whole layer shows. import_model_textures takes color_alpha : \"opacity\" always uses the color texture's alpha, \"ignore\" never."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"fixed-2-2-2-2-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Fixed","x":"Saving could close Patina. A paint layer with clearcoat (painted with car paint, lacquer or another material that has it) made every save and every autosave stop the app. Paint in the opacity, sheen and subsurface channels was lost on saving. Since 0.26, a save wrote those channels of paint layers as empty images, so they were gone when the project was opened again (\"could not restore channel sheen\"). They are saved now. Paint saved that way before 1.2.1 can't come back; fill layers keep theirs, because they come from their material. A save that fails says why instead of closing Patina, and the autosave tries again later."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"Patina Web's material library is baked again with every build. 1.2.0's downloads had the library of 0.26, with its British names; 1.2.1's has the current one. The Linux download's INSTALL.txt names what the build needs: its glibc version and SDL 3. install.sh checks for SDL 3, and says so when the system's glibc is too old for the build. The install notes and the manual no longer name version 0.2.0 in their file names. A new README: download, install, your first texture, how-tos, and this change log from 1.0.0 on. The list of every feature moved to docs/FEATURES.md ."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"new-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"New","x":"Objects in a scene. A project can hold several models, each an object with its own layers, texture set and undo history: a body, its clothes, its eyes, its hair. The Objects list over the layers shows them. Click one to paint it, and the layers below, the UV editor and the bakes are that object's. File \u203a Add a Model to the Scene (Ctrl+Shift+I) adds a model file as an object of its own, where the file puts it. Its texture set gets as many texels per meter as the object already open, so small parts like eyes get smaller textures. Files with several meshes (glTF, FBX, OBJ) open as an object each. Files with more than 24 meshes (kits of many small parts) still open as one. The MCP command open_mesh takes separate to choose. The Object tool (W) moves, turns and scales objects with a gizmo in the 3D view. Arrows move along an axis, rings turn, handles scale, and Ctrl snaps. Press W again for the next of the three. Each drag is one undo step in that object's history. A click on another object picks it. On the ground and Where it came in are on the tool's bar. Double-click another object in the 3D view to paint it. A brush stroke that starts on another object does nothing and says which ob"},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"A project with several objects is saved in one .patina file. Patina 1.1 and older open its first object only. A model dropped on the window while one is open asks whether to add it to the scene or open it instead. Drop it on the Objects list to add it right away. Objects you are not painting free their compositing memory until they are painted or exported again. The status bar names the object you paint when there are several. An image tip drawn small is smoother: its mip levels are made now. An image dropped on the Brushes panel becomes a brush of its own, kept in your brushes folder. It used to change only the current brush's tip. Help \u203a What's New opens this list in the manual."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"blender-add-on-0-14-0","page":"What's new","t":"Blender add-on 0.14.0","x":"Send to Patina sends every selected mesh, and the new Add to the Patina Scene adds them beside what Patina has. Each object's placement goes along. The automatic mesh sync only updates objects Patina's scene has. The sculpt face sets go along, for Patina's face sets."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"new-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"New","x":"Bake down from the high-poly. Paint the sculpt, then bake every layer down onto its low-poly version, with Bake \u203a Bake Down to a Low-Poly Model or Use the Quads as the Model after Patina's retopology. The low-poly becomes the model and the whole layer stack comes along. Paint, masks and fixes are measured at up to 16 points per texel, each looking along the low-poly's normal for the high-poly's surface. Painted normals are turned to fit the new surface. The high-poly's shape goes into the normal map and the ambient occlusion. The Bake-Down Check view marks where the transfer had to take the nearest point (amber) or guess (red), and where the low-poly faces away from the high-poly (magenta). The compare view can put the high-poly as it was beside the result."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"changed-2-2-2-2-2-2-2","page":"What's new","t":"Changed","x":"Bake from a high-poly mesh uses the same rays: 4 points per texel, with the distance measured from the two models. Retopology quads face outward when they are used as the model, sent to Blender or exported."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"1-0-0-2026-10-21","page":"What's new","t":"1.0.0 (2026-10-21)","x":"The first stable release. It has the Guided layout for a first project (Model, UVs, Bake, Paint and Export, each step in its own color) and the Full layout with every panel at once. Projects, presets, keys and layouts from the 0.x versions open as they were."},{"p":"changes.html","a":"before-1-0","page":"What's new","t":"Before 1.0","x":"0.47.0: each step of the Guided layout has its color; the export shows the files it writes. 0.46.0: the Guided layout: the work in five steps. 0.45.0: Select only what the camera sees ( Visible only ). 0.44.0 to 0.44.2: Blender objects reopen the project they were painted in; UVs survive a send from Edit Mode; fast painting on big texture sets. 0.43.0: symmetric islands on nearly symmetric models; the Island brush's Screen and Surface reach. 0.42.0: right-click a slider to type its value. 0.41.0: baked maps (ambient occlusion, curvature, thickness) are layers you can fix. 0.40.0 and 0.40.1: four pie menus; the wireframe and seams stay on the side you can see. 0.39.0: the path traced view and renders. 0.38.0: side views the Blender way (numpad, axis gizmo). 0.37.0 and 0.37.1: the retopology preview button; pin and unpin from the 3D view. 0.36.0: icons in the toolbars, right-click menus, the interface in other languages. 0.35.1: American English throughout. The versions before these are in docs/ROADMAP.md, each feature with the version it came in."}];
